window.PREVIEW_MOCKS["sjmc-mock-1"] = {"id":"sjmc-mock-1","title":"St John's Mock 1","fullTitle":"St John's Mock 1 (100 real PYQs, MClinPsy entrance 25 Nov)","sub":"100 questions · 9 subjects · real past papers · sit it on a 90-minute timer · untimed","sectionLabels":{"general":"General and Cognitive Psychology","assessment":"Psychological Assessment","social":"Social Psychology","personality":"Personality","physio":"Biopsychology","dev":"Developmental Psychology","clinical":"Clinical Psychology and Therapy","disorders":"Psychopathology","research":"Research Methods and Statistics"},"questions":[{"q":"A therapist uses \"time-out\" to manage a child's aggressive outbursts. Time-out is technically classified as a form of:","options":{"a":"Positive Punishment","b":"Negative Reinforcement","c":"Negative Punishment","d":"Positive Reinforcement"},"correct":"c","section":"general","explanation":"The answer is negative punishment, because time-out works by removing a positive, reinforcing stimulus in order to decrease an unwanted behavior, and that combination, removal (negative) plus decrease (punishment), defines negative punishment. Understand the two-by-two framework of operant consequences, formalized within B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning, because this is one of the most heavily tested distinctions in psychology. Two dimensions combine. The first dimension is the effect on behavior: reinforcement always increases the future probability of a behavior, whereas punishment always decreases it. The second dimension is the operation: positive means a stimulus is added or presented, whereas negative means a stimulus is removed or taken away. Note that positive and negative here mean add and subtract, not good and bad. Crossing these gives four procedures. Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant stimulus to increase behavior (giving a treat for good work). Negative reinforcement removes an aversive stimulus to increase behavior (taking an aspirin to end a headache). Positive punishment adds an aversive stimulus to decrease behavior (a spanking). Negative punishment removes a pleasant stimulus to decrease behavior. Time-out from positive reinforcement is the classic example of negative punishment: when a child has an aggressive outburst, they are removed from the reinforcing environment (attention, play, peers) for a short period, and losing access to that positive stimulation reduces the future likelihood of the aggression. The key is that something desirable is subtracted (negative) to weaken behavior (punishment), so time-out is negative punishment.","topic":"Time-out as negative punishment"},{"q":"The primary difference between a \"speed test\" and a \"power test\" in psychological assessment is that a speed test:","options":{"a":"Has items of increasing difficulty, while a power test has items of uniform difficulty.","b":"Has a strict time limit that prevents most test-takers from completing all items, while a power test has items so difficult that no one can answer them all correctly.","c":"Measures reaction time, while a power measures cognitive ability.","d":"Is administered individually, while a power test can be administered in groups."},"correct":"b","section":"assessment","explanation":"The answer is that a speed test has a strict time limit that prevents most test-takers from completing all items, while a power test has items so difficult that not everyone can answer them all correctly, because this correctly captures the fundamental distinction between the two test designs. Understand the concepts, which come from psychometric test construction. The difference between a speed test and a power test lies in what limits a test-taker's score, time pressure or item difficulty. A pure speed test consists of many items that are all relatively easy, so easy that virtually anyone could get them right given unlimited time, but it imposes a strict time limit so short that few or no test-takers can finish all the items. Because the items are easy, individual differences in scores reflect how quickly and accurately a person can work, not whether they can solve hard problems; the score measures speed and efficiency of performance. Clerical aptitude tests and symbol-cancellation tasks are typical examples. A pure power test, by contrast, gives generous or unlimited time so that time is not the constraining factor, but it contains items of graded and increasing difficulty, including items so hard that not everyone can solve them; here scores reflect the difficulty ceiling a person can reach, measuring the level or depth of ability rather than speed. Most real tests are a mix of both, imposing some time limit while also varying difficulty. The key conceptual pairing to remember is speed test equals easy items plus tight time limit, and power test equals hard items plus ample time, which is exactly what the correct option states.","topic":"Speed vs power tests"},{"q":"Karina and Rakhi are involved in an altercation that is captured on tape. In the video, Karina is seen walking with her young son when Rakhi attacks her. Karina falls to the ground and Rakhi assaults her, ultimately punching and kicking her dozens of times. A group of individuals gathers around the two women, takes out their cell phones, and records the fight. They do not help or call for help. The behaviour of the group is an example of which of the following terms?","options":{"a":"Group polarisation","b":"Social facilitation","c":"Bystander effect","d":"Social loafing"},"correct":"c","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is the bystander effect. The bystander effect is the well-documented social phenomenon in which the presence of other people reduces the likelihood that any given individual will help a person in an emergency; the more bystanders present, the less likely any one of them is to intervene. It was investigated by John Darley and Bibb Latane, prompted by the 1964 case of Kitty Genovese, whose murder was reportedly witnessed by many neighbours who did not act. Their research identified two key mechanisms. The first is diffusion of responsibility: when many people are present, each individual feels less personal responsibility to act, assuming someone else will help. The second is pluralistic ignorance: people look to others to define whether the situation is truly an emergency, and if no one else appears alarmed or is helping, each concludes it must not require intervention, so collectively no one acts. In the scenario, a crowd gathers and merely films the assault without helping or calling for help, a textbook illustration of the bystander effect driven by diffusion of responsibility. The distractors describe other group phenomena (polarisation, facilitation, and loafing) that do not concern failure to aid a victim. This item tests recognition of the bystander effect and its mechanisms.","topic":"Bystander effect"},{"q":"Roshan often experiences intense feelings of anger and frustration. In order to cope with these feelings, he enrols in a kickboxing class as an outlet for his emotions. Roshan's actions are an example of which type of defence mechanism?","options":{"a":"Sublimation","b":"Projection","c":"Displacement","d":"Repression"},"correct":"a","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is sublimation. Sigmund Freud, the founder of psychoanalysis, described defence mechanisms as unconscious strategies the ego uses to manage anxiety arising from unacceptable impulses. His daughter Anna Freud later systematised them. Sublimation is considered the most mature and adaptive defence: it channels socially unacceptable drives (here, aggression) into constructive, socially valued activities such as sport, art, or work. Roshan takes raw anger and directs it into kickboxing, a productive outlet, which is the textbook signature of sublimation. Displacement (option c) is close but differs in quality: displacement shifts an impulse onto a substitute target (yelling at a family member instead of the boss), and it is not inherently constructive, whereas sublimation transforms the impulse into something valued. Because the exam rewards recognising the adaptive, channelling nature of the act, sublimation is preferred. Understanding this distinction matters clinically because sublimation predicts healthier coping and better long-term adjustment, while primitive defences like denial or projection predict poorer functioning. This concept recurs throughout personality assessment and psychodynamic therapy.","topic":"Sublimation vs displacement"},{"q":"Which of the following brain structures is primarily responsible for regulating emotions such as fear and aggression?","options":{"a":"Hippocampus","b":"Thalamus","c":"Amygdala","d":"Cerebellum"},"correct":"c","section":"physio","explanation":"The amygdala, an almond-shaped structure within the temporal lobes and part of the limbic system described by Paul MacLean, is the brain structure most directly responsible for processing and regulating emotions, particularly fear and aggression. It rapidly evaluates incoming stimuli for threat and triggers appropriate emotional and physiological responses, including the fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon) reaction. Research by Joseph LeDoux traced the neural circuitry of fear, showing a fast, direct thalamo-amygdala pathway that allows the amygdala to respond to danger before the conscious cortex fully processes it. Classic lesion studies, including the Kluver-Bucy syndrome produced by removing the temporal lobes in monkeys, demonstrated that amygdala damage abolishes normal fear and can blunt aggression, confirming its central role. The distractors serve other functions: the hippocampus, also in the limbic system, is essential for forming new declarative memories; the thalamus is the brain's sensory relay hub; and the cerebellum coordinates movement, posture, and balance. Because the amygdala is primarily responsible for regulating fear and aggression, option c is correct.","topic":"Amygdala fear and aggression"},{"q":"What is the primary difference between Piaget and Vygotsky's theories of language development?","options":{"a":"Piaget argued thought depends on language while Vygotsky argued language depends on thought","b":"Vygotsky argued thought depends on language while Piaget argued language depends on thought","c":"Vygotsky was concerned with early language development while Piaget was concerned with later language development.","d":"None of the above"},"correct":"b","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is that Lev Vygotsky argued thought depends on language while Jean Piaget argued language depends on thought. This captures the core theoretical divide between the two developmental giants. Piaget viewed cognitive development as primary and language as a product of it: for Piaget, the child must first develop underlying cognitive structures and understanding (for example, object permanence and symbolic thought) before language can express those concepts, so language reflects thought rather than driving it. He also regarded much early speech as egocentric, fading as the child matures. Vygotsky, by contrast, in his sociocultural theory, argued that language and thought have separate roots but merge around age two, after which language becomes the primary tool that shapes and drives cognitive development. For Vygotsky, social speech is internalised into private speech and then inner speech, which guides reasoning and self-regulation, so thought is fundamentally dependent on language and social interaction. This reflects his broader emphasis on the zone of proximal development and social scaffolding. The reversed option and the timing-based option misstate the relationship. This item tests the classic Piaget-versus-Vygotsky contrast on the direction of the language-thought dependency.","topic":"Piaget versus Vygotsky thought and language"},{"q":"In psychodynamic therapy, analysis of resistance is where:","options":{"a":"The client is asked to talk about anything that comes to mind","b":"The client transfers their feelings towards significant others onto the therapist","c":"The client obstructs the analysis and the therapist explores reasons for this","d":"The therapist interprets the dreams of the client"},"correct":"c","section":"clinical","explanation":"The correct answer is that the client obstructs the analysis and the therapist explores the reasons for this. Resistance is a key concept in the psychoanalytic and psychodynamic therapy developed by Sigmund Freud. It refers to any behaviour by the client that blocks, opposes, or interferes with the progress of therapy and the uncovering of unconscious material, for example arriving late, changing the subject, falling silent, forgetting appointments, going blank during free association, or refusing to discuss certain topics. Freud regarded resistance not as mere obstruction to be overcome but as meaningful and revealing: it signals that the therapy is approaching anxiety-provoking, threatening, or repressed material that the client's defences are protecting against. Therefore the analysis of resistance involves the therapist noticing these obstructions and helping the client explore and understand why they are resisting, thereby bringing defended-against unconscious conflicts into awareness. Along with free association, dream analysis, and the analysis and interpretation of transference, the analysis of resistance is one of the central techniques Freud used to make the unconscious conscious. The distractors correctly describe these other techniques: free association (option A), transference (option B), and dream analysis (option D), which is why they are wrong for resistance specifically. This item tests understanding of resistance and its analysis in psychodynamic therapy.","topic":"Resistance in psychodynamic therapy"},{"q":"According to the ICD-11 (International Classification of Diseases), what is the minimum duration requirement for symptoms to be present before a diagnosis of schizophrenia can be made?","options":{"a":"2 weeks","b":"1 month","c":"3 months","d":"6 months"},"correct":"b","section":"disorders","explanation":"The answer is one month because the ICD-11, the World Health Organization's International Classification of Diseases, requires that the characteristic symptoms of schizophrenia be present for at least one month before the diagnosis can be made. Those characteristic symptoms include the positive symptoms such as delusions, hallucinations, disorganised thinking and speech, and experiences of passivity or control, and at least one core symptom must persist for most of the time across that month-long period. This one-month rule is worth contrasting carefully with the DSM-5, the American diagnostic system, because that difference is a favourite exam trap: the DSM-5 requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least six months, of which at least one month must consist of active-phase symptoms, with the remaining time made up of prodromal or residual features. So the same disorder carries a one-month threshold in ICD-11 but a six-month threshold in DSM-5. The two-week option is a decoy borrowed from the criteria for a major depressive episode, which requires symptoms nearly every day for two weeks. When an exam specifies ICD-11 and asks about schizophrenia duration, the answer is one month; if it specified DSM-5, the answer would be six months.","topic":"ICD-11 schizophrenia duration"},{"q":"Maximization of ____ variance, minimization of ____ variance, control of ____ variance. Select the appropriate option.","options":{"a":"secondary, true, error","b":"true, primary, secondary","c":"primary, error, secondary","d":"primary, true, error"},"correct":"c","section":"research","explanation":"This item tests the MAXMINCON principle articulated by Fred Kerlinger, whose research-methods textbook made it a cornerstone of design logic. Kerlinger argued that every experiment aims to partition the total variance in the dependent variable into three streams and to handle each differently. First, MAXimize the systematic variance produced by your independent variable, often called primary or experimental variance; you want your treatment to push the groups as far apart as possible so the effect is detectable. Second, MINimize error variance, the random noise from measurement unreliability, individual fluctuations, and uncontrolled chance factors; you shrink it through reliable instruments, standardized procedures, and adequate sample size. Third, CONtrol extraneous or secondary variance, the systematic influence of nuisance variables that are not your focus (age, gender, order effects); you neutralize these by randomization, matching, holding constant, or building them in as factors. The correct ordering in the stem is therefore maximize primary (systematic) variance, minimize error variance, control secondary (extraneous) variance, matching option C. The distractors scramble these assignments, and the tell is that anything asking you to minimize 'true' or 'primary' variance violates the whole point, since that is the signal you are hunting for. Mastering this triad lets you diagnose why a study failed: weak manipulation, sloppy measurement, or confounds.","topic":"Kerlinger MAXMINCON principle"},{"q":"We know that a common distinction is made between explicit and implicit memory. But which one of the following does NOT accurately reflect one of these terms?","options":{"a":"Explicit memory involves conscious awareness of the original information or the situation in which the learning occurred","b":"Implicit memory refers to an influence on behaviour, feelings or thoughts as a result of prior experience","c":"Explicit memory involves recollection of the original information or experience that is subsequently recalled","d":"Implicit memory involves a conscious recollection of the original events"},"correct":"d","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is that implicit memory involves a conscious recollection of the original events, which does NOT accurately reflect the term. The distinction between explicit and implicit memory, elaborated by researchers such as Daniel Schacter and Endel Tulving, divides long-term memory by whether retrieval is conscious. Explicit (declarative) memory involves the conscious, intentional recollection of facts and events, such as recalling what you did yesterday or a fact you learned; statements a and c correctly describe it as conscious awareness and deliberate recollection of the original information. Implicit (non-declarative) memory, by contrast, operates without conscious awareness: prior experiences influence current behaviour, feelings, skills, or thoughts even though the person cannot consciously recall the original learning. Examples include procedural skills like riding a bicycle, classically conditioned responses, and priming; statement b correctly describes implicit memory as an influence from prior experience. Option d is false because it attributes conscious recollection to implicit memory, which is precisely the opposite of what implicit means; the defining feature of implicit memory is that it is unconscious. This distinction matters because it is supported by neuropsychological evidence, notably amnesic patients like H.M. (studied by Brenda Milner) who lost the ability to form new explicit memories but could still learn new skills implicitly, showing the two systems rely on different brain structures.","topic":"Explicit versus implicit memory"},{"q":"A researcher is developing a new scale for resilience. To establish discriminant validity, they should demonstrate that their new scale:","options":{"a":"Correlates highly with an established measure of resilience.","b":"Correlates weakly with a theoretically unrelated construct, such as neuroticism.","c":"Covers all the facets of the resilience construct.","d":"Predicts future ability to cope with stress."},"correct":"b","section":"assessment","explanation":"The answer is that the new scale should correlate weakly with a theoretically unrelated construct such as neuroticism, because discriminant validity is demonstrated precisely when a measure shows low correlations with constructs it is supposed to be different from. Understand construct validity (Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl, 1955) and its two complementary halves, a framework crystallized by Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske in their 1959 multitrait-multimethod approach. Construct validity is the overarching question of whether a test actually measures the theoretical construct it claims to measure, and it is established by accumulating evidence of two kinds that work as mirror images. Convergent validity is evidence that the measure correlates strongly with other measures of the same or closely related constructs; a good resilience scale should correlate highly with existing resilience measures and with theoretically linked traits like optimism, because they should converge. Discriminant validity, sometimes called divergent validity, is the opposite requirement: the measure should correlate weakly, or not at all, with measures of constructs from which it is theoretically distinct. If a resilience scale correlated just as highly with neuroticism as it did with other resilience measures, that would suggest it is not measuring something distinct and is contaminated by the unrelated trait. Demonstrating a low correlation with neuroticism, a conceptually different construct, shows the scale discriminates resilience from mere emotional stability, supporting discriminant validity. Because the question specifies establishing discriminant validity and offers weak correlation with an unrelated construct, that option is correct, while high correlation with a same-construct measure would instead be convergent evidence.","topic":"Discriminant validity"},{"q":"The tendency to overestimate the influence of personality and underestimate the influence of situation is called","options":{"a":"Self-serving Bias.","b":"Fundamental Attribution Error.","c":"Confirmation Bias.","d":"Cognitive Dissonance."},"correct":"b","section":"social","explanation":"The fundamental attribution error, a term coined by Lee Ross, is one of the most robust findings in social psychology. When we try to explain why another person behaved as they did, we systematically overestimate internal, dispositional causes (their personality, character, or attitude) and underestimate external, situational causes (pressures, roles, or circumstances). Seeing someone snap at a waiter, we conclude they are rude, rather than considering that they may have just had terrible news. The classic demonstration comes from Edward Jones and Victor Harris, who found that observers attributed an essay's pro-Castro stance to the writer's true beliefs even when told the writer had been assigned that position with no choice. Fritz Heider's earlier work on attribution laid the groundwork by distinguishing personal from situational causation. Interestingly, the bias flips when we explain our own behaviour: this is the actor-observer asymmetry, where we grant ourselves situational excuses we deny others. The error partly arises because the actor is perceptually salient, the vivid figure against a background we barely notice, so their disposition grabs our causal attention. The phrase in the stem, overestimating personality and underestimating situation, is a near-verbatim definition. Recognizing this tendency fosters humility and empathy, reminding us that behaviour is powerfully shaped by context, a lesson dramatized by situationist studies like Milgram's obedience and Zimbardo's prison experiments.","topic":"Fundamental attribution error"},{"q":"Clare complains about her job duties at work, rarely completes assigned tasks, and is regularly late for work. When she is fired, she claims it was due to her co-worker badmouthing her instead of blaming it on her poor work performance. Which defense mechanism explains Clare's reaction?","options":{"a":"Displacement","b":"Denial","c":"Rationalization","d":"Sublimation"},"correct":"c","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is rationalization. Rationalization, a defence mechanism described within Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic framework and elaborated by Anna Freud, involves generating logical-sounding but false explanations to justify behaviour or outcomes that would otherwise threaten one's self-esteem. Clare's poor performance, lateness, and unfinished tasks are the real reasons for her firing, but she protects her ego by blaming a co-worker's alleged badmouthing. This substitutes a socially acceptable, self-flattering cause for the uncomfortable true one, which is the hallmark of rationalization. Denial (option b) differs because it refuses to accept the reality at all, whereas Clare accepts she was fired but distorts why. Displacement redirects an emotion onto a safer target, and sublimation channels impulses into constructive activity, neither of which fits. Recognising rationalization matters clinically because it is extremely common and often invisible to the person using it, allowing them to avoid accountability and repeat maladaptive patterns. Therapists learn to gently surface these self-serving explanations so clients can confront the genuine drivers of their difficulties and take responsibility for change.","topic":"Rationalization defence mechanism"},{"q":"A person suffered a head injury in a road accident several years ago. Since the accident, he is unable to form new memories. He can remember many things which he did before the accident, but he cannot remember what he said ten minutes ago. The part of the brain which is most probably injured is:","options":{"a":"Brain stem","b":"Hippocampus","c":"Spinal cord","d":"Reticular formation"},"correct":"b","section":"physio","explanation":"The brain region most likely injured is the hippocampus, so the answer is b. The case describes anterograde amnesia: the man can recall events from before his accident (intact remote and old memories) but cannot form new long term memories, forgetting what he said ten minutes ago. This is the classic signature of bilateral hippocampal damage, made famous by the patient H.M. (studied by William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957), who after surgical removal of his medial temporal lobes including the hippocampi was unable to form new declarative memories while retaining older memories and normal short term memory and intelligence. The hippocampus is essential for consolidation, the process of converting new short term and working memories into durable long term memories that can later be retrieved. It is not, however, the storage site for old memories, which is why remote memories laid down before the injury survive: those have already been consolidated and distributed to cortical stores, so they remain accessible even when the hippocampus is damaged. Hence damage to the hippocampus selectively impairs the formation of new memories while sparing old ones, exactly the pattern described. The exam discriminator is the dissociation between preserved old memories and an inability to lay down new ones, which points unmistakably to the hippocampus and its role in memory consolidation rather than to brainstem, spinal, or arousal structures.","topic":"Hippocampus and anterograde amnesia"},{"q":"Which term was used by Erikson to refer to the feeling experienced by some individuals in mid-life that they have achieved very little or nothing and have little to offer to the next generation?","options":{"a":"Stagnation","b":"Generativity","c":"Presbyopia","d":"Mid-life crisis"},"correct":"a","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is stagnation. Erik Erikson proposed eight psychosocial stages of development across the lifespan, each defined by a central crisis with a positive and a negative pole. The seventh stage, occurring in middle adulthood, is generativity versus stagnation. Generativity (option b) is the positive resolution: a sense of contributing to society and guiding the next generation through parenting, mentoring, work, or creativity, feeling that one is making a meaningful difference. Stagnation is the negative pole and the answer the question describes: the feeling of having achieved little or nothing and having nothing to pass on to the next generation, accompanied by self-absorption, disconnection, and a sense of unproductiveness. The distractors do not fit: presbyopia (option c) is an age-related loss of near-vision focusing ability, a purely physical change; and mid-life crisis (option d), a popular phrase associated with Elliott Jaques, describes a period of self-doubt in midlife but is not Erikson's specific term. Understanding Erikson's generativity versus stagnation matters because it frames the developmental task of middle adulthood as finding meaning through contribution, and the failure of that task, stagnation, is linked to depression and a sense of emptiness, informing how clinicians understand midlife distress.","topic":"Erikson generativity versus stagnation"},{"q":"Subjective unit of distress (SUD) is mostly used under:","options":{"a":"Exposure & response prevention","b":"Multi-model therapy","c":"Mindfulness-based stress management","d":"Systematic desensitization"},"correct":"d","section":"clinical","explanation":"The Subjective Units of Distress scale is used most centrally in systematic desensitization, so the answer is d. The SUDs scale, introduced by Joseph Wolpe, is a self report rating, typically from 0 (complete calm) to 100 (the worst distress imaginable), that lets a client quantify their level of anxiety or distress at a given moment. Systematic desensitization, also developed by Wolpe and grounded in reciprocal inhibition, is the behaviour therapy in which the client first learns deep muscle relaxation, then constructs a fear hierarchy, an ordered list of feared situations ranked by the anxiety each provokes. The SUDs ratings are precisely how that hierarchy is built and calibrated: the client assigns a SUDs value to each scene, so the items can be arranged from least to most distressing, and during treatment the therapist presents each item (in imagination or in vivo) while the client stays relaxed, moving up the hierarchy only when the SUDs for the current item has dropped to a low level. Thus SUDs is the measurement engine that organises the hierarchy and signals when to progress. While SUDs ratings are also used in exposure therapies generally, the question's framing about constructing graded levels of distress points to systematic desensitization, where the hierarchy and reciprocal inhibition with relaxation are the defining structure that SUDs serves.","topic":"SUDS in systematic desensitization"},{"q":"Negative symptoms of schizophrenia are all except","options":{"a":"Marked apathy","b":"Paucity of speech","c":"Incongruity of emotional responses","d":"Delusion"},"correct":"d","section":"disorders","explanation":"Schizophrenia symptoms are traditionally split into positive and negative, a distinction sharpened by Timothy Crow and Nancy Andreasen. Positive symptoms are additions to normal experience: delusions, hallucinations, and disorganized speech, reflecting an excess or distortion of function. Negative symptoms are subtractions, the loss or diminution of normal functions, and Andreasen's five A's capture them well: affective flattening, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (apathy), anhedonia, and asociality. In the ICD-10, the general criteria list negative symptoms as 'marked apathy, paucity of speech, and blunting or incongruity of emotional responses,' which is exactly why three of the four options here belong together as negative features. A delusion, by contrast, is a fixed false belief held against contrary evidence; it is the prototypical positive symptom and cannot sit in a list of negative ones. So when the stem asks for the exception, the delusion stands apart. Clinically this matters because negative symptoms tend to respond poorly to standard antipsychotics and are the stronger predictors of long-term disability, whereas positive symptoms like delusions often improve with dopamine-blocking medication. Recognizing which bucket a feature belongs to guides both prognosis and treatment planning, and it is a favourite examination point precisely because the boundaries feel subtle.","topic":"Negative symptoms of schizophrenia"},{"q":"Which of the following sampling methods relies on the researcher's judgment to select participants who are \"typical\" or \"representative\" of the population, and does NOT allow for the calculation of sampling error?","options":{"a":"Simple Random Sampling","b":"Stratified Random Sampling","c":"Quota Sampling","d":"Cluster Sampling"},"correct":"c","section":"research","explanation":"The answer is quota sampling, because it is the only non-probability method in the list, and the question's two clues (researcher judgment about who is typical, and no calculable sampling error) are the defining marks of non-probability sampling. Build the distinction from the ground up. In probability sampling, every population member has a known, non-zero chance of selection determined by a random mechanism; simple random, stratified, cluster and systematic sampling all belong here, and because the selection probabilities are known, statisticians can compute the sampling error, meaning how much a sample estimate is expected to deviate from the true population value. In non-probability sampling, selection depends on human judgment, convenience or availability, so selection probabilities are unknown and sampling error is mathematically undefined. Quota sampling, associated historically with commercial polling before the famous 1948 polling failures exposed its weakness, works by setting quotas for subgroups (for example 50 men and 50 women) and then letting interviewers fill those quotas with whoever seems typical or is easiest to reach. It superficially mimics stratified sampling because both use subgroups, and that resemblance is exactly the trap examiners set. The test is always the final selection step: stratified sampling selects randomly within strata, quota sampling selects by judgment within quotas. Judgment-based filling plus no sampling error calculation equals quota sampling every time.","topic":"Quota non-probability sampling"},{"q":"Any figure of form not perceived in parts but organised in perception best possible whole form is known as","options":{"a":"Law of proximity","b":"Law of pragnanz","c":"Law of figure and background","d":"Law of continuity"},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is the law of pragnanz. The law of pragnanz, also called the law of good form or the principle of simplicity, is the overarching principle of Gestalt psychology, founded by Max Wertheimer along with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka. Pragnanz is German for conciseness or good figure, and the law states that we tend to perceive ambiguous or complex stimuli in the simplest, most stable, most complete and organised form possible; we organise perception into the best possible whole rather than perceiving disconnected parts. This captures the Gestalt insight that the whole is different from, and more than, the sum of its parts. The distractors are specific Gestalt grouping principles that fall under this broader law: the law of proximity (option a) states that elements close together are perceived as a group; the law of continuity (option d) states that we perceive smooth, continuous lines rather than abrupt breaks; and figure-ground organisation (option c) is the tendency to separate an object (figure) from its surroundings (ground). Pragnanz is the general principle from which these more specific laws follow. Understanding it matters because Gestalt principles explain how the brain actively organises raw sensory input into meaningful, coherent perceptions, influencing fields from perception research to design and visual communication.","topic":"Gestalt law of Pragnanz"},{"q":"The MMPI measures which of the following?","options":{"a":"Mood","b":"Physical concerns","c":"Social attitudes","d":"All of the above"},"correct":"d","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is all of the above. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is one of the most widely used and researched objective personality and psychopathology assessment instruments. It was developed in the late 1930s and published in 1943 by Starke Hathaway, a psychologist, and J. Charnley McKinley, a psychiatrist, at the University of Minnesota. It is a broad-band, empirically keyed self-report inventory consisting of hundreds of true/false items. The original MMPI contains ten clinical scales that assess a wide range of psychological domains, and these scales span exactly the areas listed: mood and emotional disturbance (for example the Depression and Hypomania scales), physical or somatic concerns (the Hypochondriasis and Hysteria scales, which tap bodily preoccupation), and social attitudes and interpersonal orientation (the Social Introversion scale, plus scales tapping social deviance). It also includes validity scales (such as the L, F, and K scales) to detect defensiveness, exaggeration, or careless responding. Because the MMPI casts a wide net across mood, somatic, and social-attitudinal content, all three options are genuinely assessed, making the inclusive answer correct. The instrument was later revised as the MMPI-2 and MMPI-2-RF. This item tests broad awareness of the MMPI's comprehensive, multi-domain coverage of personality and psychopathology.","topic":"MMPI scope of measurement"},{"q":"Research suggests that we seek out the company of others because of which motives?","options":{"a":"To obtain information","b":"To reduce anxiety","c":"To compare ourselves with similar others","d":"All of the above"},"correct":"d","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is all of the above. Research on affiliation, the tendency to seek the company of others, identifies several motives, all of which are supported. First, we affiliate to obtain information (option a): being with others, especially in uncertain or novel situations, gives us knowledge about the environment and about how to behave. Second, we affiliate to reduce anxiety (option b): the presence of others can be calming, and classic research by Stanley Schachter showed that people facing a frightening situation preferred to wait with others rather than alone, especially with others in the same predicament, because misery loves miserable company. Third, we affiliate to compare ourselves with similar others (option c): according to social comparison theory, developed by Leon Festinger, we have a drive to evaluate our own opinions and abilities, and when objective standards are lacking we do so by comparing ourselves with other people, particularly those similar to us. Because all three motives are well established, option d is correct. The trap in options a, b, and c is that each is individually true but incomplete, and only the combined answer captures the full picture. Understanding these affiliation motives matters because the need to belong is a fundamental human drive with deep implications for social support, coping with stress, self-evaluation, and mental health, central themes in social and clinical psychology.","topic":"Affiliation motives"},{"q":"Which of the following best describes the concept of 'reciprocal determinism' as proposed by Albert Bandura?","options":{"a":"Individual behavior is solely controlled by environmental factors.","b":"Cognitive processes influence both behavior and the environment in a bidirectional manner.","c":"Personality develops strictly from genetic inheritance and biological predispositions.","d":"Social reinforcement has no impact on self-concept formation."},"correct":"b","section":"personality","explanation":"Reciprocal determinism is the central mechanism of the social cognitive theory of Albert Bandura. It holds that human functioning is the product of a continuous, three-way interaction among personal factors (cognition, beliefs, expectations), behavior, and the environment. None of the three is primary: each influences and is influenced by the other two in a bidirectional loop, which Bandura called triadic reciprocal causation. For example, a student's belief that she can succeed (personal) shapes how hard she studies (behavior), which changes the feedback she gets from teachers (environment), which in turn feeds back into her beliefs. This was a deliberate break from the radical behaviorism of B. F. Skinner, which treated the environment as the sole determinant of behavior, and from purely trait or biological views. Bandura's Bobo doll studies grounded this in observational learning, and his later work on self-efficacy showed how personal cognition steers the whole system. Option b captures the bidirectional, cognition-inclusive character of the model, which is why it is correct.","topic":"Bandura reciprocal determinism"},{"q":"Which endocrine gland is primarily responsible for controlling the body's stress response and stress-related metabolic changes?","options":{"a":"Thyroid gland","b":"Adrenal glands","c":"Pituitary gland","d":"Pancreas"},"correct":"b","section":"physio","explanation":"The answer is the adrenal glands because they are the endocrine glands that directly produce and release the hormones driving the body's stress response and the associated metabolic changes. There are two adrenal glands, one sitting on top of each kidney, and each has two functionally distinct parts. The inner adrenal medulla produces the catecholamines adrenaline (epinephrine) and noradrenaline (norepinephrine), which are released rapidly under sympathetic nervous system activation to produce the immediate fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon) reaction: increased heart rate, raised blood pressure, dilated airways, and mobilisation of glucose for quick energy. The outer adrenal cortex produces corticosteroids, most importantly cortisol, the principal stress hormone of the slower, more sustained stress response; cortisol raises blood sugar, mobilises energy reserves, and modulates immune and metabolic function during prolonged stress. This slower pathway is governed by the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, in which the hypothalamus releases CRH, prompting the pituitary to release ACTH, which then stimulates the adrenal cortex to release cortisol. That axis explains why the pituitary is a tempting distractor: it signals the adrenals, but it is the adrenals that actually manufacture the stress hormones. The thyroid governs baseline metabolism and the pancreas governs blood sugar, neither being the primary stress gland. So the adrenal glands are the correct answer for the body's stress response.","topic":"Adrenal glands stress response"},{"q":"Which, if any, of these statements about Piaget's conservation test is accurate?","options":{"a":"The conservation test can only be conducted with children aged under 5 years","b":"In the water conservation task the level of liquid is maintained at exactly the same height when it is transferred to a differently shaped beaker","c":"The preoperational child's judgement is swayed by one dimension, such as the fact that the contents of one beaker look taller than the other","d":"None of the above"},"correct":"c","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is that the preoperational child's judgement is swayed by one dimension, such as the fact that the contents of one beaker look taller than the other. Jean Piaget's conservation tasks test whether a child understands that quantity remains constant despite changes in appearance. In the classic liquid conservation task, equal amounts of water are shown in two identical beakers, then one is poured into a taller, narrower beaker; a child who has achieved conservation knows the amount is unchanged, whereas a preoperational child (roughly ages 2 to 7) typically says the taller beaker has more. This error occurs because of centration: the preoperational child centres on a single perceptual dimension (here, height) and ignores the compensating change in another dimension (width), and also lacks reversibility, the ability to mentally reverse the pouring. Option c precisely captures this centration on one dimension. The other statements are false: the test is used across a range of ages, not only under 5 (option a); and pouring liquid into a differently shaped beaker changes its apparent height rather than keeping it the same (option b), which is the very manipulation that reveals non-conservation. Understanding conservation matters because it marks the cognitive limits of preoperational thought and the transition into concrete operations, a landmark of Piaget's developmental theory.","topic":"Piaget conservation and centration"},{"q":"Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT) without anesthesia is legally allowed only","options":{"a":"In emergencies, with consent and under strict guidelines","b":"Whenever requested by psychiatrist","c":"For research purpose only","d":"Never allowed under law"},"correct":"d","section":"clinical","explanation":"This question tests the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017, which governs psychiatric treatment in India. Section 95 of the Act lists prohibited procedures, and it expressly bans electroconvulsive therapy (Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini, 1938) given without the use of muscle relaxants and anaesthesia. In older practice this was called direct or unmodified ECT, where the seizure was induced without sedation, causing violent muscle contractions and risk of injury. Modern modified ECT uses general anaesthesia and a muscle relaxant so the procedure is safe and humane. Under the 2017 Act, unmodified ECT is not permitted under any circumstances, and ECT is further prohibited for minors except with special safeguards and Mental Health Review Board approval. Because the statute makes anaesthesia mandatory, ECT without anaesthesia is simply never lawful, which is why the correct response is that it is never allowed under law. The reform reflected a broader rights-based shift in Indian mental health legislation, moving away from the older Mental Health Act of 1987 toward autonomy, dignity, and informed consent, influenced by India's ratification of the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities. Note the option framing this as permissible in emergencies is a distractor: emergency provisions in the Act allow treatment, but never the specifically prohibited unmodified form, so the blanket prohibition governs. (No official key released; answer expert-determined and flagged as debatable.)","topic":"MHCA 2017 unmodified ECT ban"},{"q":"A client reports a persistent pattern of seeking admiration, but their behavior is characterized by excessive emotionality, attention-seeking, and seductive behavior, rather than grandiosity. This presentation is more characteristic of:","options":{"a":"Narcissistic Personality Disorder","b":"Histrionic Personality Disorder","c":"Borderline Personality Disorder","d":"Dependent Personality Disorder"},"correct":"b","section":"disorders","explanation":"The answer is histrionic personality disorder, because the picture of excessive emotionality, attention-seeking, and seductive or provocative behavior, rather than grandiosity, is the defining profile of histrionic personality disorder. Understand the Cluster B personality disorders as a set, since examiners test the fine discriminations among them. Cluster B is the dramatic, emotional, and erratic cluster and contains antisocial, borderline, narcissistic, and histrionic personality disorders. Histrionic personality disorder, from a Latin root meaning theatrical or actor-like, describes people who are uncomfortable when not the center of attention and go to lengths to command it: they display rapidly shifting, shallow emotions, use physical appearance and often inappropriately seductive or provocative behavior to draw notice, speak in an impressionistic, vague style, are highly suggestible, and tend to consider relationships more intimate than they are. The crucial exam contrast is with narcissistic personality disorder, because both crave attention and admiration. The discriminator is the mechanism of that craving: the narcissist seeks admiration to confirm a grandiose sense of superiority and specialness, whereas the histrionic person seeks attention through emotional display, charm, and seductiveness without necessarily believing they are superior. The stem hands you the discriminator explicitly by saying the behavior is characterized by emotionality and seductiveness rather than grandiosity, which rules narcissism out and points to histrionic. So the pattern of dramatic, attention-hungry, seductive behavior minus grandiosity equals histrionic personality disorder.","topic":"Histrionic vs narcissistic PD"},{"q":"Duncan's multiple range test, Newman-Keuls test, Tukey's HSD test, and Scheffe test can be used only when:","options":{"a":"F ratio is not significant","b":"F ratio cannot be determined","c":"F ratio is significant","d":"T ratio is not significant"},"correct":"c","section":"research","explanation":"The correct answer is that the F ratio is significant. These four procedures, Duncan's multiple range test, the Newman-Keuls test, John Tukey's Honestly Significant Difference (HSD) test, and Henry Scheffe's test, are all post hoc (a posteriori) multiple comparison tests. They are used to follow up a one-way analysis of variance developed by Ronald Fisher. ANOVA produces an omnibus F ratio that tells you only whether at least one group mean differs from the others; it does not identify which specific pairs differ. Post hoc tests are therefore run only after a significant F establishes that a real difference exists somewhere among the means. Running them when F is non-significant would inflate the family-wise Type I error rate without any justified overall effect to decompose. Each test controls that error differently: Scheffe is the most conservative and flexible for complex contrasts, Tukey's HSD is standard for all pairwise comparisons, and Duncan's is comparatively liberal. The unifying rule tested here is that a significant omnibus F is the entry ticket for any of them.","topic":"Post hoc tests after ANOVA"},{"q":"In operant conditioning, a fixed-ratio schedule of reinforcement produces:","options":{"a":"High, steady response rates with brief pauses after reinforcement.","b":"Low, irregular response rates.","c":"Rapid extinction once reinforcement stops.","d":"Variable response rates depending on reinforcement delay."},"correct":"a","section":"general","explanation":"Schedules of reinforcement, systematized by B. F. Skinner, describe the rule linking responses to reinforcers and each produces a characteristic response pattern. In a fixed-ratio (FR) schedule, reinforcement follows a set number of responses, for example every fifth lever press (FR-5). This generates a high and steady rate of responding, because more responses mean faster reinforcement, followed by a brief post-reinforcement pause immediately after each reinforcer is delivered, producing the classic break-and-run cumulative record. By contrast, fixed-interval schedules yield a scalloped pattern with slow responding after reinforcement that accelerates toward the end of the interval. Variable schedules (variable-ratio and variable-interval) produce very steady responding without post-reinforcement pauses, and variable-ratio schedules, such as those in slot machines, are the most resistant to extinction. Continuous reinforcement extinguishes fastest. Option a correctly pairs the fixed-ratio schedule with high, steady rates plus the characteristic short pause after each reinforcement, which is the textbook signature of this schedule.","topic":"Fixed-ratio reinforcement schedule"},{"q":"How many Rorschach cards are coloured?","options":{"a":"10","b":"5","c":"3","d":"4"},"correct":"b","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is 5. The Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach and published in 1921 in his work Psychodiagnostik, is a projective personality assessment consisting of ten standardized inkblot cards shown one at a time, with the examinee asked what each might be. Of the ten cards, five are achromatic (black and grey only), and five contain colour. Among the coloured cards, two (Cards II and III) combine black with red, and three (Cards VIII, IX, and X) are fully multicoloured. So the count of cards containing any colour is five. This distinction matters interpretively: in scoring systems such as the Comprehensive System of John Exner, responses to the chromatic cards, especially colour-based determinants, are linked to how a person processes and modulates emotion and affect, whereas responses to the achromatic cards and shading relate to other dimensions such as dysphoria and anxiety. Test-takers sometimes confuse this with the total of ten cards or with the three fully coloured cards, but the precise answer to how many cards are coloured is five, making option b correct.","topic":"Rorschach chromatic cards"},{"q":"Norms that suggest to people the way they ought to behave are called:","options":{"a":"Descriptive norms","b":"Idealistic norms","c":"Prescriptive norms","d":"Perceived norms"},"correct":"c","section":"social","explanation":"Norms that tell people how they ought to behave are prescriptive norms, so the answer is c. Social psychology distinguishes two kinds of social norms, a distinction sharpened by Cialdini and colleagues. Descriptive norms describe what people actually do, the typical or common behaviour in a situation, and they influence us by signalling what is likely effective or normal. Prescriptive norms, also called injunctive norms, specify what people ought to do, what behaviour is approved or disapproved of by the group, and they influence us through the prospect of social reward or sanction. The word prescriptive itself carries the sense of prescribing or mandating, which is the cue that it concerns the ought rather than the is. The distinction matters because the two can conflict and can be harnessed in behaviour change campaigns; telling people that most others litter (a descriptive norm of bad behaviour) can backfire, whereas conveying social disapproval of littering (an injunctive or prescriptive norm) more reliably reduces it. So when a question contrasts norms about what is done with norms about what should be done, the should be done category is the prescriptive or injunctive norm. The exam discriminator is the phrase ought to behave, which precisely marks the prescriptive norm rather than the merely descriptive one.","topic":"Prescriptive versus descriptive norms"},{"q":"According to Freud, the id is to the ___ principle as the ego is to the ____ principle.","options":{"a":"aggressive, sexual","b":"sexual, aggressive","c":"pleasure, reality","d":"reality, pleasure"},"correct":"c","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is pleasure, reality. In Sigmund Freud's structural model of the psyche, personality comprises three parts: the id, the ego, and the superego. The id is the most primitive, unconscious part, present from birth, and it operates on the pleasure principle: it demands immediate gratification of instinctual drives and seeks to reduce tension without regard for reality, logic, or consequences. The ego develops to mediate between the id's demands, the constraints of the external world, and the moral standards of the superego, and it operates on the reality principle: it delays gratification and finds realistic, socially acceptable ways to satisfy the id's urges. Thus the id is to the pleasure principle as the ego is to the reality principle. The distractors confuse principles with drive contents: aggressive and sexual (options a and b) refer to Freud's two basic instincts, Eros (the life or sexual drive) and Thanatos (the death or aggressive drive), not to the operating principles of these structures. Option d simply reverses the correct pairing. This distinction is fundamental to psychoanalytic theory because the constant negotiation between the pleasure-seeking id and the reality-oriented ego, refereed by the superego, is Freud's account of how personality functions and how inner conflict and anxiety arise.","topic":"Freud id ego operating principles"},{"q":"Identify the techniques of Functional Neuroimaging:","options":{"a":"CT scan","b":"Magnetic Resonance Imaging","c":"Positron emission tomography (PET) & Single photon emission computed tomography (SPECT)","d":"All of the above"},"correct":"c","section":"physio","explanation":"PET and SPECT are the functional neuroimaging techniques because they measure brain activity (function) rather than just brain structure. The fundamental divide in neuroimaging is between structural and functional methods. Structural imaging shows the anatomy of the brain, its physical shape and any lesions or atrophy: this includes the CT scan, which uses X-rays to build cross-sectional images, and conventional MRI, which uses strong magnetic fields and radiofrequency pulses to produce highly detailed images of soft tissue. Functional imaging, by contrast, captures the brain in action by tracking physiological activity such as blood flow, glucose metabolism, or oxygen use, which rise in regions that are working harder. Positron emission tomography (PET) injects a radioactive tracer, often labeled glucose, and detects the gamma rays emitted, mapping which regions consume the most energy during a task. Single photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) works on a similar radiotracer principle, measuring regional cerebral blood flow. Both reveal what the brain is doing, not merely what it looks like. (Functional MRI, which detects the BOLD signal from blood oxygenation, is also a functional technique, but plain MRI as listed here is structural.) So the correct grouping of functional methods is PET and SPECT.","topic":"Functional versus structural neuroimaging"},{"q":"According to the schema theory, which hypothetical construct provides a prototype for subsequent attachments and can be adapted through more experience?","options":{"a":"Proximity-seeking mechanism","b":"Attachment system","c":"Bio-social relationship model","d":"Internal working model"},"correct":"d","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is the internal working model. John Bowlby, the founder of attachment theory, proposed that through early interactions with the primary caregiver, an infant builds an internal working model, a mental representation or schema of the self, of attachment figures, and of relationships in general. This cognitive-affective template captures whether the self is worthy of love and whether others can be trusted to be available and responsive. Crucially, the internal working model acts as a prototype that guides expectations, feelings, and behaviour in later relationships, including friendships and adult romantic bonds, which is the basis of the continuity hypothesis. Bowlby argued that the model is not fixed: while it tends to be stable, it can be adapted and updated through significant new relational experiences over time. This connects to the schema concept in cognitive psychology, where schemas are organised knowledge structures that shape perception and can accommodate new information. The distractors name real attachment ideas (proximity-seeking behaviour, the attachment behavioural system) but not the internal representational template that serves as a prototype. This item tests understanding of Bowlby's internal working model as the mental blueprint for future attachments.","topic":"Bowlby internal working model"},{"q":"Within the cognitive approach, the ABC model describes:","options":{"a":"A= activating event; B= beliefs about oneself; C= consequences of the beliefs about oneself.","b":"A= activating events; B= beliefs about the events; C= consequences of the beliefs about the event","c":"A= activating event; B= beliefs about the events; C= critical analysis of the beliefs about the event.","d":"A= anticipatory event; B= beliefs about the event; D= consequences of the beliefs about the event."},"correct":"b","section":"clinical","explanation":"The correct answer is A equals activating events, B equals beliefs about the events, and C equals consequences of the beliefs about the event. The ABC model is the cornerstone of Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), developed by Albert Ellis, and it is foundational to the broader cognitive approach to emotional disturbance. The model's central insight is that it is not events themselves that directly cause our emotional and behavioural reactions, but rather our beliefs and interpretations about those events. In the model, A stands for the Activating event or adversity (the situation or trigger), B stands for the Beliefs the person holds about that event (which may be rational or irrational), and C stands for the emotional and behavioural Consequences that follow, which are produced by B rather than directly by A. For example, failing an exam (A) does not itself cause despair; rather, the irrational belief 'I must always succeed and I am worthless if I fail' (B) produces the depressive consequence (C). Ellis later extended the model to D (Disputing the irrational beliefs) and E (the new Effective philosophy or emotional outcome), which is why option C wrongly assigns 'critical analysis' to C; disputing is a separate step. Options A and D distort the definitions of the components. This item tests correct knowledge of Ellis's ABC model.","topic":"Ellis ABC model"},{"q":"A person is categorised as severe mentally retarded if the IQ is between","options":{"a":"50-70","b":"35-49","c":"21-34","d":"0-20"},"correct":"c","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is 21 to 34. In the traditional classification of intellectual disability (historically termed mental retardation) based on IQ scores, using systems such as the ICD and older DSM criteria, the levels are divided into four bands. Mild intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ of roughly 50 to 70, where individuals can often achieve basic academic and vocational skills and live relatively independently with support. Moderate intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ of about 35 to 49, with individuals able to learn self-care and simple communication but needing more support. Severe intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ of approximately 20 to 34 (the question's 21 to 34), where individuals have very limited communication and require substantial daily support. Profound intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ below 20, with pervasive support needs across all areas. The distractors correspond to the adjacent bands: 50 to 70 is mild (option a), 35 to 49 is moderate (option b), and 0 to 20 is profound (option d). It is worth noting that the DSM-5 has shifted away from relying primarily on IQ cutoffs toward assessing adaptive functioning across conceptual, social, and practical domains, following the framework of the American Association on Intellectual and Developmental Disabilities. Still, knowing these classic IQ bands matters for assessment questions and for understanding the historical grading of severity.","topic":"Intellectual disability IQ severity bands"},{"q":"A researcher is interested in studying the effect of noise on the problem-solving abilities of elementary school students. She uses two classes of fourth-grade students and tests them on a 10-problem test. When one class takes the test, she turns on a radio, keeping it off for the other class. This is an example of a :","options":{"a":"true experiment","b":"quasi-experiment","c":"correlational study","d":"naturalistic observation"},"correct":"b","section":"research","explanation":"The correct answer is a quasi-experiment. The defining feature that separates a true experiment from a quasi-experiment, as detailed by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley, is random assignment of participants to conditions. In a true experiment, each individual participant is randomly assigned to the treatment or control condition, which makes the groups equivalent on average and licenses causal inference. In this study, the researcher does manipulate an independent variable (noise present versus absent) and measures a dependent variable (problem-solving performance), so it is experimental in form rather than correlational or observational. However, she does not randomly assign individual students; instead she uses two intact, pre-existing classes and assigns the noise condition to one whole class and the quiet condition to the other. Because the groups are naturally occurring rather than formed by random assignment, any pre-existing differences between the two classes (in ability, teacher, prior noise exposure, and so on) are confounded with the noise manipulation, weakening internal validity. This use of a manipulated variable with non-randomly-assigned, intact groups is precisely what defines a quasi-experiment. It is not naturalistic observation, since the researcher actively manipulates the noise, and it is not merely correlational, since there is a manipulated cause. Therefore option b is correct.","topic":"Quasi-experiment intact groups"},{"q":"Premack Principle explains:","options":{"a":"A process of Interpersonal relationship","b":"A technique of behaviour modification in children","c":"Pattern of language development","d":"Impact of parenting on behavioural problems in young children"},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The Premack principle is a behaviour modification technique, especially useful with children, so the answer is b. Formulated by David Premack, the principle states that a more probable or preferred behaviour can be used to reinforce a less probable or less preferred behaviour. In plainer terms, access to a high frequency, enjoyable activity is made contingent on first performing a low frequency, less appealing one. The everyday version is grandma's rule: you may have your dessert (preferred) only after you eat your vegetables (less preferred). In practice with children this becomes a powerful and easily applied reinforcement strategy: a child may play video games (high probability behaviour) only after finishing homework (low probability behaviour), so the preferred activity reinforces completion of the chore. The theoretical importance is that Premack reframed reinforcement relativistically; rather than reinforcers being a fixed class of stimuli, any behaviour can reinforce another as long as it is more probable, which depends on the individual and the moment. This made reinforcement more flexible and individualised than Skinner's original notion. For the exam, the discriminating idea is the contingency of a preferred activity upon a less preferred one as a method of behaviour change, which is exactly a technique of behaviour modification rather than a theory of relationships, language, or parenting impact.","topic":"Premack principle"},{"q":"What is the age range valid for Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, 5th edition (WISC-V)?","options":{"a":"5 years to 12 years","b":"6 years to 16 years 11 months","c":"7 years to 16 years 11 months","d":"6 years to 15 years 11 months"},"correct":"b","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is 6 years to 16 years 11 months. The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC), originally developed by David Wechsler, is an individually administered test of intelligence for school-age children, and its fifth edition, the WISC-V, is validated for children aged 6 years 0 months through 16 years 11 months. This age band sits between the two other Wechsler instruments: the Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence (WPPSI) covers younger children (roughly 2 years 6 months to 7 years 7 months), and the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS) covers ages 16 and above, with a slight overlap around age 16. Wechsler's scales moved beyond a single ratio IQ (William Stern, 1912) to yield a Full Scale IQ derived from multiple index scores; in the WISC-V these are the Verbal Comprehension, Visual Spatial, Fluid Reasoning, Working Memory, and Processing Speed indexes. A key Wechsler innovation was the use of the deviation IQ, in which a person's score is expressed relative to the distribution of same-age peers (mean 100, standard deviation 15) rather than as a mental-age ratio. Because the WISC-V specifically spans from age 6 to 16 years 11 months, option b is correct.","topic":"WISC-V age range"},{"q":"'The more we see and interact with a person, the more likely he or she is to become our friend or sexual partner.' This denotes:","options":{"a":"Propinquity effect","b":"Exposure effect","c":"Matching hypothesis","d":"Reciprocity"},"correct":"a","section":"social","explanation":"The statement that the more we see and interact with someone the more likely they are to become a friend or partner denotes the propinquity effect, so the answer is a. The propinquity effect (from propinquity, meaning nearness or proximity) is the social psychological finding that physical and functional proximity is one of the strongest predictors of attraction and the formation of relationships. People are far more likely to befriend, like, and partner with those they encounter frequently, neighbours, classmates, coworkers, those whose paths cross theirs by virtue of where they live, study, or work. The classic demonstration is Festinger, Schachter, and Back's study of friendship in a student housing complex, which found that friendships formed overwhelmingly among people whose apartments were close together and whose functional distance (likelihood of incidental contact) was small. The mechanism behind propinquity is closely tied to the mere exposure effect, Zajonc's finding that repeated exposure to a stimulus increases liking for it, and to the availability and ease of interaction that proximity affords. The question's phrasing emphasises seeing and interacting with a person, the contact afforded by nearness, which is the defining cause in the propinquity effect. The exam discriminator is that propinquity stresses proximity and repeated contact as the route to relationship formation, the most precise label for the stated principle.","topic":"Propinquity effect attraction"},{"q":"Allport believed that traits could be organised into three levels:","options":{"a":"primary, secondary, tertiary","b":"cognitive, emotional, physiological","c":"id, ego, superego","d":"cardinal, central, secondary"},"correct":"d","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is cardinal, central, and secondary. Gordon Allport, a pioneer of the trait approach to personality, proposed that traits, enduring dispositions that make a person's behaviour consistent across situations, could be organised into three hierarchical levels. Cardinal traits are rare, dominant traits that shape virtually every aspect of a person's life and behaviour, so pervasive that the person may become known for them (for example, a personality so generous it is called Christlike); most people do not have a cardinal trait. Central traits are the general characteristics that form the basic building blocks of personality, the handful of core qualities (such as honest, kind, or anxious) one might use to describe someone. Secondary traits are more situation-specific and peripheral, such as preferences or attitudes that appear only under particular circumstances. The distractors belong to other frameworks: id, ego, and superego (option c) are Sigmund Freud's structural components of the psyche, not traits; while primary/secondary/tertiary (option a) and cognitive/emotional/physiological (option b) are generic labels not used by Allport. Understanding Allport's hierarchy matters because he helped establish the trait perspective and the idiographic study of individual uniqueness, foundational to modern personality psychology and later trait models like the Big Five.","topic":"Allport cardinal central secondary traits"},{"q":"The primary brain region responsible for the comprehension of spoken and written language is:","options":{"a":"Broca's area (left inferior frontal gyrus)","b":"Wernicke's area (left superior temporal gyrus)","c":"Primary Auditory Cortex (Heschl's gyrus)","d":"Angular Gyrus"},"correct":"b","section":"physio","explanation":"The answer is Wernicke's area, located in the left superior temporal gyrus, because it is the primary brain region responsible for the comprehension of spoken and written language. Understand the classic language model, the Wernicke-Geschwind model, which organizes the two great language areas by function. Carl Wernicke, a German neurologist, described in 1874 patients who could speak fluently but produced meaningless, jumbled output and could not understand what was said to them, and he localized the responsible damage to the posterior superior temporal gyrus of the left hemisphere, now called Wernicke's area. This region sits near the auditory cortex and is specialized for decoding the meaning of language, both heard and read. Damage there produces Wernicke's aphasia, also called receptive or fluent aphasia: speech remains grammatically smooth and effortless in its melody but is full of paraphasias and neologisms, empty of meaning (word salad), and comprehension is severely impaired, so the patient often does not realize their speech makes no sense. Contrast this with Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus, discovered by Paul Broca in the 1860s, which handles speech production and grammar; its damage causes non-fluent, effortful, telegraphic speech with relatively preserved comprehension. The two are connected by a white-matter tract, the arcuate fasciculus, whose damage causes conduction aphasia. Because the question asks specifically about comprehension of language, the answer is Wernicke's area in the superior temporal gyrus.","topic":"Wernicke's area language comprehension"},{"q":"Type D (disorganised) babies are:","options":{"a":"Characterised by conspicuous avoidance of proximity to or interaction with the mother in the reunion episodes","b":"Characterised by actively seeking and maintaining proximity, contact or interaction with the mother, especially in the reunion episodes","c":"Characterised by conspicuous contact- and interaction-resisting behaviour in the reunion episodes","d":"Characterised by no one clear pattern, but show inconsistent and often bizarre responses to separation/reunion"},"correct":"d","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is that Type D babies show no one clear pattern, but display inconsistent and often bizarre responses to separation and reunion. The disorganised or disoriented attachment category (Type D) was identified by Mary Main and Judith Solomon as an addition to Mary Ainsworth's original three-category system (secure Type B, avoidant Type A, resistant or ambivalent Type C). Disorganised infants lack a coherent, organised strategy for coping with distress in the Strange Situation. Instead they exhibit contradictory, confused, and sometimes odd behaviours: they may freeze, rock, approach the caregiver then abruptly move away, show fearful or dazed expressions, or fall to the floor. This pattern is often associated with frightening, abusive, or severely inconsistent caregiving, where the caregiver is simultaneously the source of comfort and the source of fear, leaving the infant in an unsolvable approach-avoidance conflict (Kurt Lewin, 1935). Disorganised attachment is the strongest attachment predictor of later psychopathology. The distractors correctly describe the three organised patterns (avoidant, secure, and resistant respectively), which is why they are wrong for Type D. This item tests recognition of the defining incoherent, disoriented quality of disorganised attachment.","topic":"Disorganised Type D attachment"},{"q":"Which is the correct existing legislation for mental health services in India?","options":{"a":"Mental Health Act, 1987","b":"Persons with Mental Illness Act, 2016","c":"Mental Health Care Act, 2017","d":"Indian Mental Health Act, 2018"},"correct":"c","section":"clinical","explanation":"The correct current legislation is the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017, so the answer is c. The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 came into force in 2018 and repealed and replaced the earlier Mental Health Act of 1987. It marked a major rights based shift in Indian mental health law, aligning the country with the United Nations Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, which India ratified. Its central features include a rights based approach that guarantees access to mental healthcare and treatment; the decriminalisation of attempted suicide, presuming that a person who attempts suicide is under severe stress and should not be punished; the provision for an advance directive, allowing a person to state in advance how they wish to be treated and to appoint a nominated representative; the establishment of Mental Health Review Boards to protect rights and oversee admissions; regulation of admission, treatment, and the use of restraints and electroconvulsive therapy (Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini, 1938); and a definition of mental illness and of capacity to make treatment decisions. For Indian clinical psychology exams this is essential, current statutory knowledge. The discriminating skill is to recognise that the 1987 Act is repealed and superseded, and that the options Persons with Mental Illness Act 2016 and Indian Mental Health Act 2018 are fabricated or misnamed, leaving the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 as the genuine governing law.","topic":"Mental Healthcare Act 2017"},{"q":"Within attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder, which of the following is NOT classified as a problem relating to hyperactivity/impulsivity?","options":{"a":"Experiences difficulties in playing quietly.","b":"Rather than sitting still, squirms in seat and fidgets.","c":"Talks excessively.","d":"Doesn't appear to listen when being told something or spoken to"},"correct":"d","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is that not appearing to listen when spoken to is NOT a hyperactivity/impulsivity symptom, because it belongs instead to the inattention cluster. Attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is a neurodevelopmental disorder whose DSM criteria are organised into two symptom domains. The inattention domain includes symptoms such as failing to give close attention to details, difficulty sustaining attention, not seeming to listen when spoken to directly, failing to follow through on instructions, difficulty organising tasks, avoiding tasks requiring sustained mental effort, losing things, being easily distracted, and forgetfulness. The hyperactivity/impulsivity domain includes fidgeting and squirming, leaving one's seat, running or climbing inappropriately, being unable to play quietly, being 'on the go' as if driven by a motor, talking excessively, blurting out answers, difficulty waiting one's turn, and interrupting others. ADHD presentations can be predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive-impulsive, or combined. In this item, options A (difficulty playing quietly), B (squirming and fidgeting), and C (talking excessively) are all hyperactivity/impulsivity symptoms, whereas option D (not appearing to listen) is an inattention symptom, making it the correct exception. This item tests the student's ability to sort ADHD symptoms into the correct diagnostic cluster.","topic":"ADHD symptom clusters"},{"q":"Which scale is used when counting the number of books a person has?","options":{"a":"Nominal Scale","b":"Ordinal Scale","c":"Interval Scale","d":"Ratio Scale"},"correct":"d","section":"research","explanation":"Choosing the measurement scale, per the taxonomy of Stanley Smith Stevens, depends on the properties of the variable. Counting the number of books a person owns produces a ratio scale variable. A ratio scale has all the properties of the lower scales, distinct categories (nominal), meaningful order (ordinal), and equal intervals between values (interval), plus the crucial additional feature of a true, absolute zero point. Zero books literally means the complete absence of books, a genuine and meaningful zero, not an arbitrary reference point. This true zero is what permits ratio statements: someone with 20 books has twice as many as someone with 10, and such multiplicative comparisons are valid only on a ratio scale. Counts of discrete objects, along with weight, height, age, and reaction time, are classic ratio-scale variables. The distractors fail because a nominal scale has no quantity, an ordinal scale has no equal intervals, and an interval scale (such as Celsius temperature) lacks a true zero. Because a book count has a meaningful zero and supports ratios, option d is correct.","topic":"Ratio scale count data"},{"q":"Among the following schedules of reinforcement, which typically produces the highest rate of response and is most resistant to extinction?","options":{"a":"Fixed Ratio","b":"Variable Ratio","c":"Fixed Interval","d":"Variable Interval"},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The answer is variable ratio, because among the schedules of reinforcement the variable ratio schedule produces the highest rate of responding and the greatest resistance to extinction. Understand why this schedule is so powerful within B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning framework. A variable ratio schedule reinforces a response after an unpredictable number of responses that varies around some average; for instance, on a VR-10 schedule reinforcement comes on average every tenth response, but the actual number required fluctuates unpredictably from one reinforcement to the next. Two properties combine to make this schedule uniquely potent. First, because it is a ratio schedule, reinforcement depends directly on how many responses the organism makes, so the faster the organism responds, the more reinforcement it earns, which drives a very high, sustained rate of responding. Second, because the ratio is variable and unpredictable, the organism can never know which particular response will be the one that pays off, so the very next response might be rewarded, and this uncertainty keeps the organism responding vigorously and continuously without the post-reinforcement pauses seen in fixed schedules. This same unpredictability makes variable ratio schedules extraordinarily resistant to extinction: when reinforcement stops, the organism cannot easily tell the difference between an unusually long unrewarded run (which was normal during training) and actual extinction, so it keeps responding for a long time. The prototypical real-world example is gambling on a slot machine, which pays off on a variable ratio schedule and hooks players into persistent, rapid play. So for the highest and most extinction-resistant responding, the answer is variable ratio.","topic":"Variable ratio extinction resistance"},{"q":"What is psychometric reliability primarily concerned with?","options":{"a":"Free from measurement error","b":"Consistency of a test","c":"Dependability of a test scores","d":"All of the above"},"correct":"d","section":"assessment","explanation":"Psychometric reliability refers to the consistency, stability, and dependability of the scores a test yields, and it can be expressed in several complementary ways, all of which describe the same underlying property. In classical test theory, an observed score is modeled as a true score plus random error, and reliability is the degree to which a test is free from such measurement error (option a), which is one legitimate way to define it. Reliability is equally described as the consistency of a test across occasions, items, or raters (option b), captured by test-retest, internal consistency such as coefficient alpha of Lee Cronbach, and inter-rater reliability. And it is described as the dependability of the scores (option c), meaning one can trust that similar conditions will yield similar results. Because freedom from error, consistency, and dependability are all valid and overlapping characterizations of the single concept of reliability, the most complete and correct answer encompasses all of them. Therefore option d, all of the above, is correct.","topic":"Definition of reliability"},{"q":"How people organize information in memory and use it to understand behavior is called:","options":{"a":"Motivational approach","b":"Schema approach","c":"Perceptual approach","d":"Organizational approach"},"correct":"b","section":"social","explanation":"How people organise information in memory and use it to understand behaviour is the schema approach, so the answer is b. A schema is an organised mental structure or framework of knowledge about a concept, object, person, situation, or event, built up from experience, that guides how we perceive, interpret, encode, remember, and act on new information. The concept traces to Bartlett's classic memory research, showing that people reconstruct rather than reproduce memories, fitting them to pre existing schemas, and it was developed further by Piaget in cognitive development and by social and cognitive psychologists. In social cognition, schemas (including person schemas, role schemas, self schemas, and event schemas or scripts) are central to how we make sense of the social world and understand and predict behaviour, allowing us to process complex information efficiently, fill in gaps with default assumptions, and form expectations, though at the cost of biases and stereotyping. The schema approach therefore refers precisely to the framework that studies how knowledge is organised in memory and used to interpret and explain behaviour. The exam discriminator is matching organising information in memory and using it to understand behaviour to the schema construct, distinguishing it from motivational, perceptual, or vague organisational approaches that do not capture the specific knowledge structure idea.","topic":"Schemas in social cognition"},{"q":"Which of the following traits is not part of the five-factor model of personality?","options":{"a":"openness","b":"anxiety","c":"extraversion","d":"agreeableness"},"correct":"b","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is anxiety, which is not one of the five factors. The five-factor model of personality, also called the Big Five, was developed and validated by researchers including Paul Costa and Robert McCrae, building on earlier lexical work by Lewis Goldberg. It identifies five broad dimensions that capture the structure of human personality, commonly remembered by the acronym OCEAN: Openness to experience (imaginative, curious versus conventional), Conscientiousness (organised, dependable versus careless), Extraversion (sociable, energetic versus reserved), Agreeableness (compassionate, cooperative versus antagonistic), and Neuroticism (emotionally unstable, anxious versus calm). Among the options, openness (a), extraversion (c), and agreeableness (d) are all genuine factors. Anxiety (b) is not itself one of the five factors; rather, it is a facet or component of the broader dimension of neuroticism, which encompasses anxiety, hostility, depression, and vulnerability to stress. The trap is that anxiety feels central to personality and does relate to the model, but it sits under neuroticism rather than standing as its own factor. Knowing the correct five dimensions matters because the Big Five is the most empirically supported and cross-culturally replicated framework in personality psychology, widely used in research, clinical assessment, and organisational settings, so students must distinguish the five factors from their subordinate facets.","topic":"Big Five factors"},{"q":"For which of the following neurotransmitter is Alcohol an agonist?","options":{"a":"Serotonin","b":"Acetylcholine","c":"GABA","d":"Endorphins"},"correct":"c","section":"physio","explanation":"The correct answer is GABA. Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) is the brain's primary inhibitory neurotransmitter; when it binds the GABA-A receptor it opens a chloride channel that hyperpolarizes the neuron, reducing its likelihood of firing and thereby dampening neural activity. Ethanol acts as a positive modulator, functionally an agonist, at the GABA-A receptor: it enhances GABA's inhibitory effect, potentiating chloride influx and increasing central nervous system inhibition. This is the mechanism behind alcohol's characteristic sedative, anxiolytic, muscle-relaxant, and coordination-impairing effects, and it is the same receptor system targeted by benzodiazepines and barbiturates, which is why these drugs are cross-tolerant and dangerous when combined with alcohol. Alcohol simultaneously antagonizes the excitatory glutamate NMDA receptor, adding to its depressant profile and contributing to memory blackouts. Chronic use produces neuroadaptation of the GABA system, so that withdrawal causes rebound hyperexcitability, tremor, and seizures. While alcohol also touches serotonin, dopamine, and endorphin systems in ways that shape its rewarding effects, its defining agonist action for exam purposes is at GABA.","topic":"Alcohol as GABA agonist"},{"q":"According to Erik Erikson's stages of psychosocial development, the primary crisis to be resolved in adolescence (12-18 years) is:","options":{"a":"Industry vs. Inferiority","b":"Identity vs. Role Confusion","c":"Intimacy vs. Isolation","d":"Generativity vs. Stagnation"},"correct":"b","section":"dev","explanation":"The answer is identity vs. role confusion, because in Erik Erikson's theory of psychosocial development, the central crisis of adolescence, roughly ages 12 to 18, is identity versus role confusion. Understand Erikson's framework as a whole, because his eight stages are examined frequently and always as a matching task between life period, crisis, and resulting virtue. Erik Erikson, a neo-Freudian ego psychologist, expanded Freud's psychosexual stages into a lifespan theory of eight psychosocial stages, each defined by a conflict between two opposing tendencies that the developing person must resolve, with successful resolution yielding a particular strength or virtue. The stages are trust versus mistrust in infancy (virtue hope), autonomy versus shame and doubt in toddlerhood (will), initiative versus guilt in the preschool years (purpose), industry versus inferiority in the school years (competence), identity versus role confusion in adolescence (fidelity), intimacy versus isolation in young adulthood (love), generativity versus stagnation in middle adulthood (care), and integrity versus despair in late life (wisdom). Adolescence is the pivotal stage in Erikson's theory. During this period the young person struggles to answer the question who am I, integrating their various roles, values, beliefs, and aspirations into a coherent, stable sense of self, or identity. Failure to do so results in role confusion, a diffuse, uncertain sense of self and direction. Erikson introduced related ideas such as the identity crisis and the psychosocial moratorium, a period of exploration before commitment. The virtue emerging from successful resolution is fidelity. So the adolescent crisis is identity versus role confusion.","topic":"Erikson identity vs role confusion"},{"q":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy is indicated for the treatment of:","options":{"a":"Anxiety Disorders","b":"Borderline Personality Disorder","c":"Acute and Transient Psychotic Disorder","d":"Post-traumatic Stress Disorder"},"correct":"b","section":"clinical","explanation":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy is indicated for borderline personality disorder, so the answer is b. DBT was developed by Marsha Linehan specifically for chronically suicidal and self harming individuals, most of whom met criteria for borderline personality disorder, a condition marked by pervasive emotional dysregulation, unstable relationships and self image, impulsivity, recurrent self harm, and intense fear of abandonment. DBT integrates cognitive behavioural change strategies with acceptance based and mindfulness practices drawn from Zen, organised around the dialectic of acceptance and change: the therapist validates the patient's experience while simultaneously pressing for behavioural change. Its structure typically includes weekly individual therapy, group skills training, telephone coaching for in the moment crises, and a therapist consultation team. The four skills modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness, which directly target the deficits underlying borderline pathology. DBT has the strongest evidence base of any treatment for BPD, reducing suicide attempts, self harm, hospitalisations, and dropout. While DBT and its adaptations are now applied to other conditions involving emotion dysregulation, its original and primary indication, and the keyed answer, is borderline personality disorder. The exam discriminator is the tight historical and clinical pairing of Linehan, emotion dysregulation, self harm, and BPD that defines DBT.","topic":"DBT for borderline PD"},{"q":"Delusion of infidelity on part of the sexual partner is known as:","options":{"a":"Ekbom’s Syndrome","b":"Othello syndrome","c":"de clérambault’s syndrome","d":"Couvade syndrome"},"correct":"b","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is Othello syndrome. Othello syndrome is the eponymous name for delusional jealousy, the fixed, false belief that one's sexual partner is being unfaithful, held with delusional conviction despite no real evidence. It is named after Shakespeare's character Othello, who, convinced by manipulation that his faithful wife Desdemona has betrayed him, ultimately kills her. Clinically, the delusion of infidelity can occur as a primary delusional disorder (jealous type), or secondary to schizophrenia, mood disorders, organic brain disease, or, importantly, chronic alcohol misuse, where it is a recognised complication. The condition is significant in forensic psychiatry because the intense, controlling jealousy carries a real risk of violence toward the partner, who may be stalked, interrogated, and physically harmed. The patient typically seeks proof, checks the partner's belongings, and misinterprets innocent behaviour as evidence of betrayal. Recognising delusional jealousy as a psychiatric symptom rather than ordinary suspicion is clinically crucial. This question tests knowledge of the eponymous syndromes that cluster around delusional disorders, each tied to a specific delusional theme.","topic":"Othello syndrome delusional jealousy"},{"q":"What would be the degrees of freedom for chi-square based on a 4 x 5 contingency table?","options":{"a":"5","b":"9","c":"12","d":"20"},"correct":"c","section":"research","explanation":"For a chi-square test on a contingency table the degrees of freedom equal (rows minus one) multiplied by (columns minus one), so for a 4 by 5 table the calculation is (4 minus 1) times (5 minus 1), which is 3 times 4, equalling 12, making c correct. To understand why, recall what degrees of freedom mean: they represent the number of values in a calculation that are free to vary once certain constraints are imposed. In a contingency table the row totals and column totals (the marginal totals) are treated as fixed. Given those fixed marginals, once you know all but the last cell in each row and each column, the remaining cells are determined automatically, because each row and each column must sum to its known marginal. This is why one degree of freedom is subtracted from both the number of rows and the number of columns. The chi-square test of independence itself compares observed frequencies in each cell against the frequencies expected if the two categorical variables were independent, summing the squared differences divided by expected counts. The degrees of freedom then determine which chi-square distribution to compare the statistic against in order to find the critical value and significance. Getting the degrees of freedom right is essential because using the wrong value leads to an incorrect critical threshold and a wrong conclusion about statistical significance.","topic":"Chi-square degrees of freedom"},{"q":"The James-Lange or body reaction theory of emotion says:","options":{"a":"you feel emotion then a bodily response","b":"you react with your body first, then you feel emotion","c":"the somatic nervous system is the seat of emotion","d":"emotions and visceral reactions are simultaneous"},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is that you react with your body first, then you feel emotion. The James-Lange theory of emotion, proposed independently by William James and Carl Lange in the late 19th century, reverses the commonsense view of emotion. Rather than an event causing an emotion that then produces a bodily reaction, James and Lange argued that a stimulus first triggers a physiological or bodily response (racing heart, trembling, sweating), and our perception or interpretation of that bodily change is the emotion. In James's famous formulation, we do not tremble because we are afraid; we are afraid because we tremble. Thus the bodily reaction comes first and the felt emotion follows from perceiving it. Option a describes the opposite, commonsense sequence that the theory explicitly rejects. Option d, that emotion and visceral reaction occur simultaneously, describes the rival Cannon-Bard theory of Walter Cannon and Philip Bard, which held that the thalamus sends signals producing the emotional experience and the bodily response at the same time. Option c misstates the theory by invoking the somatic rather than the visceral, autonomic responses James-Lange emphasised. Understanding James-Lange matters because it launched the scientific debate over whether physiology drives emotion, a question later extended by the Schachter-Singer two-factor theory adding cognitive appraisal.","topic":"James-Lange theory of emotion"},{"q":"A test developer administers a new anxiety scale to a group of college students and, two weeks later, administers the same scale again to the same group. She then correlates the two sets of scores. This process is used to establish:","options":{"a":"Split-half reliability","b":"Inter-rater reliability","c":"Test-retest reliability","d":"Parallel forms reliability"},"correct":"c","section":"assessment","explanation":"The answer is test-retest reliability, because the defining operation described (same test, same people, two separated time points, correlate the scores) is exactly how test-retest reliability is computed. Understand reliability from the ground up: reliability means consistency of measurement, and psychometricians estimate it in several ways, each capturing a different source of error. Test-retest reliability captures stability over time. You administer the instrument, wait an interval (often two weeks, long enough to prevent literal memory of answers but short enough that the trait itself has not truly changed), administer it again, and correlate the two score sets; the resulting coefficient, called the coefficient of stability, tells you how much of the score reflects an enduring trait rather than momentary fluctuation. The chosen interval matters: too short and carryover memory inflates the correlation, too long and real change deflates it. Distinguish this from the other reliability types the exam pairs against it. Split-half and coefficient alpha assess internal consistency, whether items measure the same construct, from a single sitting. Parallel or alternate forms reliability uses two equivalent versions to check both stability and content equivalence. Inter-rater reliability checks agreement between judges. The signature clue for test-retest is always the mention of the same instrument given twice with a time gap, which is precisely this stem, so test-retest, the coefficient of stability, is correct.","topic":"Test-retest reliability"},{"q":"According to Social identity theory, from what do we derive a large part of our identity and feelings about ourselves?","options":{"a":"Where we were born","b":"The amount of prejudice experienced","c":"The groups we belong to","d":"Competition between groups"},"correct":"c","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is the groups we belong to. Social identity theory was developed by Henri Tajfel and John Turner in the 1970s and 1980s to explain intergroup behaviour. Its central claim is that a significant part of our self-concept, our identity and self-esteem, comes from our membership in social groups (the in-groups) such as our nationality, religion, team, or profession. We categorise ourselves and others into groups, identify with our in-groups, and compare our groups favourably against out-groups to boost self-esteem, a process that helps explain in-group favouritism, prejudice, and discrimination. Option d, competition between groups, describes a consequence of this process rather than the source of identity itself; Tajfel's famous minimal group experiments showed that even arbitrary group assignments trigger favouritism, so competition follows from identification rather than being its origin. Place of birth (option a) is merely a demographic fact, and experienced prejudice (option b) influences but does not define the mechanism. Understanding social identity theory matters because it illuminates why people cling to group loyalties, how collective identity shapes self-worth, and the psychological roots of stereotyping and intergroup conflict, all central to social and clinical psychology.","topic":"Tajfel social identity theory"},{"q":"Which of the following shows the stages in the correct developmental order?","options":{"a":"oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital","b":"latency, oral, genital, anal, phallic","c":"phallic, anal, oral, latency, genital","d":"oral, genital, phallic, latency, anal"},"correct":"a","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud's theory of psychosexual development proposes that personality develops through five stages, each defined by the erogenous zone that is the focus of the child's libidinal energy, and progressing in a fixed sequence from infancy to adulthood. The oral stage (birth to about 18 months) centres on the mouth, with pleasure from sucking and feeding. The anal stage (about 18 months to 3 years) centres on bowel and bladder control, coinciding with toilet training. The phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) centres on the genitals and is when the Oedipus and Electra complexes arise and identification with the same-sex parent occurs. The latency stage (about 6 years to puberty) is a period of dormant sexual interest, during which the child focuses energy on school, friendships, and skill-building. Finally, the genital stage (puberty onward) sees the re-emergence of sexual interest, now directed toward mature adult relationships. Only option a lists these in the correct order; the other options scramble the sequence. According to Freud, fixation at any stage due to over- or under-gratification leaves a lasting mark on adult personality. Although the theory is scientifically contested, knowing the correct order of the psychosexual stages is a staple of exams and remains central to understanding Freud's developmental framework.","topic":"Freud psychosexual stages order"},{"q":"Which part of the brainstem is responsible for controlling vital functions such as breathing and heart rate?","options":{"a":"Midbrain","b":"Pons","c":"Medulla oblongata","d":"Thalamus"},"correct":"c","section":"physio","explanation":"The correct answer is Medulla oblongata. The medulla oblongata is the lowest part of the brainstem, continuous with the spinal cord, and it houses the autonomic centres that control the body's most vital, involuntary functions, including the cardiac (heart rate) centre, the respiratory (breathing) centre, and centres for vasomotor tone, swallowing, coughing, and vomiting. Because these functions are essential to life, damage to the medulla is frequently fatal, which underscores why it is described as controlling vital functions, making option C correct. The midbrain (option A), the uppermost brainstem region, contains structures like the superior and inferior colliculi that mediate visual and auditory reflexes and relay motor and sensory signals, but it does not primarily regulate heartbeat and respiration. The pons (option B), lying between the midbrain and medulla, serves as a bridge relaying information between the cerebrum and cerebellum and contributes to regulating the rhythm of breathing, yet the fundamental control centres for heart rate and respiration reside in the medulla, not the pons. The thalamus (option D) is not part of the brainstem at all; it sits in the diencephalon and acts as the brain's main relay station for sensory information heading to the cortex. Knowing the medulla governs vital autonomic functions is fundamental to neuroanatomy. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)","topic":"Medulla oblongata vital functions"},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of Kohlberg's stages of moral reasoning?","options":{"a":"Conventional","b":"Unconventional","c":"Post-conventional","d":"Pre-conventional"},"correct":"b","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is unconventional, because this is NOT one of Lawrence Kohlberg's levels of moral reasoning. Kohlberg, building on the earlier work of Jean Piaget, proposed a stage theory of moral development organised into three levels, each containing two stages, giving six stages total. The first level is Pre-conventional morality, where moral judgments are based on external consequences: Stage 1 is obedience and punishment orientation, and Stage 2 is instrumental exchange or self-interest ('what is in it for me'). The second level is Conventional morality, where judgments are based on social approval and maintaining order: Stage 3 is good interpersonal relationships or the 'good boy, good girl' orientation, and Stage 4 is authority and social-order maintenance ('law and order'). The third level is Post-conventional morality, where judgments rest on abstract ethical principles: Stage 5 is social contract and individual rights, and Stage 6 is universal ethical principles. Kohlberg derived these from responses to moral dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma. There is no level called 'unconventional' in his framework, making option B the odd one out. Carol Gilligan later critiqued the theory for a possible male bias. This item tests recall of the three correctly named levels.","topic":"Kohlberg moral reasoning levels"},{"q":"Exposure & response prevention is the most effective treatment of:","options":{"a":"Obsessive-compulsive disorder","b":"Social anxiety disorder","c":"Claustrophobia","d":"Panic disorder"},"correct":"a","section":"clinical","explanation":"Exposure and response prevention is the most effective treatment of obsessive compulsive disorder, so the answer is a. OCD is characterised by intrusive, distressing obsessions and by compulsions, repetitive behaviours or mental acts performed to neutralise the anxiety the obsessions provoke. The behavioural model explains the disorder through negative reinforcement: the compulsion temporarily relieves the anxiety, which reinforces the ritual and prevents the person from ever learning that the feared catastrophe would not happen anyway. Exposure and response prevention (ERP), developed by Victor Meyer and refined by Edna Foa and others, directly breaks this cycle. The exposure component has the patient deliberately confront the obsessional triggers, the contaminated doorknob, the unlocked door, the intrusive blasphemous thought, while the response prevention component has them refrain from performing the compulsive ritual. By staying in contact with the trigger without ritualising, the patient experiences that anxiety habituates and declines on its own and that the feared outcome does not occur, producing extinction of the obsessional fear and the compulsive behaviour. ERP has the strongest evidence base of any psychological treatment for OCD and is the first line behavioural intervention. The exam discriminator is the tight, specific match between ERP's mechanism, confronting triggers while blocking rituals, and the obsession compulsion cycle of OCD, rather than the other anxiety conditions listed.","topic":"ERP for OCD"},{"q":"Panic disorder is where:","options":{"a":"An individual experiences a singular panic attack.","b":"There is excessive anxiety and worry about many different aspects of life.","c":"There is a fear of panicking in enclosed spaces.","d":"There are recurrent and unexpected panic attacks"},"correct":"d","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is that there are recurrent and unexpected panic attacks. Panic disorder is an anxiety disorder defined by recurrent, unexpected panic attacks, which are sudden surges of intense fear or discomfort that peak within minutes and are accompanied by physical and cognitive symptoms such as palpitations, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, chest pain, dizziness, and a fear of dying or losing control. Critically, the attacks come 'out of the blue' without an obvious trigger, and the diagnosis further requires that at least one attack be followed by a month or more of persistent worry about having more attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour to avoid them (anticipatory anxiety). A single attack (option A) is insufficient. This links to the cognitive model of panic by David M. Clark, which holds that panic arises from catastrophic misinterpretation of benign bodily sensations, and to interoceptive conditioning accounts. The distractors describe other conditions: excessive, uncontrollable worry across many life domains is generalised anxiety disorder, and fear of enclosed spaces is a specific phobia or relates to agoraphobia. Panic disorder frequently co-occurs with agoraphobia (fear of situations where escape is difficult). This item tests the defining feature of recurrent unexpected panic attacks.","topic":"Panic disorder definition"},{"q":"When comparing two independent samples and the data does not meet the assumptions for parametric testing, which non-parametric test is most commonly used?","options":{"a":"Wilcoxon signed-rank test","b":"Mann-Whitney U test","c":"Kruskal-Wallis test","d":"Friedman test"},"correct":"b","section":"research","explanation":"The answer is the Mann-Whitney U test because it is the standard non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test, used when you have two separate, unrelated groups and the data violate parametric assumptions (non-normal, ordinal, or very small samples). It was developed by Henry Mann and Donald Whitney in 1947, building on earlier work by Frank Wilcoxon, and it is mathematically equivalent to the Wilcoxon rank-sum test. Instead of comparing means, it pools all the scores from both groups, ranks them from lowest to highest, and then asks whether the ranks tend to cluster higher in one group than the other, which tells you whether the two distributions differ. Because it works on ranks rather than raw values, it makes no assumption about the shape of the population distribution. The crucial exam skill is matching the test to the design along two dimensions: how many groups, and whether they are independent or related. Two independent groups without normality gives Mann-Whitney U; two related or paired groups gives Wilcoxon signed-rank; three or more independent groups gives Kruskal-Wallis; three or more related conditions gives Friedman. Keeping that two-by-two grid in mind lets you eliminate the distractors instantly.","topic":"Mann-Whitney U test"},{"q":"Which stage is characterised by realising your full potential according to Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs?","options":{"a":"Safety Needs","b":"Social Needs","c":"Esteem Needs","d":"Self-Actualisation Needs"},"correct":"d","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is self-actualisation needs. Abraham Maslow proposed a hierarchy of needs, usually depicted as a pyramid with five levels arranged from most basic to most advanced: physiological needs (food, water, sleep), safety needs (security, stability), love and belonging (social) needs, esteem needs (respect, achievement, recognition), and at the very top, self-actualisation. Maslow argued that lower needs must be reasonably satisfied before higher ones become motivating. Self-actualisation, the apex of the hierarchy, is the drive to realise one's full potential, to become everything one is capable of becoming, and to pursue personal growth, creativity, and meaning. Maslow described self-actualised individuals as autonomous, accepting, spontaneous, problem-centred, and capable of peak experiences, and he studied exemplary figures such as Abraham Lincoln and Albert Einstein as models. Later he added self-transcendence above self-actualisation, but the classic five-level model tops out at self-actualisation. The distractors are all genuine but lower levels of the hierarchy that concern security, belonging, and status rather than fulfilment of potential. This item tests recall of the top tier of Maslow's humanistic motivational model and the definition of self-actualisation.","topic":"Maslow self-actualisation"},{"q":"Face validity refers to which of the following:","options":{"a":"Facial expression is used to make a diagnosis","b":"The scale of emotional responding.","c":"The notion that an assessment method may appear to be valid simply because it has questions which intuitively seem relevant to the trait or characteristic being measured.","d":"A construct is a hypothetical or inferred attribute that may not be directly observable or directly measurable"},"correct":"c","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is the notion that an assessment method may appear to be valid simply because it has questions which intuitively seem relevant to the trait being measured. Validity refers to whether a test actually measures what it claims to measure, and it comes in several types. Face validity is the most superficial: it concerns whether a test looks or appears, on its surface and to a layperson or test-taker, as though it measures the intended construct. A test has high face validity if its items obviously relate to the trait (for example, a depression questionnaire that asks about sadness and hopelessness). Importantly, face validity is not a statistical or scientific form of validity; a test can appear valid on its face yet fail to measure the construct accurately, and conversely a test can lack face validity yet be genuinely valid. Face validity matters mainly for practical acceptability and motivation, since tests that look relevant are more readily accepted by respondents, but sometimes low face validity is deliberately used (as in some personality inventories) to reduce faking. This contrasts with content validity, criterion validity, and construct validity (Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl, 1955), which are established through evidence. Option D actually defines a construct, which pertains to construct validity. This item tests the definition of face validity as mere surface appearance of relevance.","topic":"Face validity"},{"q":"Solomon Asch’s classic experiment was arranged to test the limits of:","options":{"a":"Social perception","b":"Conformity","c":"Coercive power","d":"Leadership"},"correct":"b","section":"social","explanation":"The correct answer is conformity. Solomon Asch's classic experiments of the 1950s were designed to test the limits of conformity, specifically how far social pressure from a group could lead an individual to give an answer they knew to be wrong. In the basic procedure, a participant was seated in a group of confederates (people secretly working with the experimenter) and shown a series of simple perceptual judgements: a standard line and three comparison lines, where the task was merely to say which comparison line matched the standard in length. The correct answer was obvious. However, on certain trials the confederates, answering first, all unanimously gave the same wrong answer. Asch found that about a third of the participants' responses (across critical trials) conformed to the group's incorrect judgement, and about 75 percent of participants conformed at least once, even though the correct answer was plainly visible. This demonstrated the power of normative social influence, the desire to fit in and avoid the discomfort of being the odd one out, even when the group is clearly wrong. Asch also showed that conformity dropped sharply when there was just one dissenting ally, when responses were private, or when the group was smaller. The study is a cornerstone of social psychology on conformity.","topic":"Asch conformity experiment"},{"q":"Select the incorrect pair:","options":{"a":"Jung: Extroversion-Introversion","b":"Kohlberg: Formal operations","c":"Adler: Ruling Dominant Type","d":"Ellis: Rational Emotive Therapy"},"correct":"b","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is Kohlberg: Formal operations, because this is the mismatched (incorrect) pair. Formal operations is the fourth and final stage of cognitive development in the theory of Jean Piaget, characterised by abstract, hypothetical, and logical reasoning that emerges in adolescence; it has nothing to do with Lawrence Kohlberg, who is famous instead for his theory of moral development (pre-conventional, conventional, and post-conventional levels). Kohlberg built on Piaget's earlier work on moral reasoning but is not associated with the formal operations stage, so pairing his name with it is wrong. The other three pairs are all correctly matched: Carl Gustav Jung introduced the personality attitudes of extraversion and introversion; Alfred Adler, founder of individual psychology, described personality types including the ruling or dominant type (alongside getting, avoiding, and socially useful types); and Albert Ellis developed Rational Emotive Therapy, later renamed Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), the pioneering cognitive therapy built on the ABC model. This item tests the student's ability to correctly attribute major theories and concepts to their originators, a frequent format in entrance exams.","topic":"Matching theorists to concepts"},{"q":"Parkinson's disease is associated with the degeneration of neurons that produce which neurotransmitter?","options":{"a":"Serotonin","b":"Dopamine","c":"Acetylcholine","d":"GABA"},"correct":"b","section":"physio","explanation":"The correct answer is Dopamine. Parkinson's disease is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder caused primarily by the death of dopamine-producing neurons in a midbrain region called the substantia nigra, part of the basal ganglia circuitry that controls movement. As these dopaminergic neurons degenerate, dopamine levels in the striatum fall, disrupting the fine regulation of voluntary movement and producing the classic motor symptoms: resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia (slowness of movement), and postural instability. This dopamine deficit is why the standard treatment is levodopa, a dopamine precursor that the brain converts into dopamine. Hence option B is correct. Serotonin (option A) is chiefly involved in mood, sleep, and appetite, and its dysregulation is linked to depression and anxiety rather than to Parkinsonian motor decline. Acetylcholine (option C) is essential for muscle activation and for memory, and its loss in the basal forebrain is a feature of Alzheimer's disease, not the defining lesion of Parkinson's, though a dopamine-acetylcholine imbalance does contribute to Parkinson's symptoms. GABA (option D) is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter, implicated in anxiety and epilepsy, not in the dopaminergic degeneration that defines Parkinson's. Understanding the dopamine link is central to the neurochemistry of movement disorders and their pharmacological treatment. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)","topic":"Dopamine in Parkinson's disease"},{"q":"Which of the following is a key characteristic of the \"slow-to-warm-up\" temperament category in the Thomas and Chess model?","options":{"a":"Positive approach to new stimuli and high regularity of biological functions.","b":"Negative withdrawal from new stimuli, slow adaptability, and mild intensity of reactions.","c":"Intense and frequent negative emotional reactions with irregular routines.","d":"A mix of easy and difficult characteristics with no clear pattern."},"correct":"b","section":"dev","explanation":"The answer is negative withdrawal from new stimuli, slow adaptability, and mild intensity of reactions, because that combination is the defining profile of the slow-to-warm-up child in the Thomas and Chess temperament model. Understand the model and this category in particular. Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess, in the New York Longitudinal Study, argued that infants display biologically based behavioral styles, or temperaments, from early life, and they rated babies on nine dimensions including activity level, rhythmicity, approach or withdrawal to novelty, adaptability, intensity of reaction, threshold of responsiveness, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span. Clustering these dimensions, they identified three main temperament types plus an unclassified group. The easy child (about 40 percent) has regular biological rhythms, approaches new situations positively, adapts readily, and shows mild to moderate, mostly positive mood. The difficult child (about 10 percent) has irregular rhythms, withdraws from novelty, adapts slowly, reacts with high intensity, and shows frequent negative mood. The slow-to-warm-up child (about 15 percent) sits between these extremes and is defined by a specific signature: an initial cautious withdrawal from or negative reaction to new stimuli and people, slow adaptation that gradually warms up with repeated, unpressured exposure, and crucially, low or mild intensity of emotional reactions rather than the stormy, high-intensity reactions of the difficult child. Such children are not intensely distressed; they are simply shy and slow to engage, needing patient, gradual exposure. The key contrast with the difficult child is intensity: both may initially withdraw, but the slow-to-warm-up child reacts mildly. So the slow-to-warm-up profile is withdrawal from novelty, slow adaptability, and mild-intensity reactions.","topic":"Thomas-Chess slow-to-warm-up temperament"},{"q":"A client engages in safety behaviors, such as gripping a shopping cart tightly when feeling dizzy in a store. In CBT for panic disorder, the therapist would likely:","options":{"a":"Prescribe these behaviors to prevent fainting.","b":"Use these behaviors as a measure of the client's insight.","c":"Work with the client to gradually drop these behaviors to test catastrophic beliefs.","d":"Explore the childhood origins of the need for safety."},"correct":"c","section":"clinical","explanation":"The answer is to work with the client to gradually drop these safety behaviors in order to test catastrophic beliefs, because in cognitive-behavioral therapy for panic disorder, safety behaviors are understood as a key factor maintaining the disorder, and eliminating them is essential to disconfirming the client's catastrophic misinterpretations. Understand the cognitive model of panic, developed by David M. Clark, which is the theoretical backbone here. Clark proposed that panic attacks arise from the catastrophic misinterpretation of benign bodily sensations. A person notices a normal or stress-induced sensation, such as a racing heart, dizziness, or breathlessness, and misinterprets it as a sign of imminent catastrophe, such as I am having a heart attack, I am going to faint, or I am losing my mind. This catastrophic thought increases anxiety, which intensifies the bodily sensations, creating a vicious cycle that spirals into a full panic attack. Safety behaviors are the subtle actions people take to prevent the feared catastrophe, such as gripping a shopping cart to avoid fainting, sitting down, or carrying water. The problem is that when the catastrophe does not occur, the person attributes the non-event to the safety behavior (I did not faint because I held on) rather than realizing the catastrophe was never going to happen. Thus safety behaviors prevent the disconfirmation of the catastrophic belief and keep the disorder alive. The therapeutic solution is a behavioral experiment: the therapist helps the client deliberately drop the safety behavior while experiencing the sensation, so they learn that the feared outcome does not occur even without the crutch. So the correct action is dropping safety behaviors to test catastrophic beliefs.","topic":"Safety behaviours in panic CBT"},{"q":"In view of most psychodynamic theorists, dissociative symptoms are produced by which of the following?","options":{"a":"Repression","b":"Ego state","c":"Conflict","d":"Gender identity issue"},"correct":"a","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is repression. In classical psychodynamic theory, first articulated by Sigmund Freud with Josef Breuer in their work on hysteria, dissociative symptoms such as amnesia, fugue, and depersonalization arise when intolerable memories, affects, or impulses are actively pushed out of conscious awareness. Repression is the primary defense mechanism: the ego banishes threatening material to the unconscious to protect itself from anxiety. Dissociation is understood as an extreme, splitting-off variant of this process, where whole segments of experience or identity become inaccessible. Pierre Janet, a contemporary of Freud, framed dissociation slightly differently as a failure of integration (desagregation), but the question asks specifically about most psychodynamic theorists, who root the phenomenon in repression. The concept matters clinically because it explains why dissociative amnesia typically covers traumatic content and can, in theory, be recovered when the repressive barrier is lifted. Conflict is the broader engine behind all defenses, but the specific mechanism producing dissociative symptoms is repression itself.","topic":"Psychodynamic view of dissociation"},{"q":"In statistical hypothesis testing, rejecting a true null hypothesis represents which type of error?","options":{"a":"Type I error","b":"Type II error","c":"Sampling error","d":"Standard error"},"correct":"a","section":"research","explanation":"The answer is a Type I error because that is defined precisely as rejecting a null hypothesis that is in fact true, which amounts to a false positive: concluding that an effect or difference exists when in reality it does not. This framework of two error types comes from the foundational work of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson on hypothesis testing. The null hypothesis states that there is no effect, no difference, or no relationship, and the researcher decides whether to reject it based on the data. Two mistakes are possible. A Type I error (also called an alpha error) occurs when the null is true but we wrongly reject it, for example concluding a drug works when it actually does nothing; its probability is the significance level, alpha, which researchers set in advance, commonly at 0.05, meaning a 5 percent risk of a false positive. A Type II error (a beta error) is the reverse: the null is actually false but we fail to reject it, a false negative, such as missing a drug's real effect; its probability is beta, and 1 minus beta is the test's statistical power. A memory aid: Type I is a false alarm (crying wolf when there is none), while Type II is a miss (failing to sound the alarm when the wolf is real). Since the question describes rejecting a true null, the answer is a Type I error.","topic":"Type I error"},{"q":"A network of associated facts and concepts that make up our knowledge of the world is referred to as:","options":{"a":"Episodic memory","b":"Explicit memory","c":"Implicit memory","d":"Semantic memory"},"correct":"d","section":"general","explanation":"A network of associated facts and concepts that constitute our general knowledge of the world is semantic memory, so d is correct. This question rests on the distinction, introduced by Endel Tulving, between two kinds of long-term declarative (explicit) memory. Semantic memory is the store of general facts, meanings, concepts, vocabulary, and knowledge about the world that is not tied to any particular time or place in which it was learned. Knowing that Paris is the capital of France, that a triangle has three sides, or what the word democracy means are all semantic memories; you typically cannot recall when or where you first acquired them. Episodic memory, by contrast, is memory for specific personally experienced events, bound to a particular context of time and place, such as remembering your last birthday party or what you ate this morning. Both semantic and episodic memory are subtypes of explicit (declarative) memory, which is consciously accessible and can be verbally described. This is contrasted with implicit (non-declarative) memory, which includes skills, habits, and conditioned responses that influence behaviour without conscious recall, such as riding a bicycle. The phrase network of associated facts and concepts strongly signals the organised, web-like structure of conceptual knowledge that defines semantic memory specifically, rather than the broader categories or the event-based alternative.","topic":"Semantic memory"},{"q":"Trail Making Test helps to assess ______ in children with ADHD.","options":{"a":"Inattention","b":"Social Skills","c":"Impulsivity","d":"Over-activity"},"correct":"a","section":"assessment","explanation":"The Trail Making Test helps assess inattention in children with ADHD, so the answer is a. The Trail Making Test has two parts. Part A requires the examinee to connect numbered circles in ascending order as quickly as possible, and Part B requires alternating between numbers and letters in sequence (1, A, 2, B, and so on). Part A primarily taps visual scanning, sustained attention, visuomotor speed, and the ability to maintain focus on a target sequence, while Part B adds set shifting and cognitive flexibility, an executive demand. In ADHD, the component the test is best suited to capture among the options given is inattention: completing the trails requires sustained, focused attention and continuous visual search, and children with the inattentive features of ADHD show slowed, error prone performance reflecting lapses of attention and difficulty maintaining the mental set. Although ADHD also involves impulsivity and hyperactivity, those are better captured by other measures such as continuous performance tests for impulsivity or behavioural observation for overactivity. The Trail Making Test specifically indexes the attentional and processing speed dimension, mapping onto the inattentive symptom cluster. The exam discriminator is matching the Trail Making Test's core demand, sustained attention and visual tracking, to inattention rather than to the motoric or impulsive symptoms of ADHD.","topic":"Trail Making Test attention"},{"q":"When people are able to think systematically about an issue and focus on arguments, they are likely to take the ______ route to persuasion.","options":{"a":"Peripheral","b":"Emotional","c":"Situational","d":"Central"},"correct":"d","section":"social","explanation":"When people think systematically about an issue and focus on arguments, they take the central route to persuasion, so the answer is d. This is the core of the Elaboration Likelihood Model developed by Richard Petty and John Cacioppo, which proposes two routes by which attitudes are changed. The central route operates when people are both motivated and able to think carefully about a message; they elaborate on it, scrutinise the strength and quality of the arguments, and are persuaded by the merits of the case. Attitude change produced this way is more enduring, more resistant to counterargument, and more predictive of behaviour, because it is grounded in genuine cognitive engagement. The peripheral route operates when motivation or ability to process is low; instead of analysing arguments, people rely on superficial cues such as the attractiveness or credibility of the source, the sheer number of arguments, catchy slogans, or emotional reactions. Persuasion via the peripheral route is weaker and more transient. The determining factors for which route is taken are the elaboration likelihood, chiefly the person's motivation (for example personal relevance) and cognitive capacity (for example freedom from distraction). The question specifies thinking systematically and focusing on arguments, which is the hallmark of the central route. The exam discriminator is matching effortful argument scrutiny to the central route as opposed to cue based peripheral processing.","topic":"ELM central route"},{"q":"According to Adler, middle children tend to be :","options":{"a":"Overachieving","b":"Competitive","c":"Resentful of the freedom of the older child","d":"Filled with feelings of inferiority"},"correct":"b","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is competitive. Alfred Adler, founder of Individual Psychology, argued that birth order shapes personality through the child's subjective experience of their family position. He described the middle (second-born) child as growing up in a race, always with an older sibling ahead to catch up to and often a younger sibling behind. This creates a characteristically competitive, ambitious striving: the middle child is spurred to overtake the pacesetter older sibling and frequently becomes achievement-oriented and even rebellious against the perceived unfairness of never being first. Adler saw this as generally healthy, and he considered the second-born often the best adjusted of the sibships. He contrasted this with the firstborn, who feels dethroned by the arrival of a sibling and may become conservative and power-oriented, the youngest or pampered child, who may remain dependent and prone to inferiority feelings, and the only child, who may struggle to share. Adler is also the originator of the inferiority complex concept. The specific hallmark of the middle child in his framework is competitiveness driven by the pace-setting sibling ahead.","topic":"Adler birth order middle child"},{"q":"The ‘satiety centre’ of the brain is located in the:","options":{"a":"Lateral hypothalamus","b":"Ventromedial hippocampus","c":"Lateral thalamus","d":"Ventromedial hypothalamus"},"correct":"d","section":"physio","explanation":"The correct answer is the ventromedial hypothalamus. The hypothalamus is the key brain region regulating hunger and eating, and two of its nuclei play opposing roles that form a classic dual-centre model (Eliot Stellar, 1954). The ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) functions as the satiety centre: when stimulated, it produces a feeling of fullness and stops eating, and crucially, when the VMH is damaged or lesioned, an animal overeats dramatically and becomes obese, a syndrome called hyperphagia, because the off switch for eating is destroyed. In contrast, the lateral hypothalamus (LH) functions as the hunger or feeding centre: stimulating it triggers eating, while lesioning it causes the animal to stop eating and lose weight (aphagia), because the on switch for hunger is destroyed. So the simple mnemonic is: ventromedial equals satiety (stop eating), lateral equals hunger (start eating). These findings come from classic lesion studies and underpin the understanding of appetite regulation, which is now known to involve hormones such as leptin (signalling satiety from fat stores) and ghrelin (signalling hunger from the stomach) acting on these hypothalamic centres. The question asks for the satiety centre, which is the ventromedial hypothalamus, so option d is correct.","topic":"Ventromedial hypothalamus satiety centre"},{"q":"Human motor development is governed by certain basic principles. Which of the following is Not a basic principle of motor development?","options":{"a":"Maturation of the motor system proceeds from gross motor to fine motor behaviours.","b":"Motor development is from cephalo-to-caudal or from head to feet.","c":"Motor development proceeds from the distal to the proximal or from the extremities to the central part of the body.","d":"Motor development plays a major role in social and behavioural expectations."},"correct":"c","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is that motor development proceeds from distal to proximal, because this statement is reversed and therefore NOT a true principle. The genuine principle is the proximodistal trend: development proceeds from proximal (near the centre or midline of the body, such as the trunk and shoulders) to distal (toward the extremities, such as the hands and fingers). Infants gain control of their arms and shoulders before they master fine finger movements, so option C states the direction backwards and is the false statement the question wants. The other two directional principles are stated correctly: the cephalocaudal trend means development proceeds from head to foot, so infants control their heads and upper bodies before their legs. The gross-to-fine principle means large-muscle movements (crawling, walking) develop before fine-motor skills (grasping small objects, writing). Option D, that motor development shapes social and behavioural expectations, is also a valid point about its wider significance. These maturational principles derive from work by developmental theorists including Arnold Gesell, who emphasised orderly, genetically guided maturation of motor skills. The item tests whether the student can spot the reversed proximodistal statement among otherwise accurate principles.","topic":"Principles of motor development"},{"q":"A child with autism is taught to request a \"break\" using a picture card instead of engaging in a tantrum. This intervention is a key component of:","options":{"a":"Cognitive-Behavioral Therapy (CBT)","b":"Applied Behavior Analysis (ABA)","c":"Play Therapy","d":"Psychoanalytic Psychotherapy"},"correct":"b","section":"clinical","explanation":"The answer is Applied Behavior Analysis, ABA, because teaching a child with autism to request a break using a picture card in place of a tantrum is a functional communication training intervention that is a core component of ABA. Understand ABA and the specific principle at work. Applied Behavior Analysis is the systematic application of the principles of operant conditioning, derived from B. F. Skinner's work, to socially significant behavior, and it is the most empirically supported intervention framework for autism spectrum disorder. A central ABA concept is that challenging behaviors like tantrums often serve a function, meaning they are maintained by their consequences; a functional behavior assessment identifies what the behavior achieves for the child, for example escape from a demand, access to attention, or access to a tangible item. In this vignette, the tantrum likely functions to obtain a break or escape from a nonpreferred activity. Functional communication training, a well-established ABA procedure, teaches the child a socially acceptable alternative behavior that serves the same function more efficiently, here handing over or pointing to a picture card that means break. When the appropriate communicative response reliably produces the desired outcome (the break) and the tantrum no longer does, the child shifts to the functional request, and the problem behavior extinguishes. This often uses picture-based systems such as the Picture Exchange Communication System (PECS). The intervention is grounded in reinforcement, replacement behavior, and functional analysis, all hallmarks of behavior analysis, so the correct framework is Applied Behavior Analysis, not the cognitive, play, or psychoanalytic approaches.","topic":"ABA functional communication training"},{"q":"Individuals with obsessive-compulsive personality disorder are primarily motivated by a need for which of the following?","options":{"a":"Efficiency","b":"Achievement","c":"Control","d":"Autonomy"},"correct":"c","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is control. Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (OCPD), classified in Cluster C of the DSM-5 by the American Psychiatric Association, is a pervasive pattern of preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism, and mental and interpersonal control, at the expense of flexibility, openness, and efficiency. The central motivating dynamic is a need for control: over one's own behavior, over other people, and over the environment. This manifests as rigid adherence to rules and schedules, excessive devotion to work, hoarding, reluctance to delegate unless others submit to one's exact methods, and moral inflexibility. Note that OCPD is distinct from Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD): OCPD is an ego-syntonic personality style without true obsessions and compulsions, whereas OCD, an anxiety-spectrum condition, involves ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts and ritualized behaviors. Psychodynamic writers tracing back to Sigmund Freud linked the anal-retentive character (orderliness, parsimony, obstinacy) to this control-oriented pattern. The perfectionism seen in OCPD is often self-defeating, producing inefficiency rather than the efficiency one might expect, which is exactly why control, not efficiency, is the underlying need.","topic":"OCPD need for control"},{"q":"A researcher is studying the same group of individuals over a 10-year period. This design is called:","options":{"a":"Cross-sectional design","b":"Case-control design","c":"Longitudinal design","d":"Experimental design"},"correct":"c","section":"research","explanation":"A longitudinal design is a research method in which the same individuals are observed and measured repeatedly over an extended period, in this case ten years, to study change and development over time. Its major strength is that, by following the same people, it can track individual growth trajectories and examine how variables change within persons, allowing stronger inferences about the sequence of developmental change; its drawbacks include high cost, long duration, and attrition (participant dropout). It contrasts most directly with the cross-sectional design, which compares different groups (often different ages) at a single point in time; cross-sectional studies are faster but cannot separate developmental change from cohort differences. A case-control design retrospectively compares individuals who have an outcome with those who do not, and an experimental design involves the deliberate manipulation of an independent variable with random assignment. Because studying the same group of individuals over a ten-year span is the defining feature of a longitudinal design, option c is correct.","topic":"Longitudinal design"},{"q":"Which of the following refers to the notion of the confirmatory bias?","options":{"a":"Clinicians ignore information that does not support their hypothesis","b":"Clinicians ignore information that does not support their stereotypes.","c":"Clinicians interpret ambiguous information as supporting their hypothesis.","d":"All of the above"},"correct":"d","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is all of the above. Confirmatory bias (confirmation bias) is the pervasive tendency to seek, interpret, favour, and recall information in a way that confirms one's pre-existing beliefs, hypotheses, or expectations, while giving disproportionately little attention to information that contradicts them. The concept was demonstrated experimentally by Peter Wason, whose selection task and 2-4-6 rule-discovery task showed that people tend to test hypotheses by looking for confirming rather than disconfirming evidence. In clinical assessment and diagnosis, confirmation bias is a serious source of error: a clinician who forms an early diagnostic hypothesis may then ignore or discount symptoms and information that do not fit that hypothesis (option A), may disregard evidence that contradicts a stereotype about a patient group (option B), and may interpret genuinely ambiguous or neutral information as though it supports the favoured hypothesis (option C). All three describe real manifestations of the same underlying bias, so the inclusive answer is correct. This bias contributes to premature closure and diagnostic overshadowing, which is why clinicians are trained to actively consider alternative hypotheses and seek disconfirming evidence, and why structured assessment reduces error. This item tests recognition that confirmation bias operates through several complementary mechanisms in clinical judgment.","topic":"Confirmation bias in clinical judgement"},{"q":"The process of recording a behaviour causes the behaviour to change, even before any treatment is implemented. This is known as:","options":{"a":"Reactivity","b":"Shaping","c":"Applied Behaviour Analysis","d":"Chaining"},"correct":"a","section":"assessment","explanation":"Reactivity is the phenomenon in which the act of observing or recording a behavior changes that behavior, even before any intervention is introduced. It is a central concept in behavioral assessment and self-monitoring. When a person knows they are being watched, or when they themselves begin keeping a record of a target behavior (counting cigarettes smoked, logging snacks eaten, tallying times they bite their nails), the behavior tends to shift, usually in the socially desirable direction. Reactivity is closely tied to the famous Hawthorne effect (Hawthorne studies by Elton Mayo and colleagues, 1924-1932; term by Henry Landsberger, 1958), where workers at the Hawthorne plant improved their productivity simply because they were being studied, regardless of the actual changes made to their conditions. In clinical practice this cuts two ways. On one hand reactivity is a threat to the validity of baseline data: the recorded behavior may not represent the person's true habitual rate. On the other hand clinicians deliberately exploit reactivity, because self-monitoring can itself be therapeutic, the simple act of tracking an undesired behavior often reduces it. The key idea the question tests is that the change here arises from measurement and awareness, not from any treatment, which is precisely the definition of reactivity. The other options are all behavioral techniques for building or changing behavior, not effects of observation.","topic":"Reactivity in behavioural assessment"},{"q":"When a person begins by making a small request that the other person is not likely to refuse is known as","options":{"a":"Deadline technique","b":"Foot-in-the-door-technique","c":"Door-in-the-face technique","d":"Conformity technique"},"correct":"b","section":"social","explanation":"This is a compliance technique from social psychology, studied experimentally by Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser. The foot-in-the-door technique works by first securing agreement to a trivial request that almost no one would refuse (signing a petition, wearing a small badge). Once a person has said yes, a larger request that follows is far more likely to be granted. The mechanism is best explained through Daryl Bem's self-perception theory: after agreeing to the small favour, people infer from their own behaviour that they are the kind of helpful, cooperative person who does such things, and to stay consistent with that new self-image they comply with the bigger ask. It also draws on Leon Festinger's pressure toward consistency. Notice the contrast with its opposite, the door-in-the-face (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1975) technique, where a large refused request is followed by a smaller one and works through reciprocal concession. The key phrase in the stem, 'begins by making a small request that the other person is not likely to refuse,' is the defining signature of foot-in-the-door. Marketers, fundraisers, and negotiators exploit this escalation from small to large commitments precisely because early agreement quietly reshapes how people see themselves, making later, costlier compliance feel natural rather than coerced.","topic":"Foot-in-the-door technique"},{"q":"Trait approaches to personality focuses on","options":{"a":"comprehending human personality by examining certain broad patterns.","b":"specific psychological attributes along which individuals tend to differ in consistent and stable way.","c":"representing and communicating a set of expected behaviour based on similarities.","d":"situational characteristics play an important role in determining our behaviour."},"correct":"b","section":"personality","explanation":"The correct answer is: specific psychological attributes along which individuals tend to differ in consistent and stable way.. The trait tradition (Allport, Cattell, Eysenck, the Big Five) defines a trait as a specific, enduring attribute along which people differ consistently (across situations) and stably (across time). Option (b) states exactly that. (d) is the situationist critique of trait theory, the opposite emphasis.","topic":"Trait versus type approach"},{"q":"The ______ lobe processes auditory information.","options":{"a":"Occipital","b":"Parietal","c":"Temporal","d":"Frontal"},"correct":"c","section":"physio","explanation":"The answer is the temporal lobe because it contains the primary auditory cortex, the cortical destination for sound. The cerebral cortex is divided into four lobes, and a reliable way to master them is to pair each with its primary sensory or motor function. The occipital lobe, at the back of the head, is dedicated to vision and houses the primary visual cortex. The parietal lobe, behind the central sulcus, processes somatosensory information (touch, pressure, temperature, pain) and spatial awareness, with the primary somatosensory cortex along its postcentral gyrus. The frontal lobe handles voluntary movement (primary motor cortex on the precentral gyrus), executive functions, planning, and (in the left hemisphere) Broca's area for speech production. The temporal lobe, sitting on the side of the brain roughly behind the temple and above the ear, is the home of the primary auditory cortex, located on the superior temporal gyrus (Heschl's gyrus), which receives and processes auditory information relayed up from the cochlea via the auditory pathway. The temporal lobe also contains Wernicke's area for language comprehension and parts of the hippocampus for memory, both of which fit logically with hearing and understanding spoken language. So auditory processing maps to the temporal lobe. A useful mnemonic: temporal is near the temple and the ear, so it handles what you hear. Lock the four-way map (occipital-vision, parietal-touch, frontal-movement, temporal-hearing) and these lobe questions become automatic.","topic":"Temporal lobe auditory processing"},{"q":"When infants and very young children live in environments where they have no exposure to or experience with language, their language development is severely limited. These limitations are not reversible. This supports which of the following terms?","options":{"a":"Sensitive periods of development","b":"Semantics","c":"Syntax","d":"Nativism"},"correct":"a","section":"dev","explanation":"The correct answer is sensitive periods of development. A sensitive period (or critical period) is a specific window of time early in development during which the brain is especially receptive to particular environmental input, and during which certain abilities, such as language, are most readily acquired. If the necessary experience is absent during this window, the ability may fail to develop normally and, crucially, the deficit can be permanent and not fully reversible even with later exposure. Eric Lenneberg formulated the critical period hypothesis for language, proposing that language must be acquired before puberty for native-like mastery to be possible. Tragic natural cases such as Genie, a child deprived of language until age 13, support this: despite later intervention she never acquired normal grammar, illustrating the irreversibility the question emphasises. The concept parallels Konrad Lorenz's work on imprinting in birds, another time-bounded sensitive period. Nativism (option D), Noam Chomsky's view that humans possess an innate language acquisition device, is related but does not by itself explain the irreversible harm from deprivation within a bounded time frame. Semantics and syntax are simply components of language. This item tests the concept of a bounded, largely irreversible developmental window.","topic":"Sensitive period for language"},{"q":"Habit reversal training (HRT) is primarily used as a behavioral intervention to reduce which type of repetitive behavior?","options":{"a":"Tics","b":"Stuttering","c":"Obsessive-compulsive behaviors","d":"Aggressive behaviors"},"correct":"a","section":"clinical","explanation":"The answer is tics because Habit Reversal Training is the behavioural treatment of choice for tic disorders (including Tourette's syndrome) as well as for other body-focused repetitive behaviours such as trichotillomania (hair-pulling) and nail-biting. HRT was developed by Nathan Azrin and Gregory Nunn in the 1970s and consists of several linked components that work together. The first is awareness training, in which the person learns to notice the tic or habit and the premonitory urge or early-warning sensations that precede it, since these behaviours are often carried out semi-automatically without full awareness. The second is developing a competing response, a physically incompatible action that the person performs deliberately whenever they feel the urge (for example, tensing an opposing muscle group so the tic cannot be executed). The third involves social support and motivation strategies to reinforce the new response. By repeatedly substituting the competing response for the tic whenever the urge arises, the tic weakens over time. This is a first-line non-pharmacological treatment and, in expanded form, is part of the package known as CBIT (Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics). The distractors each have their own signature treatment, and matching disorder to treatment is the exam skill: OCD to exposure and response prevention, phobias to systematic desensitization (Joseph Wolpe, 1958), and tics to habit reversal training. So the correct answer is tics.","topic":"Habit reversal training for tics"},{"q":"When an individual has dissociative amnesia it is usually associated with several types of memory disturbances. In contrast, in generalized amnesia :","options":{"a":"There is a failure of recall of the entire past week","b":"There is a failure of recall that encompasses the person's entire life","c":"There is a failure of recall that encompasses the person's childhood","d":"There is a failure of recall that for traumatic events"},"correct":"b","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is that there is a failure of recall that encompasses the person's entire life. Dissociative amnesia, described in the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association, is an inability to recall important autobiographical information, usually of a traumatic or stressful nature, that is too extensive to be ordinary forgetting and is not due to a substance or neurological condition. It takes several forms distinguished by the scope of memory loss. Localized amnesia is failure to recall events during a specific circumscribed period (for example, the hours around a trauma); selective amnesia is failure to recall some but not all events of a period; and systematized amnesia targets a specific category of information. Generalized amnesia is the rarest and most severe form: it involves a complete loss of memory for one's entire life history, including personal identity, autobiographical events, and sometimes previously learned skills and knowledge. Because it encompasses the person's whole life rather than a limited window, option b is correct. The other options describe the more circumscribed subtypes (localized for a week, selective or partial for childhood or for traumatic events only), which is precisely the contrast the question draws with the total sweep of generalized amnesia.","topic":"Generalized dissociative amnesia"},{"q":"When two variables that are being used as predictors in a statistical analysis have a high correlation with each other, this statistical problem is known as:","options":{"a":"Heteroscedasticity","b":"Multicollinearity","c":"Autocorrelation","d":"Homogeneity of variance"},"correct":"b","section":"research","explanation":"The answer is multicollinearity because that is the precise term for the situation in regression where two or more predictor (independent) variables are highly correlated with one another. The prefix 'multi' plus 'collinearity' literally signals several predictors lying along the same line, moving together. Multicollinearity is a problem because multiple regression tries to estimate the unique, independent contribution of each predictor to the outcome, but when predictors overlap heavily, the model cannot cleanly separate their effects. The practical consequences are that the estimated regression coefficients become unstable and can swing wildly with small changes in the data, their standard errors inflate, and it becomes hard to tell which predictor is really doing the work, even though the model's overall predictive accuracy may stay fine. It is commonly diagnosed with the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) or by examining a correlation matrix among predictors, and it is often remedied by dropping or combining redundant variables. The distractors name genuinely different regression concerns: heteroscedasticity is about the residuals having non-constant variance, autocorrelation is about residuals being correlated over time or sequence, and homogeneity of variance is actually a desirable assumption rather than a fault. Because the question describes two predictors being highly correlated with each other, the answer is multicollinearity.","topic":"Multicollinearity"},{"q":"Alcohol-induced amnesia may be an example of :","options":{"a":"Dependent memory","b":"State-dependent memory","c":"Independent memory","d":"State-independent memory"},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is state-dependent memory. State-dependent memory refers to the finding that retrieval is enhanced when a person's internal physiological or psychological state at recall matches their state during original encoding. It is a form of encoding specificity, the broader principle articulated by Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson that memory is best when retrieval cues reinstate the conditions of learning. With alcohol, information encoded while intoxicated is often recalled more easily when the person is again intoxicated than when sober, and vice versa; this is why a person may forget, while sober, where they placed something while drunk, then recall it upon drinking again. Early demonstrations of drug and alcohol state-dependent learning were reported by researchers including Donald Overton. This differs from context-dependent memory, where the match is in external environment (studied by Duncan Godden and Alan Baddeley in their diver experiments), and from mood-dependent memory, where the match is in emotional state, studied by Gordon Bower. Note this is distinct from an alcohol blackout, in which severe intoxication blocks the formation of new memories entirely. The item specifically describes retrieval governed by internal state, which is the definition of state-dependent memory.","topic":"State-dependent memory"},{"q":"A clinician is interested in understanding a client's characteristic way of thinking, feeling, and behaving in close relationships, by having them tell stories about ambiguous pictures. The MOST appropriate test to administer would be the:","options":{"a":"Rorschach Inkblot Test","b":"Thematic Apperception Test (TAT)","c":"Sentence Completion Test","d":"Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI)"},"correct":"b","section":"assessment","explanation":"The answer is the Thematic Apperception Test, the TAT, because the technique of having a client tell stories about ambiguous pictures in order to reveal their characteristic ways of thinking, feeling, and relating is precisely what the TAT was designed to do. Understand the logic of projective testing and the specific design of the TAT. Projective techniques rest on the projective hypothesis: when a person is confronted with an ambiguous stimulus that has no clear meaning of its own, they impose structure on it by projecting their own inner needs, conflicts, motives, and relationship patterns onto it, so their response reveals otherwise hidden aspects of personality. The TAT, developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan at Harvard in the 1930s, uses a set of cards depicting ambiguous interpersonal scenes, often people in emotionally suggestive but unclear situations. The examinee is asked to make up a story for each card: what is happening, what led up to it, what the characters are thinking and feeling, and how it turns out. Because the pictures involve people in relationships, the stories tend to expose the storyteller's dominant needs, press (environmental forces), and characteristic interpersonal themes, which Murray analyzed through his theory of needs and press. Clinicians examine recurring themes, the hero the client identifies with, and how conflicts are resolved. The stem's exact scenario, understanding a client's way of relating in close relationships by having them tell stories about ambiguous pictures, is the definition of the TAT, distinguishing it from the inkblot-based Rorschach and the objective MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943).","topic":"TAT projective storytelling"},{"q":"According to the \"Dodo Bird Verdict,\" what is the primary factor responsible for equivalent outcomes across different therapeutic modalities?","options":{"a":"The specific techniques used by the therapist","b":"Common factors such as the therapeutic alliance and empathy","c":"The theoretical orientation of the clinician","d":"The specific diagnosis of the patient"},"correct":"b","section":"clinical","explanation":"The answer is common factors such as the therapeutic alliance and empathy because the Dodo Bird Verdict is the proposition that different, seemingly quite distinct forms of psychotherapy produce roughly equivalent outcomes, and that this equivalence is best explained not by the unique techniques of any one approach but by the factors they all share. The colourful name comes from Lewis Carroll's Alice in Wonderland, in which the Dodo bird, after a chaotic race, declares that 'everybody has won, and all must have prizes'. The idea was introduced to psychotherapy research by Saul Rosenzweig in 1936 and later supported empirically by meta-analyses, notably by Lester Luborsky and colleagues, which found comparatively small differences in effectiveness between bona fide therapies. If technique is not the main driver, what is? The common-factors view, associated with researchers such as Jerome Frank and later Bruce Wampold, points to elements present across virtually all therapies: a strong therapeutic alliance (the collaborative, trusting bond between client and therapist), therapist empathy, warmth, and positive regard, the instillation of hope and positive expectations, and having a coherent rationale and structure for change. On this account, the relationship itself does much of the healing. The exam trap is choosing 'specific techniques', which is exactly what the verdict de-emphasises. The correct answer is the shared common factors, above all the therapeutic alliance and empathy.","topic":"Dodo bird verdict common factors"},{"q":"Hypnagogic hallucinations most commonly occur in:","options":{"a":"Schizophrenia","b":"Delirium","c":"Drug dependence","d":"Normal"},"correct":"d","section":"disorders","explanation":"The correct answer is normal. Hypnagogic hallucinations are vivid perceptual experiences (most often visual or auditory) that occur during the transition from wakefulness into sleep, that is, while falling asleep. Their counterpart, hypnopompic hallucinations, occur on waking up, during the transition from sleep to wakefulness. The crucial teaching point is that these hallucinations occurring at the threshold of sleep are normal phenomena, experienced by a large proportion of healthy people, and are not by themselves indicative of any psychiatric or neurological disorder. They arise because the brain is shifting between sleep and waking states, and fragments of dream-like imagery intrude into a partially conscious mind. Common examples include hearing one's name called, hearing a doorbell or knock, brief flashes of imagery, or the sensation of falling (the hypnic jerk). Because they happen at sleep onset in ordinary people, the correct association is with the normal state rather than with illness. Clinically, very frequent or intense hypnagogic hallucinations, especially combined with sleep paralysis, excessive daytime sleepiness, and cataplexy, form part of the tetrad of narcolepsy, but in isolation occasional hypnagogic hallucinations are a benign, normal experience.","topic":"Hypnagogic hallucinations in normals"},{"q":"It has been found that the Muller-Lyer illusion exists in certain cultures in which there are :","options":{"a":"Lot of trees","b":"Buildings with corners","c":"Structures without many corners and angles","d":"More men than women"},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is buildings with corners. The Muller-Lyer illusion, devised by Franz Carl Muller-Lyer, is the classic figure in which two lines of equal length appear unequal because one has outward-pointing arrowheads (fins) and the other inward-pointing ones. A leading explanation is the carpentered world hypothesis, advanced by Marshall Segall, Donald Campbell, and Melville Herskovits in their cross-cultural research. They argued that people raised in carpentered environments, cities and towns full of rectangular buildings, straight edges, and right-angled corners, learn to interpret the arrow configurations as depth cues (like the near and far corners of a room or building), which biases their length judgments and makes them highly susceptible to the illusion. In contrast, people from non-carpentered environments with few straight lines and corners, such as certain rural or open-landscape cultures, showed markedly weaker susceptibility. This finding challenged the assumption that perception is purely innate and universal, demonstrating that ecological and cultural experience shapes even basic visual processing. Therefore the illusion is stronger in cultures whose environments feature buildings with corners and angles, making option b correct.","topic":"Muller-Lyer carpentered world hypothesis"},{"q":"IQ if any body has a mental age of 8 and a chronological age of 10 is:","options":{"a":"108","b":"125","c":"80","d":"92"},"correct":"c","section":"assessment","explanation":"The correct answer is 80. The intelligence quotient, in its original ratio form introduced by William Stern and popularised through the Stanford-Binet test revised by Lewis Terman, is calculated with the formula IQ equals mental age divided by chronological age, multiplied by 100. Here the mental age is 8 and the chronological age is 10, so IQ equals (8 divided by 10) times 100, which equals 0.8 times 100, equals 80. The logic is that a child whose mental age matches their chronological age has an IQ of 100 (average), a child whose mental development is ahead of their years scores above 100, and a child whose mental age lags behind their chronological age, as here, scores below 100. Option b (125) results from reversing the ratio, dividing chronological age by mental age, which is the wrong direction; option d (92) is near average but does not match the calculation; and option a (108) does not follow from the formula. Mental age, the concept underlying this, originated with Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon, who devised the first practical intelligence test. Although modern tests use deviation IQ (David Wechsler) rather than this ratio formula, understanding the classic ratio IQ remains a staple of assessment questions and illustrates the historical foundation of intelligence testing.","topic":"Ratio IQ calculation"},{"q":"If Ramesh works in a call centre, and has to work very hard, do a lot of work, and that too at a fast rate, he experiences:","options":{"a":"Conflict","b":"Frustration","c":"Unpredictability","d":"Pressure"},"correct":"d","section":"clinical","explanation":"The correct answer is pressure. In the psychology of stress, several distinct sources of stress are commonly distinguished, and pressure is the demand to perform, produce, or behave in a certain way, often at a high level, at speed, or to meet expectations and deadlines. Ramesh's situation, having to work very hard, complete a large volume of work, and do so at a fast rate, is a textbook example of pressure: he faces intense demands on his performance and pace. The distractors describe other sources of stress: conflict (option a) arises when a person must choose between incompatible goals or motives, such as an approach-avoidance conflict studied by Kurt Lewin, and is about competing demands rather than workload; frustration (option b) is the stress that results when progress toward a goal is blocked or thwarted, such as being unable to complete a task because of obstacles; and unpredictability (option c) is the stress that comes from not knowing what will happen or being unable to anticipate events, which increases stress because control and predictability tend to reduce it. Understanding these categories matters because identifying the specific source of a person's stress guides how to address it: pressure calls for time management, workload adjustment, and coping skills, whereas conflict, frustration, and unpredictability each require different strategies, a distinction central to stress and health psychology.","topic":"Sources of stress pressure"},{"q":"Which is NOT typically considered a learning difficulty?","options":{"a":"Dysgraphia","b":"ADHD","c":"Dyslexia","d":"Auditory processing disorder"},"correct":"b","section":"disorders","explanation":"Learning difficulties, or specific learning disorders, are neurodevelopmental conditions that impair specific academic skills despite adequate general intelligence and instruction. Dyslexia affects reading and decoding, dysgraphia affects written expression and handwriting, dyscalculia affects mathematics, and auditory processing disorder impairs the brain's processing of the sounds it hears, all of which directly interfere with acquiring particular academic skills and are classed among learning difficulties. ADHD (attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder), by contrast, is a neurodevelopmental disorder of attention, impulse control, and hyperactivity. Although ADHD frequently co-occurs with learning disorders and can secondarily hinder academic performance, it is not itself a specific learning disorder: its core deficit is in attention and self-regulation, not in a specific academic skill such as reading, writing, or arithmetic. This distinction is important for accurate diagnosis and intervention. Because dysgraphia, dyslexia, and auditory processing disorder are learning difficulties while ADHD is an attention disorder, option b is the correct exception.","topic":"Specific learning disorders versus ADHD"},{"q":"Which approach to perception emphasizes the role of unconscious inference and probabilistic reasoning in interpreting sensory information?","options":{"a":"Computational approach","b":"Constructivist approach","c":"Direct perception approach","d":"Information processing approach"},"correct":"b","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is Constructivist approach. The constructivist approach to perception holds that perception is an active process in which the brain constructs our experience of the world by combining incoming sensory data with prior knowledge, expectations, and probabilistic inference. Its intellectual root is Hermann von Helmholtz's idea of unconscious inference: because the retinal image is ambiguous and impoverished, the perceptual system unconsciously infers the most likely interpretation of the scene, effectively making a best guess. Richard Gregory later championed this top-down, hypothesis-testing view of perception. Because the question emphasises unconscious inference and probabilistic reasoning, the constructivist approach is the correct match, making option B right. The direct perception approach (option C), developed by James J. Gibson, is the direct opposite: it argues that the environment provides rich, sufficient information (invariants and affordances) so that perception is direct and requires no inference or internal construction. The computational approach (option D pairing aside), associated with David Marr, treats vision as a series of information-processing computations from image to 3-D model, focusing on the algorithms rather than on unconscious inference per se. The information processing approach describes perception and cognition as flowing through stages, a general framework rather than the specific inference-based constructivist claim. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)","topic":"Constructivist perception unconscious inference"},{"q":"What term describes the phenomenon where individuals fail to notice significant changes in their enviornment, even when those changes are obvious?","options":{"a":"Change blindness","b":"Inattentional blindness","c":"Perceptual constancy","d":"Selective attention"},"correct":"a","section":"general","explanation":"The correct answer is Change blindness. Change blindness is the surprising failure to detect a substantial change in a visual scene, especially when the change coincides with a brief disruption such as a flicker, a blink, an eye movement, or a cut in a film. Because our visual system does not store a complete, detailed representation of every part of a scene, we often miss even large, obvious alterations when attention is not directed to the changing element. This is precisely the phenomenon the question describes, making option A correct. It was demonstrated in striking experiments by Daniel Simons and colleagues, including studies in which an experimenter asking for directions was swapped for a different person mid-conversation without the participant noticing. Inattentional blindness (option B), also studied by Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris in the famous invisible gorilla experiment, is the related but distinct failure to notice an unexpected object that is in plain view because attention is engaged elsewhere; it concerns missing something present, not missing a change between two views. Perceptual constancy (option C) is the ability to perceive an object as stable in size, shape, or colour despite changing sensory input, an unrelated concept. Selective attention (option D) is the general process of focusing on certain stimuli while filtering out others. Change blindness specifically names the failure to spot changes. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)","topic":"Change blindness"}]};
window.PREVIEW_DEEP = Object.assign(window.PREVIEW_DEEP||{}, {"sjmc-mock-1": [{"q":"A therapist uses \"time-out\" to manage a child's aggressive outbursts. Time-out is technically classified as a form of:","concept":"The answer is negative punishment, because time-out works by removing a positive, reinforcing stimulus in order to decrease an unwanted behavior, and that combination, removal (negative) plus decrease (punishment), defines negative punishment. Understand the two-by-two framework of operant consequences, formalized within B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning, because this is one of the most heavily tested distinctions in psychology. Two dimensions combine. The first dimension is the effect on behavior: reinforcement always increases the future probability of a behavior, whereas punishment always decreases it. The second dimension is the operation: positive means a stimulus is added or presented, whereas negative means a stimulus is removed or taken away. Note that positive and negative here mean add and subtract, not good and bad. Crossing these gives four procedures. Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant stimulus to increase behavior (giving a treat for good work). Negative reinforcement removes an aversive stimulus to increase behavior (taking an aspirin to end a headache). Positive punishment adds an aversive stimulus to decrease behavior (a spanking). Negative punishment removes a pleasant stimulus to decrease behavior. Time-out from positive reinforcement is the classic example of negative punishment: when a child has an aggressive outburst, they are removed from the reinforcing environment (attention, play, peers) for a short period, and losing access to that positive stimulation reduces the future likelihood of the aggression. The key is that something desirable is subtracted (negative) to weaken behavior (punishment), so time-out is negative punishment.","traps":{"a":"Positive punishment adds an aversive stimulus to decrease behavior, such as a scolding; time-out does not add anything, it removes access to reinforcement, so it is not positive punishment.","b":"Negative reinforcement removes an aversive stimulus to increase a behavior, whereas time-out aims to decrease the aggressive behavior, so it cannot be a reinforcement procedure of any kind.","d":"Positive reinforcement adds a pleasant stimulus to increase behavior, the opposite of time-out, which withdraws pleasant stimulation to reduce behavior."}},{"q":"The primary difference between a \"speed test\" and a \"power test\" in psychological assessment is that a speed test:","concept":"The answer is that a speed test has a strict time limit that prevents most test-takers from completing all items, while a power test has items so difficult that not everyone can answer them all correctly, because this correctly captures the fundamental distinction between the two test designs. Understand the concepts, which come from psychometric test construction. The difference between a speed test and a power test lies in what limits a test-taker's score, time pressure or item difficulty. A pure speed test consists of many items that are all relatively easy, so easy that virtually anyone could get them right given unlimited time, but it imposes a strict time limit so short that few or no test-takers can finish all the items. Because the items are easy, individual differences in scores reflect how quickly and accurately a person can work, not whether they can solve hard problems; the score measures speed and efficiency of performance. Clerical aptitude tests and symbol-cancellation tasks are typical examples. A pure power test, by contrast, gives generous or unlimited time so that time is not the constraining factor, but it contains items of graded and increasing difficulty, including items so hard that not everyone can solve them; here scores reflect the difficulty ceiling a person can reach, measuring the level or depth of ability rather than speed. Most real tests are a mix of both, imposing some time limit while also varying difficulty. The key conceptual pairing to remember is speed test equals easy items plus tight time limit, and power test equals hard items plus ample time, which is exactly what the correct option states.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the definitions: it is the power test that classically uses items of increasing difficulty, and the claim that a power test has uniform-difficulty items is incorrect, so this option scrambles the two concepts.","c":"Measuring reaction time versus cognitive ability is not the defining distinction; both speed and power tests can measure cognitive ability, and the real difference lies in time limits and item difficulty, not reaction time.","d":"Individual versus group administration is a mode-of-administration issue unrelated to the speed-power distinction; both speed and power tests can be given individually or in groups."}},{"q":"Karina and Rakhi are involved in an altercation that is captured on tape. In the video, Karina is seen walking with her young son when Rakhi attacks her. Karina falls to the ground and Rakhi assaults her, ultimately punching and kicking her dozens of times. A group of individuals gathers around the two women, takes out their cell phones, and records the fight. They do not help or call for help. The behaviour of the group is an example of which of the following terms?","concept":"The correct answer is the bystander effect. The bystander effect is the well-documented social phenomenon in which the presence of other people reduces the likelihood that any given individual will help a person in an emergency; the more bystanders present, the less likely any one of them is to intervene. It was investigated by John Darley and Bibb Latane, prompted by the 1964 case of Kitty Genovese, whose murder was reportedly witnessed by many neighbours who did not act. Their research identified two key mechanisms. The first is diffusion of responsibility: when many people are present, each individual feels less personal responsibility to act, assuming someone else will help. The second is pluralistic ignorance: people look to others to define whether the situation is truly an emergency, and if no one else appears alarmed or is helping, each concludes it must not require intervention, so collectively no one acts. In the scenario, a crowd gathers and merely films the assault without helping or calling for help, a textbook illustration of the bystander effect driven by diffusion of responsibility. The distractors describe other group phenomena (polarisation, facilitation, and loafing) that do not concern failure to aid a victim. This item tests recognition of the bystander effect and its mechanisms.","traps":{"a":"Group polarisation (Serge Moscovici and Marisa Zavalloni, 1969) is the tendency for group discussion to strengthen members' initial attitudes toward a more extreme position, not the failure to help in an emergency.","b":"Social facilitation (Norman Triplett, 1898) concerns how an audience affects task performance, not whether onlookers intervene to help a victim.","d":"Social loafing is reduced individual effort on a shared group task, not the failure of bystanders to assist someone in distress."}},{"q":"Roshan often experiences intense feelings of anger and frustration. In order to cope with these feelings, he enrols in a kickboxing class as an outlet for his emotions. Roshan's actions are an example of which type of defence mechanism?","concept":"The correct answer is sublimation. Sigmund Freud, the founder of psychoanalysis, described defence mechanisms as unconscious strategies the ego uses to manage anxiety arising from unacceptable impulses. His daughter Anna Freud later systematised them. Sublimation is considered the most mature and adaptive defence: it channels socially unacceptable drives (here, aggression) into constructive, socially valued activities such as sport, art, or work. Roshan takes raw anger and directs it into kickboxing, a productive outlet, which is the textbook signature of sublimation. Displacement (option c) is close but differs in quality: displacement shifts an impulse onto a substitute target (yelling at a family member instead of the boss), and it is not inherently constructive, whereas sublimation transforms the impulse into something valued. Because the exam rewards recognising the adaptive, channelling nature of the act, sublimation is preferred. Understanding this distinction matters clinically because sublimation predicts healthier coping and better long-term adjustment, while primitive defences like denial or projection predict poorer functioning. This concept recurs throughout personality assessment and psychodynamic therapy.","traps":{"b":"Projection is attributing one's own unacceptable impulses to others, which Roshan is not doing.","c":"Displacement redirects an impulse onto a safer target, but kickboxing is a socially valued outlet, making sublimation the better fit.","d":"Repression is unconsciously blocking feelings from awareness, whereas Roshan is consciously channelling them into activity."}},{"q":"Which of the following brain structures is primarily responsible for regulating emotions such as fear and aggression?","concept":"The amygdala, an almond-shaped structure within the temporal lobes and part of the limbic system described by Paul MacLean, is the brain structure most directly responsible for processing and regulating emotions, particularly fear and aggression. It rapidly evaluates incoming stimuli for threat and triggers appropriate emotional and physiological responses, including the fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon) reaction. Research by Joseph LeDoux traced the neural circuitry of fear, showing a fast, direct thalamo-amygdala pathway that allows the amygdala to respond to danger before the conscious cortex fully processes it. Classic lesion studies, including the Kluver-Bucy syndrome produced by removing the temporal lobes in monkeys, demonstrated that amygdala damage abolishes normal fear and can blunt aggression, confirming its central role. The distractors serve other functions: the hippocampus, also in the limbic system, is essential for forming new declarative memories; the thalamus is the brain's sensory relay hub; and the cerebellum coordinates movement, posture, and balance. Because the amygdala is primarily responsible for regulating fear and aggression, option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"The hippocampus is central to forming new long-term memories, not primarily to regulating fear and aggression.","b":"The thalamus is a sensory relay station directing signals to the cortex, not the primary emotion regulator.","d":"The cerebellum coordinates movement and balance, not emotional regulation."}},{"q":"What is the primary difference between Piaget and Vygotsky's theories of language development?","concept":"The correct answer is that Lev Vygotsky argued thought depends on language while Jean Piaget argued language depends on thought. This captures the core theoretical divide between the two developmental giants. Piaget viewed cognitive development as primary and language as a product of it: for Piaget, the child must first develop underlying cognitive structures and understanding (for example, object permanence and symbolic thought) before language can express those concepts, so language reflects thought rather than driving it. He also regarded much early speech as egocentric, fading as the child matures. Vygotsky, by contrast, in his sociocultural theory, argued that language and thought have separate roots but merge around age two, after which language becomes the primary tool that shapes and drives cognitive development. For Vygotsky, social speech is internalised into private speech and then inner speech, which guides reasoning and self-regulation, so thought is fundamentally dependent on language and social interaction. This reflects his broader emphasis on the zone of proximal development and social scaffolding. The reversed option and the timing-based option misstate the relationship. This item tests the classic Piaget-versus-Vygotsky contrast on the direction of the language-thought dependency.","traps":{"a":"This reverses the two theorists: it wrongly assigns the language-drives-thought view to Piaget and the thought-drives-language view to Vygotsky.","c":"Both theorists studied language across development; the distinction is not about early versus late timing but about the direction of the thought-language relationship.","d":"One option (B) correctly captures the contrast, so 'none of the above' is incorrect."}},{"q":"In psychodynamic therapy, analysis of resistance is where:","concept":"The correct answer is that the client obstructs the analysis and the therapist explores the reasons for this. Resistance is a key concept in the psychoanalytic and psychodynamic therapy developed by Sigmund Freud. It refers to any behaviour by the client that blocks, opposes, or interferes with the progress of therapy and the uncovering of unconscious material, for example arriving late, changing the subject, falling silent, forgetting appointments, going blank during free association, or refusing to discuss certain topics. Freud regarded resistance not as mere obstruction to be overcome but as meaningful and revealing: it signals that the therapy is approaching anxiety-provoking, threatening, or repressed material that the client's defences are protecting against. Therefore the analysis of resistance involves the therapist noticing these obstructions and helping the client explore and understand why they are resisting, thereby bringing defended-against unconscious conflicts into awareness. Along with free association, dream analysis, and the analysis and interpretation of transference, the analysis of resistance is one of the central techniques Freud used to make the unconscious conscious. The distractors correctly describe these other techniques: free association (option A), transference (option B), and dream analysis (option D), which is why they are wrong for resistance specifically. This item tests understanding of resistance and its analysis in psychodynamic therapy.","traps":{"a":"Talking about anything that comes to mind is free association, a different psychoanalytic technique, not analysis of resistance.","b":"Transferring feelings from significant others onto the therapist is transference, not resistance.","d":"Interpreting the client's dreams is dream analysis, another distinct psychoanalytic technique, not analysis of resistance."}},{"q":"According to the ICD-11 (International Classification of Diseases), what is the minimum duration requirement for symptoms to be present before a diagnosis of schizophrenia can be made?","concept":"The answer is one month because the ICD-11, the World Health Organization's International Classification of Diseases, requires that the characteristic symptoms of schizophrenia be present for at least one month before the diagnosis can be made. Those characteristic symptoms include the positive symptoms such as delusions, hallucinations, disorganised thinking and speech, and experiences of passivity or control, and at least one core symptom must persist for most of the time across that month-long period. This one-month rule is worth contrasting carefully with the DSM-5, the American diagnostic system, because that difference is a favourite exam trap: the DSM-5 requires continuous signs of disturbance for at least six months, of which at least one month must consist of active-phase symptoms, with the remaining time made up of prodromal or residual features. So the same disorder carries a one-month threshold in ICD-11 but a six-month threshold in DSM-5. The two-week option is a decoy borrowed from the criteria for a major depressive episode, which requires symptoms nearly every day for two weeks. When an exam specifies ICD-11 and asks about schizophrenia duration, the answer is one month; if it specified DSM-5, the answer would be six months.","traps":{"a":"Two weeks is the minimum duration linked to a major depressive episode, not to schizophrenia.","c":"Three months is not a recognised diagnostic threshold for schizophrenia in the ICD system.","d":"Six months is the DSM-5 total-duration criterion (including prodromal and residual phases), not the ICD-11 requirement."}},{"q":"Maximization of ____ variance, minimization of ____ variance, control of ____ variance. Select the appropriate option.","concept":"This item tests the MAXMINCON principle articulated by Fred Kerlinger, whose research-methods textbook made it a cornerstone of design logic. Kerlinger argued that every experiment aims to partition the total variance in the dependent variable into three streams and to handle each differently. First, MAXimize the systematic variance produced by your independent variable, often called primary or experimental variance; you want your treatment to push the groups as far apart as possible so the effect is detectable. Second, MINimize error variance, the random noise from measurement unreliability, individual fluctuations, and uncontrolled chance factors; you shrink it through reliable instruments, standardized procedures, and adequate sample size. Third, CONtrol extraneous or secondary variance, the systematic influence of nuisance variables that are not your focus (age, gender, order effects); you neutralize these by randomization, matching, holding constant, or building them in as factors. The correct ordering in the stem is therefore maximize primary (systematic) variance, minimize error variance, control secondary (extraneous) variance, matching option C. The distractors scramble these assignments, and the tell is that anything asking you to minimize 'true' or 'primary' variance violates the whole point, since that is the signal you are hunting for. Mastering this triad lets you diagnose why a study failed: weak manipulation, sloppy measurement, or confounds.","traps":{"a":"This inverts the principle by minimizing 'true' variance, yet true systematic variance is exactly what a good design tries to maximize.","b":"Placing 'primary' in the minimize slot is wrong; you never minimize the systematic variance you are studying.","d":"Minimizing 'true' variance and controlling 'error' contradicts the rule; error is minimized, not merely controlled, and true variance is maximized."}},{"q":"We know that a common distinction is made between explicit and implicit memory. But which one of the following does NOT accurately reflect one of these terms?","concept":"The correct answer is that implicit memory involves a conscious recollection of the original events, which does NOT accurately reflect the term. The distinction between explicit and implicit memory, elaborated by researchers such as Daniel Schacter and Endel Tulving, divides long-term memory by whether retrieval is conscious. Explicit (declarative) memory involves the conscious, intentional recollection of facts and events, such as recalling what you did yesterday or a fact you learned; statements a and c correctly describe it as conscious awareness and deliberate recollection of the original information. Implicit (non-declarative) memory, by contrast, operates without conscious awareness: prior experiences influence current behaviour, feelings, skills, or thoughts even though the person cannot consciously recall the original learning. Examples include procedural skills like riding a bicycle, classically conditioned responses, and priming; statement b correctly describes implicit memory as an influence from prior experience. Option d is false because it attributes conscious recollection to implicit memory, which is precisely the opposite of what implicit means; the defining feature of implicit memory is that it is unconscious. This distinction matters because it is supported by neuropsychological evidence, notably amnesic patients like H.M. (studied by Brenda Milner) who lost the ability to form new explicit memories but could still learn new skills implicitly, showing the two systems rely on different brain structures.","traps":{"a":"Explicit memory does involve conscious awareness of the learned information, so this statement is accurate and not the answer.","b":"Implicit memory does influence behaviour and thoughts through prior experience without conscious recall, so this is accurate.","c":"Explicit memory does involve deliberate recollection of the original information, so this statement is accurate."}},{"q":"A researcher is developing a new scale for resilience. To establish discriminant validity, they should demonstrate that their new scale:","concept":"The answer is that the new scale should correlate weakly with a theoretically unrelated construct such as neuroticism, because discriminant validity is demonstrated precisely when a measure shows low correlations with constructs it is supposed to be different from. Understand construct validity (Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl, 1955) and its two complementary halves, a framework crystallized by Donald Campbell and Donald Fiske in their 1959 multitrait-multimethod approach. Construct validity is the overarching question of whether a test actually measures the theoretical construct it claims to measure, and it is established by accumulating evidence of two kinds that work as mirror images. Convergent validity is evidence that the measure correlates strongly with other measures of the same or closely related constructs; a good resilience scale should correlate highly with existing resilience measures and with theoretically linked traits like optimism, because they should converge. Discriminant validity, sometimes called divergent validity, is the opposite requirement: the measure should correlate weakly, or not at all, with measures of constructs from which it is theoretically distinct. If a resilience scale correlated just as highly with neuroticism as it did with other resilience measures, that would suggest it is not measuring something distinct and is contaminated by the unrelated trait. Demonstrating a low correlation with neuroticism, a conceptually different construct, shows the scale discriminates resilience from mere emotional stability, supporting discriminant validity. Because the question specifies establishing discriminant validity and offers weak correlation with an unrelated construct, that option is correct, while high correlation with a same-construct measure would instead be convergent evidence.","traps":{"a":"Correlating highly with an established measure of the same construct demonstrates convergent validity, not discriminant validity; convergent evidence shows the new scale agrees with other measures of resilience.","c":"Covering all facets of the construct concerns content validity, which is about representativeness of the item domain, not about distinguishing the construct from unrelated ones.","d":"Predicting a future outcome such as the ability to cope with stress is predictive (criterion) validity, evidence that the scale forecasts a relevant behavior, not that it is distinct from an unrelated construct."}},{"q":"The tendency to overestimate the influence of personality and underestimate the influence of situation is called","concept":"The fundamental attribution error, a term coined by Lee Ross, is one of the most robust findings in social psychology. When we try to explain why another person behaved as they did, we systematically overestimate internal, dispositional causes (their personality, character, or attitude) and underestimate external, situational causes (pressures, roles, or circumstances). Seeing someone snap at a waiter, we conclude they are rude, rather than considering that they may have just had terrible news. The classic demonstration comes from Edward Jones and Victor Harris, who found that observers attributed an essay's pro-Castro stance to the writer's true beliefs even when told the writer had been assigned that position with no choice. Fritz Heider's earlier work on attribution laid the groundwork by distinguishing personal from situational causation. Interestingly, the bias flips when we explain our own behaviour: this is the actor-observer asymmetry, where we grant ourselves situational excuses we deny others. The error partly arises because the actor is perceptually salient, the vivid figure against a background we barely notice, so their disposition grabs our causal attention. The phrase in the stem, overestimating personality and underestimating situation, is a near-verbatim definition. Recognizing this tendency fosters humility and empathy, reminding us that behaviour is powerfully shaped by context, a lesson dramatized by situationist studies like Milgram's obedience and Zimbardo's prison experiments.","traps":{"a":"Self-serving bias is crediting our successes to ourselves and blaming failures on circumstances, not the general over-weighting of others' dispositions.","c":"Confirmation bias (Peter Wason, 1960) is the tendency to seek and favour information that supports what we already believe, unrelated to attributing causes of behaviour.","d":"Cognitive dissonance (Leon Festinger, 1957) is the discomfort of holding conflicting beliefs and the drive to reduce it, not an error in explaining someone's behaviour."}},{"q":"Clare complains about her job duties at work, rarely completes assigned tasks, and is regularly late for work. When she is fired, she claims it was due to her co-worker badmouthing her instead of blaming it on her poor work performance. Which defense mechanism explains Clare's reaction?","concept":"The correct answer is rationalization. Rationalization, a defence mechanism described within Sigmund Freud's psychoanalytic framework and elaborated by Anna Freud, involves generating logical-sounding but false explanations to justify behaviour or outcomes that would otherwise threaten one's self-esteem. Clare's poor performance, lateness, and unfinished tasks are the real reasons for her firing, but she protects her ego by blaming a co-worker's alleged badmouthing. This substitutes a socially acceptable, self-flattering cause for the uncomfortable true one, which is the hallmark of rationalization. Denial (option b) differs because it refuses to accept the reality at all, whereas Clare accepts she was fired but distorts why. Displacement redirects an emotion onto a safer target, and sublimation channels impulses into constructive activity, neither of which fits. Recognising rationalization matters clinically because it is extremely common and often invisible to the person using it, allowing them to avoid accountability and repeat maladaptive patterns. Therapists learn to gently surface these self-serving explanations so clients can confront the genuine drivers of their difficulties and take responsibility for change.","traps":{"a":"Displacement would mean redirecting an emotion onto a substitute target, but Clare is inventing an acceptable reason, not shifting emotion.","b":"Denial refuses to acknowledge a reality entirely; Clare acknowledges she was fired but supplies a false cause.","d":"Sublimation channels impulses into productive activity, which is not what is happening here."}},{"q":"A person suffered a head injury in a road accident several years ago. Since the accident, he is unable to form new memories. He can remember many things which he did before the accident, but he cannot remember what he said ten minutes ago. The part of the brain which is most probably injured is:","concept":"The brain region most likely injured is the hippocampus, so the answer is b. The case describes anterograde amnesia: the man can recall events from before his accident (intact remote and old memories) but cannot form new long term memories, forgetting what he said ten minutes ago. This is the classic signature of bilateral hippocampal damage, made famous by the patient H.M. (studied by William Scoville and Brenda Milner, 1957), who after surgical removal of his medial temporal lobes including the hippocampi was unable to form new declarative memories while retaining older memories and normal short term memory and intelligence. The hippocampus is essential for consolidation, the process of converting new short term and working memories into durable long term memories that can later be retrieved. It is not, however, the storage site for old memories, which is why remote memories laid down before the injury survive: those have already been consolidated and distributed to cortical stores, so they remain accessible even when the hippocampus is damaged. Hence damage to the hippocampus selectively impairs the formation of new memories while sparing old ones, exactly the pattern described. The exam discriminator is the dissociation between preserved old memories and an inability to lay down new ones, which points unmistakably to the hippocampus and its role in memory consolidation rather than to brainstem, spinal, or arousal structures.","traps":{"a":"The brain stem governs vital autonomic functions like breathing and heart rate; damage there would not selectively impair new memory formation.","c":"The spinal cord conducts motor and sensory signals between body and brain; it has no role in forming memories.","d":"The reticular formation regulates arousal, sleep, and alertness; its damage affects consciousness and attention, not the specific encoding of new memories."}},{"q":"Which term was used by Erikson to refer to the feeling experienced by some individuals in mid-life that they have achieved very little or nothing and have little to offer to the next generation?","concept":"The correct answer is stagnation. Erik Erikson proposed eight psychosocial stages of development across the lifespan, each defined by a central crisis with a positive and a negative pole. The seventh stage, occurring in middle adulthood, is generativity versus stagnation. Generativity (option b) is the positive resolution: a sense of contributing to society and guiding the next generation through parenting, mentoring, work, or creativity, feeling that one is making a meaningful difference. Stagnation is the negative pole and the answer the question describes: the feeling of having achieved little or nothing and having nothing to pass on to the next generation, accompanied by self-absorption, disconnection, and a sense of unproductiveness. The distractors do not fit: presbyopia (option c) is an age-related loss of near-vision focusing ability, a purely physical change; and mid-life crisis (option d), a popular phrase associated with Elliott Jaques, describes a period of self-doubt in midlife but is not Erikson's specific term. Understanding Erikson's generativity versus stagnation matters because it frames the developmental task of middle adulthood as finding meaning through contribution, and the failure of that task, stagnation, is linked to depression and a sense of emptiness, informing how clinicians understand midlife distress.","traps":{"b":"Generativity is the positive pole of the same stage, the sense of contributing to the next generation, the opposite of the feeling described.","c":"Presbyopia is an age-related decline in near vision, a physical rather than psychosocial phenomenon.","d":"Mid-life crisis is a popular term (associated with Elliott Jaques) but is not Erikson's specific term for this feeling."}},{"q":"Subjective unit of distress (SUD) is mostly used under:","concept":"The Subjective Units of Distress scale is used most centrally in systematic desensitization, so the answer is d. The SUDs scale, introduced by Joseph Wolpe, is a self report rating, typically from 0 (complete calm) to 100 (the worst distress imaginable), that lets a client quantify their level of anxiety or distress at a given moment. Systematic desensitization, also developed by Wolpe and grounded in reciprocal inhibition, is the behaviour therapy in which the client first learns deep muscle relaxation, then constructs a fear hierarchy, an ordered list of feared situations ranked by the anxiety each provokes. The SUDs ratings are precisely how that hierarchy is built and calibrated: the client assigns a SUDs value to each scene, so the items can be arranged from least to most distressing, and during treatment the therapist presents each item (in imagination or in vivo) while the client stays relaxed, moving up the hierarchy only when the SUDs for the current item has dropped to a low level. Thus SUDs is the measurement engine that organises the hierarchy and signals when to progress. While SUDs ratings are also used in exposure therapies generally, the question's framing about constructing graded levels of distress points to systematic desensitization, where the hierarchy and reciprocal inhibition with relaxation are the defining structure that SUDs serves.","traps":{"a":"Exposure and response prevention does monitor distress, but it is the specific OCD protocol of confronting triggers while preventing rituals; SUDs is most classically tied to desensitization's graded hierarchy.","b":"Multimodal therapy (Lazarus) is a broad assessment framework (the BASIC ID), not a procedure built around a SUDs anxiety hierarchy.","c":"Mindfulness based stress management cultivates nonjudgmental present awareness; it does not centrally rely on SUDs ratings to grade exposure."}},{"q":"Negative symptoms of schizophrenia are all except","concept":"Schizophrenia symptoms are traditionally split into positive and negative, a distinction sharpened by Timothy Crow and Nancy Andreasen. Positive symptoms are additions to normal experience: delusions, hallucinations, and disorganized speech, reflecting an excess or distortion of function. Negative symptoms are subtractions, the loss or diminution of normal functions, and Andreasen's five A's capture them well: affective flattening, alogia (poverty of speech), avolition (apathy), anhedonia, and asociality. In the ICD-10, the general criteria list negative symptoms as 'marked apathy, paucity of speech, and blunting or incongruity of emotional responses,' which is exactly why three of the four options here belong together as negative features. A delusion, by contrast, is a fixed false belief held against contrary evidence; it is the prototypical positive symptom and cannot sit in a list of negative ones. So when the stem asks for the exception, the delusion stands apart. Clinically this matters because negative symptoms tend to respond poorly to standard antipsychotics and are the stronger predictors of long-term disability, whereas positive symptoms like delusions often improve with dopamine-blocking medication. Recognizing which bucket a feature belongs to guides both prognosis and treatment planning, and it is a favourite examination point precisely because the boundaries feel subtle.","traps":{"a":"Marked apathy is a core negative symptom reflecting loss of drive and motivation (avolition).","b":"Paucity of speech is alogia, a classic negative symptom describing impoverished, reduced output.","c":"Under ICD-10, blunting or incongruity of emotional responses is explicitly grouped among the negative symptoms, so it does not fit 'except'."}},{"q":"Which of the following sampling methods relies on the researcher's judgment to select participants who are \"typical\" or \"representative\" of the population, and does NOT allow for the calculation of sampling error?","concept":"The answer is quota sampling, because it is the only non-probability method in the list, and the question's two clues (researcher judgment about who is typical, and no calculable sampling error) are the defining marks of non-probability sampling. Build the distinction from the ground up. In probability sampling, every population member has a known, non-zero chance of selection determined by a random mechanism; simple random, stratified, cluster and systematic sampling all belong here, and because the selection probabilities are known, statisticians can compute the sampling error, meaning how much a sample estimate is expected to deviate from the true population value. In non-probability sampling, selection depends on human judgment, convenience or availability, so selection probabilities are unknown and sampling error is mathematically undefined. Quota sampling, associated historically with commercial polling before the famous 1948 polling failures exposed its weakness, works by setting quotas for subgroups (for example 50 men and 50 women) and then letting interviewers fill those quotas with whoever seems typical or is easiest to reach. It superficially mimics stratified sampling because both use subgroups, and that resemblance is exactly the trap examiners set. The test is always the final selection step: stratified sampling selects randomly within strata, quota sampling selects by judgment within quotas. Judgment-based filling plus no sampling error calculation equals quota sampling every time.","traps":{"a":"Simple random sampling gives every member of the population an equal, chance-determined probability of selection, involves no researcher judgment, and fully supports calculation of sampling error.","b":"Stratified random sampling also selects randomly within each stratum, making it a probability method with calculable sampling error, the opposite of judgment-based selection.","d":"Cluster sampling randomly selects intact groups, so despite its larger error it remains a probability technique in which sampling error can still be estimated."}},{"q":"Any figure of form not perceived in parts but organised in perception best possible whole form is known as","concept":"The correct answer is the law of pragnanz. The law of pragnanz, also called the law of good form or the principle of simplicity, is the overarching principle of Gestalt psychology, founded by Max Wertheimer along with Wolfgang Kohler and Kurt Koffka. Pragnanz is German for conciseness or good figure, and the law states that we tend to perceive ambiguous or complex stimuli in the simplest, most stable, most complete and organised form possible; we organise perception into the best possible whole rather than perceiving disconnected parts. This captures the Gestalt insight that the whole is different from, and more than, the sum of its parts. The distractors are specific Gestalt grouping principles that fall under this broader law: the law of proximity (option a) states that elements close together are perceived as a group; the law of continuity (option d) states that we perceive smooth, continuous lines rather than abrupt breaks; and figure-ground organisation (option c) is the tendency to separate an object (figure) from its surroundings (ground). Pragnanz is the general principle from which these more specific laws follow. Understanding it matters because Gestalt principles explain how the brain actively organises raw sensory input into meaningful, coherent perceptions, influencing fields from perception research to design and visual communication.","traps":{"a":"The law of proximity states that objects close together are grouped, not the principle of perceiving the best whole form.","c":"The figure-ground principle (Edgar Rubin, 1915) concerns distinguishing an object from its background, not organising into the simplest whole.","d":"The law of continuity concerns perceiving smooth continuous lines, not the overall simplest, most complete form."}},{"q":"The MMPI measures which of the following?","concept":"The correct answer is all of the above. The Minnesota Multiphasic Personality Inventory (MMPI) is one of the most widely used and researched objective personality and psychopathology assessment instruments. It was developed in the late 1930s and published in 1943 by Starke Hathaway, a psychologist, and J. Charnley McKinley, a psychiatrist, at the University of Minnesota. It is a broad-band, empirically keyed self-report inventory consisting of hundreds of true/false items. The original MMPI contains ten clinical scales that assess a wide range of psychological domains, and these scales span exactly the areas listed: mood and emotional disturbance (for example the Depression and Hypomania scales), physical or somatic concerns (the Hypochondriasis and Hysteria scales, which tap bodily preoccupation), and social attitudes and interpersonal orientation (the Social Introversion scale, plus scales tapping social deviance). It also includes validity scales (such as the L, F, and K scales) to detect defensiveness, exaggeration, or careless responding. Because the MMPI casts a wide net across mood, somatic, and social-attitudinal content, all three options are genuinely assessed, making the inclusive answer correct. The instrument was later revised as the MMPI-2 and MMPI-2-RF. This item tests broad awareness of the MMPI's comprehensive, multi-domain coverage of personality and psychopathology.","traps":{"a":"Mood-related pathology (for example the Depression scale) is assessed by the MMPI, but selecting it alone ignores the many other domains it covers.","b":"Physical or bodily concerns (the Hypochondriasis scale) are measured, but this is only one of the several clinical scales.","c":"Social attitudes and introversion (the Social Introversion scale) are assessed, but again this is only one facet of the broad inventory."}},{"q":"Research suggests that we seek out the company of others because of which motives?","concept":"The correct answer is all of the above. Research on affiliation, the tendency to seek the company of others, identifies several motives, all of which are supported. First, we affiliate to obtain information (option a): being with others, especially in uncertain or novel situations, gives us knowledge about the environment and about how to behave. Second, we affiliate to reduce anxiety (option b): the presence of others can be calming, and classic research by Stanley Schachter showed that people facing a frightening situation preferred to wait with others rather than alone, especially with others in the same predicament, because misery loves miserable company. Third, we affiliate to compare ourselves with similar others (option c): according to social comparison theory, developed by Leon Festinger, we have a drive to evaluate our own opinions and abilities, and when objective standards are lacking we do so by comparing ourselves with other people, particularly those similar to us. Because all three motives are well established, option d is correct. The trap in options a, b, and c is that each is individually true but incomplete, and only the combined answer captures the full picture. Understanding these affiliation motives matters because the need to belong is a fundamental human drive with deep implications for social support, coping with stress, self-evaluation, and mental health, central themes in social and clinical psychology.","traps":{"a":"Obtaining information is a genuine affiliation motive, but choosing only a ignores the other established motives.","b":"Reducing anxiety is a real reason for affiliation, but selecting only b overlooks the other motives.","c":"Social comparison is a valid affiliation motive, but choosing only c misses that all listed motives apply."}},{"q":"Which of the following best describes the concept of 'reciprocal determinism' as proposed by Albert Bandura?","concept":"Reciprocal determinism is the central mechanism of the social cognitive theory of Albert Bandura. It holds that human functioning is the product of a continuous, three-way interaction among personal factors (cognition, beliefs, expectations), behavior, and the environment. None of the three is primary: each influences and is influenced by the other two in a bidirectional loop, which Bandura called triadic reciprocal causation. For example, a student's belief that she can succeed (personal) shapes how hard she studies (behavior), which changes the feedback she gets from teachers (environment), which in turn feeds back into her beliefs. This was a deliberate break from the radical behaviorism of B. F. Skinner, which treated the environment as the sole determinant of behavior, and from purely trait or biological views. Bandura's Bobo doll studies grounded this in observational learning, and his later work on self-efficacy showed how personal cognition steers the whole system. Option b captures the bidirectional, cognition-inclusive character of the model, which is why it is correct.","traps":{"a":"This is pure environmental determinism (classic Watsonian behaviorism), the opposite of Bandura's interactionist stance.","c":"This is biological or genetic determinism, which ignores the environmental and cognitive arms of Bandura's triad.","d":"Bandura placed self-concept and self-efficacy at the center of his theory, so denying reinforcement's effect on it contradicts him."}},{"q":"Which endocrine gland is primarily responsible for controlling the body's stress response and stress-related metabolic changes?","concept":"The answer is the adrenal glands because they are the endocrine glands that directly produce and release the hormones driving the body's stress response and the associated metabolic changes. There are two adrenal glands, one sitting on top of each kidney, and each has two functionally distinct parts. The inner adrenal medulla produces the catecholamines adrenaline (epinephrine) and noradrenaline (norepinephrine), which are released rapidly under sympathetic nervous system activation to produce the immediate fight-or-flight (Walter Cannon) reaction: increased heart rate, raised blood pressure, dilated airways, and mobilisation of glucose for quick energy. The outer adrenal cortex produces corticosteroids, most importantly cortisol, the principal stress hormone of the slower, more sustained stress response; cortisol raises blood sugar, mobilises energy reserves, and modulates immune and metabolic function during prolonged stress. This slower pathway is governed by the hypothalamic-pituitary-adrenal (HPA) axis, in which the hypothalamus releases CRH, prompting the pituitary to release ACTH, which then stimulates the adrenal cortex to release cortisol. That axis explains why the pituitary is a tempting distractor: it signals the adrenals, but it is the adrenals that actually manufacture the stress hormones. The thyroid governs baseline metabolism and the pancreas governs blood sugar, neither being the primary stress gland. So the adrenal glands are the correct answer for the body's stress response.","traps":{"a":"The thyroid gland regulates the body's baseline metabolic rate through thyroid hormones, not the acute stress response asked about here.","c":"The pituitary gland releases ACTH which signals the adrenals, so it participates in the stress axis, but the adrenals are the glands that actually produce the stress hormones.","d":"The pancreas regulates blood glucose through insulin and glucagon; it is not the primary driver of the body's stress response."}},{"q":"Which, if any, of these statements about Piaget's conservation test is accurate?","concept":"The correct answer is that the preoperational child's judgement is swayed by one dimension, such as the fact that the contents of one beaker look taller than the other. Jean Piaget's conservation tasks test whether a child understands that quantity remains constant despite changes in appearance. In the classic liquid conservation task, equal amounts of water are shown in two identical beakers, then one is poured into a taller, narrower beaker; a child who has achieved conservation knows the amount is unchanged, whereas a preoperational child (roughly ages 2 to 7) typically says the taller beaker has more. This error occurs because of centration: the preoperational child centres on a single perceptual dimension (here, height) and ignores the compensating change in another dimension (width), and also lacks reversibility, the ability to mentally reverse the pouring. Option c precisely captures this centration on one dimension. The other statements are false: the test is used across a range of ages, not only under 5 (option a); and pouring liquid into a differently shaped beaker changes its apparent height rather than keeping it the same (option b), which is the very manipulation that reveals non-conservation. Understanding conservation matters because it marks the cognitive limits of preoperational thought and the transition into concrete operations, a landmark of Piaget's developmental theory.","traps":{"a":"The conservation test is used across a range of ages, including children over 5, to see when conservation is achieved, so this is false.","b":"Transferring liquid to a differently shaped beaker changes its apparent height; the level does not stay the same, so this statement is false.","d":"'None of the above' is wrong because statement c is an accurate description of preoperational reasoning."}},{"q":"Electroconvulsive Therapy (ECT) without anesthesia is legally allowed only","concept":"This question tests the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017, which governs psychiatric treatment in India. Section 95 of the Act lists prohibited procedures, and it expressly bans electroconvulsive therapy (Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini, 1938) given without the use of muscle relaxants and anaesthesia. In older practice this was called direct or unmodified ECT, where the seizure was induced without sedation, causing violent muscle contractions and risk of injury. Modern modified ECT uses general anaesthesia and a muscle relaxant so the procedure is safe and humane. Under the 2017 Act, unmodified ECT is not permitted under any circumstances, and ECT is further prohibited for minors except with special safeguards and Mental Health Review Board approval. Because the statute makes anaesthesia mandatory, ECT without anaesthesia is simply never lawful, which is why the correct response is that it is never allowed under law. The reform reflected a broader rights-based shift in Indian mental health legislation, moving away from the older Mental Health Act of 1987 toward autonomy, dignity, and informed consent, influenced by India's ratification of the UN Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities. Note the option framing this as permissible in emergencies is a distractor: emergency provisions in the Act allow treatment, but never the specifically prohibited unmodified form, so the blanket prohibition governs. (No official key released; answer expert-determined and flagged as debatable.)","traps":{"a":"Even in emergencies, Indian law requires ECT to be given with anaesthesia and muscle relaxants, so consent alone cannot legalise the unmodified form.","b":"A psychiatrist's request cannot override a statutory prohibition; unmodified ECT is barred regardless of clinical preference.","c":"The law provides no research exemption permitting ECT without anaesthesia; the prohibition is absolute."}},{"q":"A client reports a persistent pattern of seeking admiration, but their behavior is characterized by excessive emotionality, attention-seeking, and seductive behavior, rather than grandiosity. This presentation is more characteristic of:","concept":"The answer is histrionic personality disorder, because the picture of excessive emotionality, attention-seeking, and seductive or provocative behavior, rather than grandiosity, is the defining profile of histrionic personality disorder. Understand the Cluster B personality disorders as a set, since examiners test the fine discriminations among them. Cluster B is the dramatic, emotional, and erratic cluster and contains antisocial, borderline, narcissistic, and histrionic personality disorders. Histrionic personality disorder, from a Latin root meaning theatrical or actor-like, describes people who are uncomfortable when not the center of attention and go to lengths to command it: they display rapidly shifting, shallow emotions, use physical appearance and often inappropriately seductive or provocative behavior to draw notice, speak in an impressionistic, vague style, are highly suggestible, and tend to consider relationships more intimate than they are. The crucial exam contrast is with narcissistic personality disorder, because both crave attention and admiration. The discriminator is the mechanism of that craving: the narcissist seeks admiration to confirm a grandiose sense of superiority and specialness, whereas the histrionic person seeks attention through emotional display, charm, and seductiveness without necessarily believing they are superior. The stem hands you the discriminator explicitly by saying the behavior is characterized by emotionality and seductiveness rather than grandiosity, which rules narcissism out and points to histrionic. So the pattern of dramatic, attention-hungry, seductive behavior minus grandiosity equals histrionic personality disorder.","traps":{"a":"Narcissistic personality disorder centers on grandiosity, entitlement, and a need for admiration built on a sense of superiority, whereas this client's pattern is dramatic emotionality and seductiveness without that grandiose core.","c":"Borderline personality disorder is defined by pervasive instability of relationships, identity, and affect with fears of abandonment and self-harm, not by the theatrical, attention-seeking style described.","d":"Dependent personality disorder features submissive, clinging behavior and an excessive need to be taken care of, the opposite of the flamboyant, admiration-courting presentation here."}},{"q":"Duncan's multiple range test, Newman-Keuls test, Tukey's HSD test, and Scheffe test can be used only when:","concept":"The correct answer is that the F ratio is significant. These four procedures, Duncan's multiple range test, the Newman-Keuls test, John Tukey's Honestly Significant Difference (HSD) test, and Henry Scheffe's test, are all post hoc (a posteriori) multiple comparison tests. They are used to follow up a one-way analysis of variance developed by Ronald Fisher. ANOVA produces an omnibus F ratio that tells you only whether at least one group mean differs from the others; it does not identify which specific pairs differ. Post hoc tests are therefore run only after a significant F establishes that a real difference exists somewhere among the means. Running them when F is non-significant would inflate the family-wise Type I error rate without any justified overall effect to decompose. Each test controls that error differently: Scheffe is the most conservative and flexible for complex contrasts, Tukey's HSD is standard for all pairwise comparisons, and Duncan's is comparatively liberal. The unifying rule tested here is that a significant omnibus F is the entry ticket for any of them.","traps":{"a":"If the F ratio is not significant, there is no overall difference to decompose, so post hoc tests are not warranted.","b":"Post hoc tests presuppose a computed and significant F, so an undeterminable F rules them out.","d":"The t ratio governs pairwise t-tests, not the omnibus ANOVA that these post hoc procedures follow."}},{"q":"In operant conditioning, a fixed-ratio schedule of reinforcement produces:","concept":"Schedules of reinforcement, systematized by B. F. Skinner, describe the rule linking responses to reinforcers and each produces a characteristic response pattern. In a fixed-ratio (FR) schedule, reinforcement follows a set number of responses, for example every fifth lever press (FR-5). This generates a high and steady rate of responding, because more responses mean faster reinforcement, followed by a brief post-reinforcement pause immediately after each reinforcer is delivered, producing the classic break-and-run cumulative record. By contrast, fixed-interval schedules yield a scalloped pattern with slow responding after reinforcement that accelerates toward the end of the interval. Variable schedules (variable-ratio and variable-interval) produce very steady responding without post-reinforcement pauses, and variable-ratio schedules, such as those in slot machines, are the most resistant to extinction. Continuous reinforcement extinguishes fastest. Option a correctly pairs the fixed-ratio schedule with high, steady rates plus the characteristic short pause after each reinforcement, which is the textbook signature of this schedule.","traps":{"b":"Low, irregular rates are more typical of fixed-interval schedules early in the interval, not fixed-ratio schedules.","c":"Rapid extinction is characteristic of continuous reinforcement, not intermittent ratio schedules, which are more extinction-resistant.","d":"Variable, delay-dependent responding does not describe the crisp, count-based fixed-ratio pattern."}},{"q":"How many Rorschach cards are coloured?","concept":"The correct answer is 5. The Rorschach Inkblot Test, created by the Swiss psychiatrist Hermann Rorschach and published in 1921 in his work Psychodiagnostik, is a projective personality assessment consisting of ten standardized inkblot cards shown one at a time, with the examinee asked what each might be. Of the ten cards, five are achromatic (black and grey only), and five contain colour. Among the coloured cards, two (Cards II and III) combine black with red, and three (Cards VIII, IX, and X) are fully multicoloured. So the count of cards containing any colour is five. This distinction matters interpretively: in scoring systems such as the Comprehensive System of John Exner, responses to the chromatic cards, especially colour-based determinants, are linked to how a person processes and modulates emotion and affect, whereas responses to the achromatic cards and shading relate to other dimensions such as dysphoria and anxiety. Test-takers sometimes confuse this with the total of ten cards or with the three fully coloured cards, but the precise answer to how many cards are coloured is five, making option b correct.","traps":{"a":"10 is the total number of Rorschach cards, not the number that are coloured.","c":"3 corresponds to the fully multicoloured cards only, omitting the two black-red cards.","d":"4 is not a correct count of the coloured cards in the standard set."}},{"q":"Norms that suggest to people the way they ought to behave are called:","concept":"Norms that tell people how they ought to behave are prescriptive norms, so the answer is c. Social psychology distinguishes two kinds of social norms, a distinction sharpened by Cialdini and colleagues. Descriptive norms describe what people actually do, the typical or common behaviour in a situation, and they influence us by signalling what is likely effective or normal. Prescriptive norms, also called injunctive norms, specify what people ought to do, what behaviour is approved or disapproved of by the group, and they influence us through the prospect of social reward or sanction. The word prescriptive itself carries the sense of prescribing or mandating, which is the cue that it concerns the ought rather than the is. The distinction matters because the two can conflict and can be harnessed in behaviour change campaigns; telling people that most others litter (a descriptive norm of bad behaviour) can backfire, whereas conveying social disapproval of littering (an injunctive or prescriptive norm) more reliably reduces it. So when a question contrasts norms about what is done with norms about what should be done, the should be done category is the prescriptive or injunctive norm. The exam discriminator is the phrase ought to behave, which precisely marks the prescriptive norm rather than the merely descriptive one.","traps":{"a":"Descriptive norms capture what people actually do, the common behaviour, not what they ought to do.","b":"Idealistic norms is not the standard social psychological term; the recognised label for ought norms is prescriptive or injunctive.","d":"Perceived norms refers to a person's beliefs about what others do or approve of, which can be inaccurate, and is not specifically the ought category."}},{"q":"According to Freud, the id is to the ___ principle as the ego is to the ____ principle.","concept":"The correct answer is pleasure, reality. In Sigmund Freud's structural model of the psyche, personality comprises three parts: the id, the ego, and the superego. The id is the most primitive, unconscious part, present from birth, and it operates on the pleasure principle: it demands immediate gratification of instinctual drives and seeks to reduce tension without regard for reality, logic, or consequences. The ego develops to mediate between the id's demands, the constraints of the external world, and the moral standards of the superego, and it operates on the reality principle: it delays gratification and finds realistic, socially acceptable ways to satisfy the id's urges. Thus the id is to the pleasure principle as the ego is to the reality principle. The distractors confuse principles with drive contents: aggressive and sexual (options a and b) refer to Freud's two basic instincts, Eros (the life or sexual drive) and Thanatos (the death or aggressive drive), not to the operating principles of these structures. Option d simply reverses the correct pairing. This distinction is fundamental to psychoanalytic theory because the constant negotiation between the pleasure-seeking id and the reality-oriented ego, refereed by the superego, is Freud's account of how personality functions and how inner conflict and anxiety arise.","traps":{"a":"Aggressive and sexual name drives (Thanatos and Eros), not the guiding principles of the id and ego.","b":"Sexual and aggressive again describe drive contents rather than the id's pleasure principle and the ego's reality principle.","d":"This reverses the correct pairing; the id follows the pleasure principle and the ego the reality principle, not the other way around."}},{"q":"Identify the techniques of Functional Neuroimaging:","concept":"PET and SPECT are the functional neuroimaging techniques because they measure brain activity (function) rather than just brain structure. The fundamental divide in neuroimaging is between structural and functional methods. Structural imaging shows the anatomy of the brain, its physical shape and any lesions or atrophy: this includes the CT scan, which uses X-rays to build cross-sectional images, and conventional MRI, which uses strong magnetic fields and radiofrequency pulses to produce highly detailed images of soft tissue. Functional imaging, by contrast, captures the brain in action by tracking physiological activity such as blood flow, glucose metabolism, or oxygen use, which rise in regions that are working harder. Positron emission tomography (PET) injects a radioactive tracer, often labeled glucose, and detects the gamma rays emitted, mapping which regions consume the most energy during a task. Single photon emission computed tomography (SPECT) works on a similar radiotracer principle, measuring regional cerebral blood flow. Both reveal what the brain is doing, not merely what it looks like. (Functional MRI, which detects the BOLD signal from blood oxygenation, is also a functional technique, but plain MRI as listed here is structural.) So the correct grouping of functional methods is PET and SPECT.","traps":{"a":"A CT scan is a structural technique using X-rays to image anatomy; it shows what the brain looks like, not what it is doing.","b":"Conventional Magnetic Resonance Imaging is primarily a structural method showing detailed anatomy; it is functional only in the special fMRI variant, which is not what plain MRI denotes here.","d":"'All of the above' is wrong because it would include CT and structural MRI, which are anatomical rather than functional imaging methods."}},{"q":"According to the schema theory, which hypothetical construct provides a prototype for subsequent attachments and can be adapted through more experience?","concept":"The correct answer is the internal working model. John Bowlby, the founder of attachment theory, proposed that through early interactions with the primary caregiver, an infant builds an internal working model, a mental representation or schema of the self, of attachment figures, and of relationships in general. This cognitive-affective template captures whether the self is worthy of love and whether others can be trusted to be available and responsive. Crucially, the internal working model acts as a prototype that guides expectations, feelings, and behaviour in later relationships, including friendships and adult romantic bonds, which is the basis of the continuity hypothesis. Bowlby argued that the model is not fixed: while it tends to be stable, it can be adapted and updated through significant new relational experiences over time. This connects to the schema concept in cognitive psychology, where schemas are organised knowledge structures that shape perception and can accommodate new information. The distractors name real attachment ideas (proximity-seeking behaviour, the attachment behavioural system) but not the internal representational template that serves as a prototype. This item tests understanding of Bowlby's internal working model as the mental blueprint for future attachments.","traps":{"a":"Proximity-seeking is a behaviour (staying close to the caregiver), not a mental representation or prototype that guides future relationships.","b":"The attachment system is the overall behavioural-motivational system regulating closeness; it is not the specific cognitive template used as a prototype for later bonds.","c":"Bio-social relationship model is not an established attachment-theory construct and does not name the internal representation described."}},{"q":"Within the cognitive approach, the ABC model describes:","concept":"The correct answer is A equals activating events, B equals beliefs about the events, and C equals consequences of the beliefs about the event. The ABC model is the cornerstone of Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), developed by Albert Ellis, and it is foundational to the broader cognitive approach to emotional disturbance. The model's central insight is that it is not events themselves that directly cause our emotional and behavioural reactions, but rather our beliefs and interpretations about those events. In the model, A stands for the Activating event or adversity (the situation or trigger), B stands for the Beliefs the person holds about that event (which may be rational or irrational), and C stands for the emotional and behavioural Consequences that follow, which are produced by B rather than directly by A. For example, failing an exam (A) does not itself cause despair; rather, the irrational belief 'I must always succeed and I am worthless if I fail' (B) produces the depressive consequence (C). Ellis later extended the model to D (Disputing the irrational beliefs) and E (the new Effective philosophy or emotional outcome), which is why option C wrongly assigns 'critical analysis' to C; disputing is a separate step. Options A and D distort the definitions of the components. This item tests correct knowledge of Ellis's ABC model.","traps":{"a":"This narrows the beliefs and consequences to be only 'about oneself', whereas in the ABC model the beliefs concern the activating event, not specifically oneself.","c":"This wrongly defines C as 'critical analysis' of beliefs; C stands for the emotional and behavioural consequences, while critical analysis or disputing is a separate step (D).","d":"This mislabels A as an 'anticipatory event' (it is the activating event) and confusingly uses D instead of C for consequences."}},{"q":"A person is categorised as severe mentally retarded if the IQ is between","concept":"The correct answer is 21 to 34. In the traditional classification of intellectual disability (historically termed mental retardation) based on IQ scores, using systems such as the ICD and older DSM criteria, the levels are divided into four bands. Mild intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ of roughly 50 to 70, where individuals can often achieve basic academic and vocational skills and live relatively independently with support. Moderate intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ of about 35 to 49, with individuals able to learn self-care and simple communication but needing more support. Severe intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ of approximately 20 to 34 (the question's 21 to 34), where individuals have very limited communication and require substantial daily support. Profound intellectual disability corresponds to an IQ below 20, with pervasive support needs across all areas. The distractors correspond to the adjacent bands: 50 to 70 is mild (option a), 35 to 49 is moderate (option b), and 0 to 20 is profound (option d). It is worth noting that the DSM-5 has shifted away from relying primarily on IQ cutoffs toward assessing adaptive functioning across conceptual, social, and practical domains, following the framework of the American Association on Intellectual and Developmental Disabilities. Still, knowing these classic IQ bands matters for assessment questions and for understanding the historical grading of severity.","traps":{"a":"An IQ of 50 to 70 corresponds to mild intellectual disability, not severe.","b":"An IQ of 35 to 49 corresponds to moderate intellectual disability, not severe.","d":"An IQ below 20 corresponds to profound intellectual disability, more severe than the severe category."}},{"q":"A researcher is interested in studying the effect of noise on the problem-solving abilities of elementary school students. She uses two classes of fourth-grade students and tests them on a 10-problem test. When one class takes the test, she turns on a radio, keeping it off for the other class. This is an example of a :","concept":"The correct answer is a quasi-experiment. The defining feature that separates a true experiment from a quasi-experiment, as detailed by Donald Campbell and Julian Stanley, is random assignment of participants to conditions. In a true experiment, each individual participant is randomly assigned to the treatment or control condition, which makes the groups equivalent on average and licenses causal inference. In this study, the researcher does manipulate an independent variable (noise present versus absent) and measures a dependent variable (problem-solving performance), so it is experimental in form rather than correlational or observational. However, she does not randomly assign individual students; instead she uses two intact, pre-existing classes and assigns the noise condition to one whole class and the quiet condition to the other. Because the groups are naturally occurring rather than formed by random assignment, any pre-existing differences between the two classes (in ability, teacher, prior noise exposure, and so on) are confounded with the noise manipulation, weakening internal validity. This use of a manipulated variable with non-randomly-assigned, intact groups is precisely what defines a quasi-experiment. It is not naturalistic observation, since the researcher actively manipulates the noise, and it is not merely correlational, since there is a manipulated cause. Therefore option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"A true experiment requires random assignment of individuals to conditions, which is absent when intact classes are used.","c":"This study manipulates an independent variable (noise), so it is experimental, not merely correlational.","d":"The researcher actively manipulates conditions rather than passively observing, so it is not naturalistic observation."}},{"q":"Premack Principle explains:","concept":"The Premack principle is a behaviour modification technique, especially useful with children, so the answer is b. Formulated by David Premack, the principle states that a more probable or preferred behaviour can be used to reinforce a less probable or less preferred behaviour. In plainer terms, access to a high frequency, enjoyable activity is made contingent on first performing a low frequency, less appealing one. The everyday version is grandma's rule: you may have your dessert (preferred) only after you eat your vegetables (less preferred). In practice with children this becomes a powerful and easily applied reinforcement strategy: a child may play video games (high probability behaviour) only after finishing homework (low probability behaviour), so the preferred activity reinforces completion of the chore. The theoretical importance is that Premack reframed reinforcement relativistically; rather than reinforcers being a fixed class of stimuli, any behaviour can reinforce another as long as it is more probable, which depends on the individual and the moment. This made reinforcement more flexible and individualised than Skinner's original notion. For the exam, the discriminating idea is the contingency of a preferred activity upon a less preferred one as a method of behaviour change, which is exactly a technique of behaviour modification rather than a theory of relationships, language, or parenting impact.","traps":{"a":"Interpersonal relationship processes are not what the Premack principle addresses; it is a reinforcement contingency, not a relational model.","c":"Language development patterns are unrelated; the principle concerns the relative probability of behaviours, not linguistic acquisition.","d":"The impact of parenting on behavioural problems is a developmental research topic, not the definition of the Premack reinforcement technique."}},{"q":"What is the age range valid for Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children, 5th edition (WISC-V)?","concept":"The correct answer is 6 years to 16 years 11 months. The Wechsler Intelligence Scale for Children (WISC), originally developed by David Wechsler, is an individually administered test of intelligence for school-age children, and its fifth edition, the WISC-V, is validated for children aged 6 years 0 months through 16 years 11 months. This age band sits between the two other Wechsler instruments: the Wechsler Preschool and Primary Scale of Intelligence (WPPSI) covers younger children (roughly 2 years 6 months to 7 years 7 months), and the Wechsler Adult Intelligence Scale (WAIS) covers ages 16 and above, with a slight overlap around age 16. Wechsler's scales moved beyond a single ratio IQ (William Stern, 1912) to yield a Full Scale IQ derived from multiple index scores; in the WISC-V these are the Verbal Comprehension, Visual Spatial, Fluid Reasoning, Working Memory, and Processing Speed indexes. A key Wechsler innovation was the use of the deviation IQ, in which a person's score is expressed relative to the distribution of same-age peers (mean 100, standard deviation 15) rather than as a mental-age ratio. Because the WISC-V specifically spans from age 6 to 16 years 11 months, option b is correct.","traps":{"a":"5 to 12 years is too narrow and understates the upper limit of 16 years 11 months.","c":"The lower bound is 6 years, not 7, so this misstates the starting age.","d":"The upper bound is 16 years 11 months, not 15 years 11 months, so this understates the top of the range."}},{"q":"'The more we see and interact with a person, the more likely he or she is to become our friend or sexual partner.' This denotes:","concept":"The statement that the more we see and interact with someone the more likely they are to become a friend or partner denotes the propinquity effect, so the answer is a. The propinquity effect (from propinquity, meaning nearness or proximity) is the social psychological finding that physical and functional proximity is one of the strongest predictors of attraction and the formation of relationships. People are far more likely to befriend, like, and partner with those they encounter frequently, neighbours, classmates, coworkers, those whose paths cross theirs by virtue of where they live, study, or work. The classic demonstration is Festinger, Schachter, and Back's study of friendship in a student housing complex, which found that friendships formed overwhelmingly among people whose apartments were close together and whose functional distance (likelihood of incidental contact) was small. The mechanism behind propinquity is closely tied to the mere exposure effect, Zajonc's finding that repeated exposure to a stimulus increases liking for it, and to the availability and ease of interaction that proximity affords. The question's phrasing emphasises seeing and interacting with a person, the contact afforded by nearness, which is the defining cause in the propinquity effect. The exam discriminator is that propinquity stresses proximity and repeated contact as the route to relationship formation, the most precise label for the stated principle.","traps":{"b":"The exposure effect (mere exposure) is the closely related underlying mechanism, but the principle stated, that contact through nearness breeds relationships, is specifically labelled the propinquity effect.","c":"The matching hypothesis (Elaine Walster and colleagues, 1966) holds that people pair with others of similar attractiveness, a different determinant of attraction than proximity and contact.","d":"Reciprocity is the tendency to like those who like us; it concerns mutual liking, not the proximity and repeated interaction described."}},{"q":"Allport believed that traits could be organised into three levels:","concept":"The correct answer is cardinal, central, and secondary. Gordon Allport, a pioneer of the trait approach to personality, proposed that traits, enduring dispositions that make a person's behaviour consistent across situations, could be organised into three hierarchical levels. Cardinal traits are rare, dominant traits that shape virtually every aspect of a person's life and behaviour, so pervasive that the person may become known for them (for example, a personality so generous it is called Christlike); most people do not have a cardinal trait. Central traits are the general characteristics that form the basic building blocks of personality, the handful of core qualities (such as honest, kind, or anxious) one might use to describe someone. Secondary traits are more situation-specific and peripheral, such as preferences or attitudes that appear only under particular circumstances. The distractors belong to other frameworks: id, ego, and superego (option c) are Sigmund Freud's structural components of the psyche, not traits; while primary/secondary/tertiary (option a) and cognitive/emotional/physiological (option b) are generic labels not used by Allport. Understanding Allport's hierarchy matters because he helped establish the trait perspective and the idiographic study of individual uniqueness, foundational to modern personality psychology and later trait models like the Big Five.","traps":{"a":"Primary, secondary, tertiary is a generic ranking, not Allport's specific trait terminology.","b":"Cognitive, emotional, physiological describes components of responses, not Allport's levels of traits.","c":"Id, ego, superego are Freud's structures of personality, not Allport's trait levels."}},{"q":"The primary brain region responsible for the comprehension of spoken and written language is:","concept":"The answer is Wernicke's area, located in the left superior temporal gyrus, because it is the primary brain region responsible for the comprehension of spoken and written language. Understand the classic language model, the Wernicke-Geschwind model, which organizes the two great language areas by function. Carl Wernicke, a German neurologist, described in 1874 patients who could speak fluently but produced meaningless, jumbled output and could not understand what was said to them, and he localized the responsible damage to the posterior superior temporal gyrus of the left hemisphere, now called Wernicke's area. This region sits near the auditory cortex and is specialized for decoding the meaning of language, both heard and read. Damage there produces Wernicke's aphasia, also called receptive or fluent aphasia: speech remains grammatically smooth and effortless in its melody but is full of paraphasias and neologisms, empty of meaning (word salad), and comprehension is severely impaired, so the patient often does not realize their speech makes no sense. Contrast this with Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus, discovered by Paul Broca in the 1860s, which handles speech production and grammar; its damage causes non-fluent, effortful, telegraphic speech with relatively preserved comprehension. The two are connected by a white-matter tract, the arcuate fasciculus, whose damage causes conduction aphasia. Because the question asks specifically about comprehension of language, the answer is Wernicke's area in the superior temporal gyrus.","traps":{"a":"Broca's area in the left inferior frontal gyrus governs speech production and grammatical output, so its damage impairs the ability to produce fluent speech, not the comprehension of language.","c":"The primary auditory cortex (Heschl's gyrus) processes basic acoustic features of sound such as frequency and intensity, but it does not extract linguistic meaning, which is the job of the adjacent Wernicke's area.","d":"The angular gyrus contributes to reading, writing, and cross-modal integration such as linking written and spoken forms, playing a supporting role rather than being the primary center for comprehending spoken and written language."}},{"q":"Type D (disorganised) babies are:","concept":"The correct answer is that Type D babies show no one clear pattern, but display inconsistent and often bizarre responses to separation and reunion. The disorganised or disoriented attachment category (Type D) was identified by Mary Main and Judith Solomon as an addition to Mary Ainsworth's original three-category system (secure Type B, avoidant Type A, resistant or ambivalent Type C). Disorganised infants lack a coherent, organised strategy for coping with distress in the Strange Situation. Instead they exhibit contradictory, confused, and sometimes odd behaviours: they may freeze, rock, approach the caregiver then abruptly move away, show fearful or dazed expressions, or fall to the floor. This pattern is often associated with frightening, abusive, or severely inconsistent caregiving, where the caregiver is simultaneously the source of comfort and the source of fear, leaving the infant in an unsolvable approach-avoidance conflict (Kurt Lewin, 1935). Disorganised attachment is the strongest attachment predictor of later psychopathology. The distractors correctly describe the three organised patterns (avoidant, secure, and resistant respectively), which is why they are wrong for Type D. This item tests recognition of the defining incoherent, disoriented quality of disorganised attachment.","traps":{"a":"This describes the insecure-avoidant (Type A) pattern of avoiding the caregiver, not the disorganised Type D.","b":"Actively seeking and maintaining proximity describes secure (Type B) attachment, where the infant uses the caregiver as a secure base.","c":"Conspicuous contact-resisting behaviour describes the insecure-resistant or ambivalent (Type C) pattern, not disorganised attachment."}},{"q":"Which is the correct existing legislation for mental health services in India?","concept":"The correct current legislation is the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017, so the answer is c. The Mental Healthcare Act, 2017 came into force in 2018 and repealed and replaced the earlier Mental Health Act of 1987. It marked a major rights based shift in Indian mental health law, aligning the country with the United Nations Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, which India ratified. Its central features include a rights based approach that guarantees access to mental healthcare and treatment; the decriminalisation of attempted suicide, presuming that a person who attempts suicide is under severe stress and should not be punished; the provision for an advance directive, allowing a person to state in advance how they wish to be treated and to appoint a nominated representative; the establishment of Mental Health Review Boards to protect rights and oversee admissions; regulation of admission, treatment, and the use of restraints and electroconvulsive therapy (Ugo Cerletti and Lucio Bini, 1938); and a definition of mental illness and of capacity to make treatment decisions. For Indian clinical psychology exams this is essential, current statutory knowledge. The discriminating skill is to recognise that the 1987 Act is repealed and superseded, and that the options Persons with Mental Illness Act 2016 and Indian Mental Health Act 2018 are fabricated or misnamed, leaving the Mental Healthcare Act 2017 as the genuine governing law.","traps":{"a":"The Mental Health Act, 1987 was the previous law but it has been repealed by the 2017 Act, so it is no longer the existing legislation.","b":"Persons with Mental Illness Act, 2016 is not a real statute; the 2016 era law relevant to disability is the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act, not this.","d":"Indian Mental Health Act, 2018 is a fabricated title; the operative law came into force in 2018 but is named the Mental Healthcare Act, 2017."}},{"q":"Within attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder, which of the following is NOT classified as a problem relating to hyperactivity/impulsivity?","concept":"The correct answer is that not appearing to listen when spoken to is NOT a hyperactivity/impulsivity symptom, because it belongs instead to the inattention cluster. Attention deficit/hyperactivity disorder (ADHD) is a neurodevelopmental disorder whose DSM criteria are organised into two symptom domains. The inattention domain includes symptoms such as failing to give close attention to details, difficulty sustaining attention, not seeming to listen when spoken to directly, failing to follow through on instructions, difficulty organising tasks, avoiding tasks requiring sustained mental effort, losing things, being easily distracted, and forgetfulness. The hyperactivity/impulsivity domain includes fidgeting and squirming, leaving one's seat, running or climbing inappropriately, being unable to play quietly, being 'on the go' as if driven by a motor, talking excessively, blurting out answers, difficulty waiting one's turn, and interrupting others. ADHD presentations can be predominantly inattentive, predominantly hyperactive-impulsive, or combined. In this item, options A (difficulty playing quietly), B (squirming and fidgeting), and C (talking excessively) are all hyperactivity/impulsivity symptoms, whereas option D (not appearing to listen) is an inattention symptom, making it the correct exception. This item tests the student's ability to sort ADHD symptoms into the correct diagnostic cluster.","traps":{"a":"Difficulty playing quietly is a listed hyperactivity-impulsivity symptom, so it does not fit the 'not hyperactive' answer.","b":"Squirming and fidgeting is a classic hyperactivity symptom, so it belongs to that cluster and is not the exception.","c":"Talking excessively is a recognised hyperactivity-impulsivity symptom, so it is not the odd one out."}},{"q":"Which scale is used when counting the number of books a person has?","concept":"Choosing the measurement scale, per the taxonomy of Stanley Smith Stevens, depends on the properties of the variable. Counting the number of books a person owns produces a ratio scale variable. A ratio scale has all the properties of the lower scales, distinct categories (nominal), meaningful order (ordinal), and equal intervals between values (interval), plus the crucial additional feature of a true, absolute zero point. Zero books literally means the complete absence of books, a genuine and meaningful zero, not an arbitrary reference point. This true zero is what permits ratio statements: someone with 20 books has twice as many as someone with 10, and such multiplicative comparisons are valid only on a ratio scale. Counts of discrete objects, along with weight, height, age, and reaction time, are classic ratio-scale variables. The distractors fail because a nominal scale has no quantity, an ordinal scale has no equal intervals, and an interval scale (such as Celsius temperature) lacks a true zero. Because a book count has a meaningful zero and supports ratios, option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"A nominal scale labels unordered categories; a count of books is a true quantity, not a mere category.","b":"An ordinal scale gives rank order without equal intervals or a true zero; a book count has both.","c":"An interval scale lacks a true zero, but zero books genuinely means the absence of books, so this is not interval."}},{"q":"Among the following schedules of reinforcement, which typically produces the highest rate of response and is most resistant to extinction?","concept":"The answer is variable ratio, because among the schedules of reinforcement the variable ratio schedule produces the highest rate of responding and the greatest resistance to extinction. Understand why this schedule is so powerful within B. F. Skinner's operant conditioning framework. A variable ratio schedule reinforces a response after an unpredictable number of responses that varies around some average; for instance, on a VR-10 schedule reinforcement comes on average every tenth response, but the actual number required fluctuates unpredictably from one reinforcement to the next. Two properties combine to make this schedule uniquely potent. First, because it is a ratio schedule, reinforcement depends directly on how many responses the organism makes, so the faster the organism responds, the more reinforcement it earns, which drives a very high, sustained rate of responding. Second, because the ratio is variable and unpredictable, the organism can never know which particular response will be the one that pays off, so the very next response might be rewarded, and this uncertainty keeps the organism responding vigorously and continuously without the post-reinforcement pauses seen in fixed schedules. This same unpredictability makes variable ratio schedules extraordinarily resistant to extinction: when reinforcement stops, the organism cannot easily tell the difference between an unusually long unrewarded run (which was normal during training) and actual extinction, so it keeps responding for a long time. The prototypical real-world example is gambling on a slot machine, which pays off on a variable ratio schedule and hooks players into persistent, rapid play. So for the highest and most extinction-resistant responding, the answer is variable ratio.","traps":{"a":"A fixed ratio schedule produces high response rates but includes a characteristic post-reinforcement pause after each reinforcement, and it is less resistant to extinction than a variable ratio schedule.","c":"A fixed interval schedule produces a low, scalloped pattern of responding with slow rates early in the interval, so it yields neither the highest response rate nor the greatest extinction resistance.","d":"A variable interval schedule produces steady but moderate response rates, lower than the high-output variable ratio schedule, and less resistant to extinction."}},{"q":"What is psychometric reliability primarily concerned with?","concept":"Psychometric reliability refers to the consistency, stability, and dependability of the scores a test yields, and it can be expressed in several complementary ways, all of which describe the same underlying property. In classical test theory, an observed score is modeled as a true score plus random error, and reliability is the degree to which a test is free from such measurement error (option a), which is one legitimate way to define it. Reliability is equally described as the consistency of a test across occasions, items, or raters (option b), captured by test-retest, internal consistency such as coefficient alpha of Lee Cronbach, and inter-rater reliability. And it is described as the dependability of the scores (option c), meaning one can trust that similar conditions will yield similar results. Because freedom from error, consistency, and dependability are all valid and overlapping characterizations of the single concept of reliability, the most complete and correct answer encompasses all of them. Therefore option d, all of the above, is correct.","traps":{"a":"Freedom from measurement error is one aspect of reliability but not the whole concept, so it is incomplete on its own.","b":"Consistency is a central aspect of reliability but, taken alone, does not capture the full definition.","c":"Dependability of scores is another facet of reliability but is only one part of the complete answer."}},{"q":"How people organize information in memory and use it to understand behavior is called:","concept":"How people organise information in memory and use it to understand behaviour is the schema approach, so the answer is b. A schema is an organised mental structure or framework of knowledge about a concept, object, person, situation, or event, built up from experience, that guides how we perceive, interpret, encode, remember, and act on new information. The concept traces to Bartlett's classic memory research, showing that people reconstruct rather than reproduce memories, fitting them to pre existing schemas, and it was developed further by Piaget in cognitive development and by social and cognitive psychologists. In social cognition, schemas (including person schemas, role schemas, self schemas, and event schemas or scripts) are central to how we make sense of the social world and understand and predict behaviour, allowing us to process complex information efficiently, fill in gaps with default assumptions, and form expectations, though at the cost of biases and stereotyping. The schema approach therefore refers precisely to the framework that studies how knowledge is organised in memory and used to interpret and explain behaviour. The exam discriminator is matching organising information in memory and using it to understand behaviour to the schema construct, distinguishing it from motivational, perceptual, or vague organisational approaches that do not capture the specific knowledge structure idea.","traps":{"a":"The motivational approach explains behaviour in terms of needs, drives, and goals, not in terms of organised knowledge structures in memory.","c":"The perceptual approach concerns how we sense and interpret stimuli, a narrower process than the organised memory frameworks schemas describe.","d":"Organizational approach is a vague distractor; the established term for organised knowledge structures used to understand behaviour is schema."}},{"q":"Which of the following traits is not part of the five-factor model of personality?","concept":"The correct answer is anxiety, which is not one of the five factors. The five-factor model of personality, also called the Big Five, was developed and validated by researchers including Paul Costa and Robert McCrae, building on earlier lexical work by Lewis Goldberg. It identifies five broad dimensions that capture the structure of human personality, commonly remembered by the acronym OCEAN: Openness to experience (imaginative, curious versus conventional), Conscientiousness (organised, dependable versus careless), Extraversion (sociable, energetic versus reserved), Agreeableness (compassionate, cooperative versus antagonistic), and Neuroticism (emotionally unstable, anxious versus calm). Among the options, openness (a), extraversion (c), and agreeableness (d) are all genuine factors. Anxiety (b) is not itself one of the five factors; rather, it is a facet or component of the broader dimension of neuroticism, which encompasses anxiety, hostility, depression, and vulnerability to stress. The trap is that anxiety feels central to personality and does relate to the model, but it sits under neuroticism rather than standing as its own factor. Knowing the correct five dimensions matters because the Big Five is the most empirically supported and cross-culturally replicated framework in personality psychology, widely used in research, clinical assessment, and organisational settings, so students must distinguish the five factors from their subordinate facets.","traps":{"a":"Openness (to experience) is one of the five factors, so it is not the answer.","c":"Extraversion is a core dimension of the five-factor model, so it is not the answer.","d":"Agreeableness is one of the Big Five traits, so it is not the answer."}},{"q":"For which of the following neurotransmitter is Alcohol an agonist?","concept":"The correct answer is GABA. Gamma-aminobutyric acid (GABA) is the brain's primary inhibitory neurotransmitter; when it binds the GABA-A receptor it opens a chloride channel that hyperpolarizes the neuron, reducing its likelihood of firing and thereby dampening neural activity. Ethanol acts as a positive modulator, functionally an agonist, at the GABA-A receptor: it enhances GABA's inhibitory effect, potentiating chloride influx and increasing central nervous system inhibition. This is the mechanism behind alcohol's characteristic sedative, anxiolytic, muscle-relaxant, and coordination-impairing effects, and it is the same receptor system targeted by benzodiazepines and barbiturates, which is why these drugs are cross-tolerant and dangerous when combined with alcohol. Alcohol simultaneously antagonizes the excitatory glutamate NMDA receptor, adding to its depressant profile and contributing to memory blackouts. Chronic use produces neuroadaptation of the GABA system, so that withdrawal causes rebound hyperexcitability, tremor, and seizures. While alcohol also touches serotonin, dopamine, and endorphin systems in ways that shape its rewarding effects, its defining agonist action for exam purposes is at GABA.","traps":{"a":"Alcohol does modulate serotonin, but its primary agonist action underlying sedation is at GABA receptors.","b":"Alcohol tends to disrupt cholinergic transmission rather than act as a clear agonist there.","d":"Alcohol can indirectly raise endorphin release, but the defining pharmacological agonist effect is at the GABA-A receptor."}},{"q":"According to Erik Erikson's stages of psychosocial development, the primary crisis to be resolved in adolescence (12-18 years) is:","concept":"The answer is identity vs. role confusion, because in Erik Erikson's theory of psychosocial development, the central crisis of adolescence, roughly ages 12 to 18, is identity versus role confusion. Understand Erikson's framework as a whole, because his eight stages are examined frequently and always as a matching task between life period, crisis, and resulting virtue. Erik Erikson, a neo-Freudian ego psychologist, expanded Freud's psychosexual stages into a lifespan theory of eight psychosocial stages, each defined by a conflict between two opposing tendencies that the developing person must resolve, with successful resolution yielding a particular strength or virtue. The stages are trust versus mistrust in infancy (virtue hope), autonomy versus shame and doubt in toddlerhood (will), initiative versus guilt in the preschool years (purpose), industry versus inferiority in the school years (competence), identity versus role confusion in adolescence (fidelity), intimacy versus isolation in young adulthood (love), generativity versus stagnation in middle adulthood (care), and integrity versus despair in late life (wisdom). Adolescence is the pivotal stage in Erikson's theory. During this period the young person struggles to answer the question who am I, integrating their various roles, values, beliefs, and aspirations into a coherent, stable sense of self, or identity. Failure to do so results in role confusion, a diffuse, uncertain sense of self and direction. Erikson introduced related ideas such as the identity crisis and the psychosocial moratorium, a period of exploration before commitment. The virtue emerging from successful resolution is fidelity. So the adolescent crisis is identity versus role confusion.","traps":{"a":"Industry vs. inferiority is Erikson's crisis of the school-age years (roughly 6 to 11), concerning competence and productivity, not the adolescent identity task.","c":"Intimacy vs. isolation is the crisis of young adulthood, concerning the capacity to form committed, close relationships, which follows adolescence rather than defining it.","d":"Generativity vs. stagnation is the crisis of middle adulthood, concerning contributing to the next generation, several stages beyond adolescence."}},{"q":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy is indicated for the treatment of:","concept":"Dialectical Behaviour Therapy is indicated for borderline personality disorder, so the answer is b. DBT was developed by Marsha Linehan specifically for chronically suicidal and self harming individuals, most of whom met criteria for borderline personality disorder, a condition marked by pervasive emotional dysregulation, unstable relationships and self image, impulsivity, recurrent self harm, and intense fear of abandonment. DBT integrates cognitive behavioural change strategies with acceptance based and mindfulness practices drawn from Zen, organised around the dialectic of acceptance and change: the therapist validates the patient's experience while simultaneously pressing for behavioural change. Its structure typically includes weekly individual therapy, group skills training, telephone coaching for in the moment crises, and a therapist consultation team. The four skills modules are mindfulness, distress tolerance, emotion regulation, and interpersonal effectiveness, which directly target the deficits underlying borderline pathology. DBT has the strongest evidence base of any treatment for BPD, reducing suicide attempts, self harm, hospitalisations, and dropout. While DBT and its adaptations are now applied to other conditions involving emotion dysregulation, its original and primary indication, and the keyed answer, is borderline personality disorder. The exam discriminator is the tight historical and clinical pairing of Linehan, emotion dysregulation, self harm, and BPD that defines DBT.","traps":{"a":"Anxiety disorders are first line treated with exposure based CBT, not the comprehensive emotion regulation programme of DBT.","c":"Acute and transient psychotic disorder requires antipsychotic medication and psychosocial support, not DBT skills training.","d":"Post traumatic stress disorder is primarily treated with trauma focused CBT, prolonged exposure, or EMDR (Francine Shapiro), not DBT as the indicated treatment."}},{"q":"Delusion of infidelity on part of the sexual partner is known as:","concept":"The correct answer is Othello syndrome. Othello syndrome is the eponymous name for delusional jealousy, the fixed, false belief that one's sexual partner is being unfaithful, held with delusional conviction despite no real evidence. It is named after Shakespeare's character Othello, who, convinced by manipulation that his faithful wife Desdemona has betrayed him, ultimately kills her. Clinically, the delusion of infidelity can occur as a primary delusional disorder (jealous type), or secondary to schizophrenia, mood disorders, organic brain disease, or, importantly, chronic alcohol misuse, where it is a recognised complication. The condition is significant in forensic psychiatry because the intense, controlling jealousy carries a real risk of violence toward the partner, who may be stalked, interrogated, and physically harmed. The patient typically seeks proof, checks the partner's belongings, and misinterprets innocent behaviour as evidence of betrayal. Recognising delusional jealousy as a psychiatric symptom rather than ordinary suspicion is clinically crucial. This question tests knowledge of the eponymous syndromes that cluster around delusional disorders, each tied to a specific delusional theme.","traps":{"a":"Ekbom's syndrome is delusional parasitosis, the fixed belief that one is infested with insects or parasites, not jealousy.","c":"De Clerambault's syndrome (erotomania) is the delusion that another person, often of higher status, is secretly in love with the patient, the opposite theme.","d":"Couvade syndrome is when an expectant father develops pregnancy-like physical symptoms, unrelated to jealousy."}},{"q":"What would be the degrees of freedom for chi-square based on a 4 x 5 contingency table?","concept":"For a chi-square test on a contingency table the degrees of freedom equal (rows minus one) multiplied by (columns minus one), so for a 4 by 5 table the calculation is (4 minus 1) times (5 minus 1), which is 3 times 4, equalling 12, making c correct. To understand why, recall what degrees of freedom mean: they represent the number of values in a calculation that are free to vary once certain constraints are imposed. In a contingency table the row totals and column totals (the marginal totals) are treated as fixed. Given those fixed marginals, once you know all but the last cell in each row and each column, the remaining cells are determined automatically, because each row and each column must sum to its known marginal. This is why one degree of freedom is subtracted from both the number of rows and the number of columns. The chi-square test of independence itself compares observed frequencies in each cell against the frequencies expected if the two categorical variables were independent, summing the squared differences divided by expected counts. The degrees of freedom then determine which chi-square distribution to compare the statistic against in order to find the critical value and significance. Getting the degrees of freedom right is essential because using the wrong value leads to an incorrect critical threshold and a wrong conclusion about statistical significance.","traps":{"a":"Five would only be correct if you mistakenly used the larger dimension minus nothing or confused degrees of freedom with the number of columns; it ignores the (r-1)(c-1) formula entirely.","b":"Nine is the product of the raw dimensions minus an incorrect single adjustment, or 3 times 3; it would be right only for a 4 by 4 table, not a 4 by 5.","d":"Twenty is simply the total number of cells (4 times 5) with no subtraction for the fixed marginals, which overstates the degrees of freedom."}},{"q":"The James-Lange or body reaction theory of emotion says:","concept":"The correct answer is that you react with your body first, then you feel emotion. The James-Lange theory of emotion, proposed independently by William James and Carl Lange in the late 19th century, reverses the commonsense view of emotion. Rather than an event causing an emotion that then produces a bodily reaction, James and Lange argued that a stimulus first triggers a physiological or bodily response (racing heart, trembling, sweating), and our perception or interpretation of that bodily change is the emotion. In James's famous formulation, we do not tremble because we are afraid; we are afraid because we tremble. Thus the bodily reaction comes first and the felt emotion follows from perceiving it. Option a describes the opposite, commonsense sequence that the theory explicitly rejects. Option d, that emotion and visceral reaction occur simultaneously, describes the rival Cannon-Bard theory of Walter Cannon and Philip Bard, which held that the thalamus sends signals producing the emotional experience and the bodily response at the same time. Option c misstates the theory by invoking the somatic rather than the visceral, autonomic responses James-Lange emphasised. Understanding James-Lange matters because it launched the scientific debate over whether physiology drives emotion, a question later extended by the Schachter-Singer two-factor theory adding cognitive appraisal.","traps":{"a":"Feeling the emotion first and then the bodily response is the commonsense view that James-Lange specifically reverses.","c":"The James-Lange theory emphasises visceral (autonomic) bodily reactions, not the somatic nervous system as the seat of emotion.","d":"Simultaneous emotion and visceral reaction describes the Cannon-Bard theory, not James-Lange."}},{"q":"A test developer administers a new anxiety scale to a group of college students and, two weeks later, administers the same scale again to the same group. She then correlates the two sets of scores. This process is used to establish:","concept":"The answer is test-retest reliability, because the defining operation described (same test, same people, two separated time points, correlate the scores) is exactly how test-retest reliability is computed. Understand reliability from the ground up: reliability means consistency of measurement, and psychometricians estimate it in several ways, each capturing a different source of error. Test-retest reliability captures stability over time. You administer the instrument, wait an interval (often two weeks, long enough to prevent literal memory of answers but short enough that the trait itself has not truly changed), administer it again, and correlate the two score sets; the resulting coefficient, called the coefficient of stability, tells you how much of the score reflects an enduring trait rather than momentary fluctuation. The chosen interval matters: too short and carryover memory inflates the correlation, too long and real change deflates it. Distinguish this from the other reliability types the exam pairs against it. Split-half and coefficient alpha assess internal consistency, whether items measure the same construct, from a single sitting. Parallel or alternate forms reliability uses two equivalent versions to check both stability and content equivalence. Inter-rater reliability checks agreement between judges. The signature clue for test-retest is always the mention of the same instrument given twice with a time gap, which is precisely this stem, so test-retest, the coefficient of stability, is correct.","traps":{"a":"Split-half reliability is estimated from a single administration by dividing the test into two halves and correlating them, so it needs only one testing session, not two separated by two weeks.","b":"Inter-rater reliability measures agreement between different observers or scorers rating the same responses, which is irrelevant to re-administering the same scale to the same people over time.","d":"Parallel forms reliability correlates two different but equivalent versions of a test, whereas this scenario uses the identical scale both times, not an alternate form."}},{"q":"According to Social identity theory, from what do we derive a large part of our identity and feelings about ourselves?","concept":"The correct answer is the groups we belong to. Social identity theory was developed by Henri Tajfel and John Turner in the 1970s and 1980s to explain intergroup behaviour. Its central claim is that a significant part of our self-concept, our identity and self-esteem, comes from our membership in social groups (the in-groups) such as our nationality, religion, team, or profession. We categorise ourselves and others into groups, identify with our in-groups, and compare our groups favourably against out-groups to boost self-esteem, a process that helps explain in-group favouritism, prejudice, and discrimination. Option d, competition between groups, describes a consequence of this process rather than the source of identity itself; Tajfel's famous minimal group experiments showed that even arbitrary group assignments trigger favouritism, so competition follows from identification rather than being its origin. Place of birth (option a) is merely a demographic fact, and experienced prejudice (option b) influences but does not define the mechanism. Understanding social identity theory matters because it illuminates why people cling to group loyalties, how collective identity shapes self-worth, and the psychological roots of stereotyping and intergroup conflict, all central to social and clinical psychology.","traps":{"a":"Place of birth is a demographic fact, not the source of identity emphasised by social identity theory.","b":"Experienced prejudice can affect self-concept, but it is not the core mechanism the theory identifies for deriving identity.","d":"Intergroup competition is a consequence and driver of group behaviour within the theory, not the source from which identity is derived."}},{"q":"Which of the following shows the stages in the correct developmental order?","concept":"The correct answer is oral, anal, phallic, latency, genital. Sigmund Freud's theory of psychosexual development proposes that personality develops through five stages, each defined by the erogenous zone that is the focus of the child's libidinal energy, and progressing in a fixed sequence from infancy to adulthood. The oral stage (birth to about 18 months) centres on the mouth, with pleasure from sucking and feeding. The anal stage (about 18 months to 3 years) centres on bowel and bladder control, coinciding with toilet training. The phallic stage (about 3 to 6 years) centres on the genitals and is when the Oedipus and Electra complexes arise and identification with the same-sex parent occurs. The latency stage (about 6 years to puberty) is a period of dormant sexual interest, during which the child focuses energy on school, friendships, and skill-building. Finally, the genital stage (puberty onward) sees the re-emergence of sexual interest, now directed toward mature adult relationships. Only option a lists these in the correct order; the other options scramble the sequence. According to Freud, fixation at any stage due to over- or under-gratification leaves a lasting mark on adult personality. Although the theory is scientifically contested, knowing the correct order of the psychosexual stages is a staple of exams and remains central to understanding Freud's developmental framework.","traps":{"b":"This ordering starts with latency and scrambles the sequence, which is incorrect.","c":"This reverses the early stages (phallic before anal before oral), which is wrong.","d":"This places genital far too early and ends with anal, disrupting the correct sequence."}},{"q":"Which part of the brainstem is responsible for controlling vital functions such as breathing and heart rate?","concept":"The correct answer is Medulla oblongata. The medulla oblongata is the lowest part of the brainstem, continuous with the spinal cord, and it houses the autonomic centres that control the body's most vital, involuntary functions, including the cardiac (heart rate) centre, the respiratory (breathing) centre, and centres for vasomotor tone, swallowing, coughing, and vomiting. Because these functions are essential to life, damage to the medulla is frequently fatal, which underscores why it is described as controlling vital functions, making option C correct. The midbrain (option A), the uppermost brainstem region, contains structures like the superior and inferior colliculi that mediate visual and auditory reflexes and relay motor and sensory signals, but it does not primarily regulate heartbeat and respiration. The pons (option B), lying between the midbrain and medulla, serves as a bridge relaying information between the cerebrum and cerebellum and contributes to regulating the rhythm of breathing, yet the fundamental control centres for heart rate and respiration reside in the medulla, not the pons. The thalamus (option D) is not part of the brainstem at all; it sits in the diencephalon and acts as the brain's main relay station for sensory information heading to the cortex. Knowing the medulla governs vital autonomic functions is fundamental to neuroanatomy. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)","traps":{"a":"The midbrain handles reflexes for vision and hearing and relays motor and sensory signals, not primary control of breathing and heart rate.","b":"The pons relays signals between brain regions and helps regulate breathing rhythm, but the core vital centres for heart rate and respiration are in the medulla.","d":"The thalamus is not part of the brainstem; it is a sensory relay station in the diencephalon."}},{"q":"Which of the following is NOT one of Kohlberg's stages of moral reasoning?","concept":"The correct answer is unconventional, because this is NOT one of Lawrence Kohlberg's levels of moral reasoning. Kohlberg, building on the earlier work of Jean Piaget, proposed a stage theory of moral development organised into three levels, each containing two stages, giving six stages total. The first level is Pre-conventional morality, where moral judgments are based on external consequences: Stage 1 is obedience and punishment orientation, and Stage 2 is instrumental exchange or self-interest ('what is in it for me'). The second level is Conventional morality, where judgments are based on social approval and maintaining order: Stage 3 is good interpersonal relationships or the 'good boy, good girl' orientation, and Stage 4 is authority and social-order maintenance ('law and order'). The third level is Post-conventional morality, where judgments rest on abstract ethical principles: Stage 5 is social contract and individual rights, and Stage 6 is universal ethical principles. Kohlberg derived these from responses to moral dilemmas such as the Heinz dilemma. There is no level called 'unconventional' in his framework, making option B the odd one out. Carol Gilligan later critiqued the theory for a possible male bias. This item tests recall of the three correctly named levels.","traps":{"a":"Conventional is a genuine level of Kohlberg's theory (Level 2), so it is not the false option.","c":"Post-conventional is Kohlberg's highest level (Level 3), based on abstract principles and justice, so it is a real stage.","d":"Pre-conventional is Kohlberg's first level (Level 1), based on obedience and self-interest, so it is a real stage."}},{"q":"Exposure & response prevention is the most effective treatment of:","concept":"Exposure and response prevention is the most effective treatment of obsessive compulsive disorder, so the answer is a. OCD is characterised by intrusive, distressing obsessions and by compulsions, repetitive behaviours or mental acts performed to neutralise the anxiety the obsessions provoke. The behavioural model explains the disorder through negative reinforcement: the compulsion temporarily relieves the anxiety, which reinforces the ritual and prevents the person from ever learning that the feared catastrophe would not happen anyway. Exposure and response prevention (ERP), developed by Victor Meyer and refined by Edna Foa and others, directly breaks this cycle. The exposure component has the patient deliberately confront the obsessional triggers, the contaminated doorknob, the unlocked door, the intrusive blasphemous thought, while the response prevention component has them refrain from performing the compulsive ritual. By staying in contact with the trigger without ritualising, the patient experiences that anxiety habituates and declines on its own and that the feared outcome does not occur, producing extinction of the obsessional fear and the compulsive behaviour. ERP has the strongest evidence base of any psychological treatment for OCD and is the first line behavioural intervention. The exam discriminator is the tight, specific match between ERP's mechanism, confronting triggers while blocking rituals, and the obsession compulsion cycle of OCD, rather than the other anxiety conditions listed.","traps":{"b":"Social anxiety disorder is best treated with exposure plus cognitive restructuring within CBT; ERP's ritual prevention focus is specific to OCD compulsions.","c":"Claustrophobia, a specific phobia, responds to graded exposure or systematic desensitization (Joseph Wolpe, 1958), not specifically to response prevention of rituals.","d":"Panic disorder is treated with interoceptive exposure and cognitive therapy targeting catastrophic misinterpretation, not ERP for compulsions."}},{"q":"Panic disorder is where:","concept":"The correct answer is that there are recurrent and unexpected panic attacks. Panic disorder is an anxiety disorder defined by recurrent, unexpected panic attacks, which are sudden surges of intense fear or discomfort that peak within minutes and are accompanied by physical and cognitive symptoms such as palpitations, sweating, trembling, shortness of breath, chest pain, dizziness, and a fear of dying or losing control. Critically, the attacks come 'out of the blue' without an obvious trigger, and the diagnosis further requires that at least one attack be followed by a month or more of persistent worry about having more attacks or a maladaptive change in behaviour to avoid them (anticipatory anxiety). A single attack (option A) is insufficient. This links to the cognitive model of panic by David M. Clark, which holds that panic arises from catastrophic misinterpretation of benign bodily sensations, and to interoceptive conditioning accounts. The distractors describe other conditions: excessive, uncontrollable worry across many life domains is generalised anxiety disorder, and fear of enclosed spaces is a specific phobia or relates to agoraphobia. Panic disorder frequently co-occurs with agoraphobia (fear of situations where escape is difficult). This item tests the defining feature of recurrent unexpected panic attacks.","traps":{"a":"A single, isolated panic attack does not meet the criteria for panic disorder, which requires recurrent attacks plus persistent concern about them.","b":"Excessive worry about many aspects of life describes generalised anxiety disorder, not panic disorder.","c":"Fear tied specifically to enclosed spaces describes a form of agoraphobia or claustrophobia, not the core definition of panic disorder."}},{"q":"When comparing two independent samples and the data does not meet the assumptions for parametric testing, which non-parametric test is most commonly used?","concept":"The answer is the Mann-Whitney U test because it is the standard non-parametric alternative to the independent-samples t-test, used when you have two separate, unrelated groups and the data violate parametric assumptions (non-normal, ordinal, or very small samples). It was developed by Henry Mann and Donald Whitney in 1947, building on earlier work by Frank Wilcoxon, and it is mathematically equivalent to the Wilcoxon rank-sum test. Instead of comparing means, it pools all the scores from both groups, ranks them from lowest to highest, and then asks whether the ranks tend to cluster higher in one group than the other, which tells you whether the two distributions differ. Because it works on ranks rather than raw values, it makes no assumption about the shape of the population distribution. The crucial exam skill is matching the test to the design along two dimensions: how many groups, and whether they are independent or related. Two independent groups without normality gives Mann-Whitney U; two related or paired groups gives Wilcoxon signed-rank; three or more independent groups gives Kruskal-Wallis; three or more related conditions gives Friedman. Keeping that two-by-two grid in mind lets you eliminate the distractors instantly.","traps":{"a":"The Wilcoxon signed-rank test is for two related or paired samples (such as before-and-after measures on the same people), not for two independent groups.","c":"The Kruskal-Wallis test extends the logic to three or more independent groups, so it is the wrong choice for exactly two groups.","d":"The Friedman test is the non-parametric option for repeated measures across three or more conditions on the same subjects, not for two independent samples."}},{"q":"Which stage is characterised by realising your full potential according to Maslow's Hierarchy of Needs?","concept":"The correct answer is self-actualisation needs. Abraham Maslow proposed a hierarchy of needs, usually depicted as a pyramid with five levels arranged from most basic to most advanced: physiological needs (food, water, sleep), safety needs (security, stability), love and belonging (social) needs, esteem needs (respect, achievement, recognition), and at the very top, self-actualisation. Maslow argued that lower needs must be reasonably satisfied before higher ones become motivating. Self-actualisation, the apex of the hierarchy, is the drive to realise one's full potential, to become everything one is capable of becoming, and to pursue personal growth, creativity, and meaning. Maslow described self-actualised individuals as autonomous, accepting, spontaneous, problem-centred, and capable of peak experiences, and he studied exemplary figures such as Abraham Lincoln and Albert Einstein as models. Later he added self-transcendence above self-actualisation, but the classic five-level model tops out at self-actualisation. The distractors are all genuine but lower levels of the hierarchy that concern security, belonging, and status rather than fulfilment of potential. This item tests recall of the top tier of Maslow's humanistic motivational model and the definition of self-actualisation.","traps":{"a":"Safety needs (security, stability, protection) sit near the base of the pyramid and concern basic survival, not fulfilment of one's highest potential.","b":"Social needs (belonging, love, friendship) are mid-level needs about relationships, not the peak of realising one's potential.","c":"Esteem needs (respect, recognition, self-worth) lie just below the top; they concern status and confidence, not the full realisation of potential that defines the apex."}},{"q":"Face validity refers to which of the following:","concept":"The correct answer is the notion that an assessment method may appear to be valid simply because it has questions which intuitively seem relevant to the trait being measured. Validity refers to whether a test actually measures what it claims to measure, and it comes in several types. Face validity is the most superficial: it concerns whether a test looks or appears, on its surface and to a layperson or test-taker, as though it measures the intended construct. A test has high face validity if its items obviously relate to the trait (for example, a depression questionnaire that asks about sadness and hopelessness). Importantly, face validity is not a statistical or scientific form of validity; a test can appear valid on its face yet fail to measure the construct accurately, and conversely a test can lack face validity yet be genuinely valid. Face validity matters mainly for practical acceptability and motivation, since tests that look relevant are more readily accepted by respondents, but sometimes low face validity is deliberately used (as in some personality inventories) to reduce faking. This contrasts with content validity, criterion validity, and construct validity (Lee Cronbach and Paul Meehl, 1955), which are established through evidence. Option D actually defines a construct, which pertains to construct validity. This item tests the definition of face validity as mere surface appearance of relevance.","traps":{"a":"Using facial expression to diagnose is a play on the word 'face' and has nothing to do with the psychometric concept of face validity.","b":"The scale of emotional responding is unrelated to face validity, which concerns how valid a test appears on its surface.","d":"This describes the definition of a construct, relevant to construct validity, not the surface-appearance meaning of face validity."}},{"q":"Solomon Asch’s classic experiment was arranged to test the limits of:","concept":"The correct answer is conformity. Solomon Asch's classic experiments of the 1950s were designed to test the limits of conformity, specifically how far social pressure from a group could lead an individual to give an answer they knew to be wrong. In the basic procedure, a participant was seated in a group of confederates (people secretly working with the experimenter) and shown a series of simple perceptual judgements: a standard line and three comparison lines, where the task was merely to say which comparison line matched the standard in length. The correct answer was obvious. However, on certain trials the confederates, answering first, all unanimously gave the same wrong answer. Asch found that about a third of the participants' responses (across critical trials) conformed to the group's incorrect judgement, and about 75 percent of participants conformed at least once, even though the correct answer was plainly visible. This demonstrated the power of normative social influence, the desire to fit in and avoid the discomfort of being the odd one out, even when the group is clearly wrong. Asch also showed that conformity dropped sharply when there was just one dissenting ally, when responses were private, or when the group was smaller. The study is a cornerstone of social psychology on conformity.","traps":{"a":"Social perception concerns how we form impressions and make inferences about other people, not the yielding to group pressure that Asch studied.","c":"Coercive power is influence based on the ability to punish; Asch's groups applied no punishment, only the implicit pressure of unanimous opinion.","d":"Leadership concerns guiding and influencing a group toward goals, not the individual conforming to a group's judgement, which is what Asch tested."}},{"q":"Select the incorrect pair:","concept":"The correct answer is Kohlberg: Formal operations, because this is the mismatched (incorrect) pair. Formal operations is the fourth and final stage of cognitive development in the theory of Jean Piaget, characterised by abstract, hypothetical, and logical reasoning that emerges in adolescence; it has nothing to do with Lawrence Kohlberg, who is famous instead for his theory of moral development (pre-conventional, conventional, and post-conventional levels). Kohlberg built on Piaget's earlier work on moral reasoning but is not associated with the formal operations stage, so pairing his name with it is wrong. The other three pairs are all correctly matched: Carl Gustav Jung introduced the personality attitudes of extraversion and introversion; Alfred Adler, founder of individual psychology, described personality types including the ruling or dominant type (alongside getting, avoiding, and socially useful types); and Albert Ellis developed Rational Emotive Therapy, later renamed Rational Emotive Behaviour Therapy (REBT), the pioneering cognitive therapy built on the ABC model. This item tests the student's ability to correctly attribute major theories and concepts to their originators, a frequent format in entrance exams.","traps":{"a":"This pair is correct: Carl Jung introduced the extraversion-introversion dimension of personality, so it is not the incorrect pair the question seeks.","c":"This pair is correct: Alfred Adler described personality types including the ruling or dominant type, so it is properly matched.","d":"This pair is correct: Albert Ellis founded Rational Emotive Therapy (later REBT), so it is accurately paired."}},{"q":"Parkinson's disease is associated with the degeneration of neurons that produce which neurotransmitter?","concept":"The correct answer is Dopamine. Parkinson's disease is a progressive neurodegenerative disorder caused primarily by the death of dopamine-producing neurons in a midbrain region called the substantia nigra, part of the basal ganglia circuitry that controls movement. As these dopaminergic neurons degenerate, dopamine levels in the striatum fall, disrupting the fine regulation of voluntary movement and producing the classic motor symptoms: resting tremor, rigidity, bradykinesia (slowness of movement), and postural instability. This dopamine deficit is why the standard treatment is levodopa, a dopamine precursor that the brain converts into dopamine. Hence option B is correct. Serotonin (option A) is chiefly involved in mood, sleep, and appetite, and its dysregulation is linked to depression and anxiety rather than to Parkinsonian motor decline. Acetylcholine (option C) is essential for muscle activation and for memory, and its loss in the basal forebrain is a feature of Alzheimer's disease, not the defining lesion of Parkinson's, though a dopamine-acetylcholine imbalance does contribute to Parkinson's symptoms. GABA (option D) is the brain's main inhibitory neurotransmitter, implicated in anxiety and epilepsy, not in the dopaminergic degeneration that defines Parkinson's. Understanding the dopamine link is central to the neurochemistry of movement disorders and their pharmacological treatment. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)","traps":{"a":"Serotonin is linked to mood regulation and depression, not to the motor degeneration of Parkinson's disease.","c":"Acetylcholine is depleted in Alzheimer's disease and drives muscle contraction, but Parkinson's centres on dopamine loss.","d":"GABA is the main inhibitory neurotransmitter and is implicated in anxiety and seizures, not in the dopaminergic loss of Parkinson's."}},{"q":"Which of the following is a key characteristic of the \"slow-to-warm-up\" temperament category in the Thomas and Chess model?","concept":"The answer is negative withdrawal from new stimuli, slow adaptability, and mild intensity of reactions, because that combination is the defining profile of the slow-to-warm-up child in the Thomas and Chess temperament model. Understand the model and this category in particular. Alexander Thomas and Stella Chess, in the New York Longitudinal Study, argued that infants display biologically based behavioral styles, or temperaments, from early life, and they rated babies on nine dimensions including activity level, rhythmicity, approach or withdrawal to novelty, adaptability, intensity of reaction, threshold of responsiveness, quality of mood, distractibility, and attention span. Clustering these dimensions, they identified three main temperament types plus an unclassified group. The easy child (about 40 percent) has regular biological rhythms, approaches new situations positively, adapts readily, and shows mild to moderate, mostly positive mood. The difficult child (about 10 percent) has irregular rhythms, withdraws from novelty, adapts slowly, reacts with high intensity, and shows frequent negative mood. The slow-to-warm-up child (about 15 percent) sits between these extremes and is defined by a specific signature: an initial cautious withdrawal from or negative reaction to new stimuli and people, slow adaptation that gradually warms up with repeated, unpressured exposure, and crucially, low or mild intensity of emotional reactions rather than the stormy, high-intensity reactions of the difficult child. Such children are not intensely distressed; they are simply shy and slow to engage, needing patient, gradual exposure. The key contrast with the difficult child is intensity: both may initially withdraw, but the slow-to-warm-up child reacts mildly. So the slow-to-warm-up profile is withdrawal from novelty, slow adaptability, and mild-intensity reactions.","traps":{"a":"A positive approach to new stimuli with high regularity of biological functions describes the easy temperament, not the slow-to-warm-up child who withdraws from novelty.","c":"Intense, frequent negative reactions with irregular routines describes the difficult temperament, whose reactions are high in intensity, unlike the mild-intensity reactions of the slow-to-warm-up child.","d":"A mix of easy and difficult characteristics with no clear pattern describes the unclassified or average children in the model, not the specific slow-to-warm-up category defined by cautious withdrawal and mild intensity."}},{"q":"A client engages in safety behaviors, such as gripping a shopping cart tightly when feeling dizzy in a store. In CBT for panic disorder, the therapist would likely:","concept":"The answer is to work with the client to gradually drop these safety behaviors in order to test catastrophic beliefs, because in cognitive-behavioral therapy for panic disorder, safety behaviors are understood as a key factor maintaining the disorder, and eliminating them is essential to disconfirming the client's catastrophic misinterpretations. Understand the cognitive model of panic, developed by David M. Clark, which is the theoretical backbone here. Clark proposed that panic attacks arise from the catastrophic misinterpretation of benign bodily sensations. A person notices a normal or stress-induced sensation, such as a racing heart, dizziness, or breathlessness, and misinterprets it as a sign of imminent catastrophe, such as I am having a heart attack, I am going to faint, or I am losing my mind. This catastrophic thought increases anxiety, which intensifies the bodily sensations, creating a vicious cycle that spirals into a full panic attack. Safety behaviors are the subtle actions people take to prevent the feared catastrophe, such as gripping a shopping cart to avoid fainting, sitting down, or carrying water. The problem is that when the catastrophe does not occur, the person attributes the non-event to the safety behavior (I did not faint because I held on) rather than realizing the catastrophe was never going to happen. Thus safety behaviors prevent the disconfirmation of the catastrophic belief and keep the disorder alive. The therapeutic solution is a behavioral experiment: the therapist helps the client deliberately drop the safety behavior while experiencing the sensation, so they learn that the feared outcome does not occur even without the crutch. So the correct action is dropping safety behaviors to test catastrophic beliefs.","traps":{"a":"Prescribing safety behaviors to prevent fainting would reinforce the client's catastrophic belief that they are in danger, the opposite of the corrective approach in CBT for panic; moreover, panic-related dizziness does not actually cause fainting.","b":"Using safety behaviors as a measure of insight misunderstands their role; in CBT they are targets to be eliminated because they maintain the disorder, not indicators to be passively assessed.","d":"Exploring the childhood origins of the need for safety is a psychodynamic approach; CBT for panic focuses on present-day maintaining factors and behavioral experiments rather than childhood exploration."}},{"q":"In view of most psychodynamic theorists, dissociative symptoms are produced by which of the following?","concept":"The correct answer is repression. In classical psychodynamic theory, first articulated by Sigmund Freud with Josef Breuer in their work on hysteria, dissociative symptoms such as amnesia, fugue, and depersonalization arise when intolerable memories, affects, or impulses are actively pushed out of conscious awareness. Repression is the primary defense mechanism: the ego banishes threatening material to the unconscious to protect itself from anxiety. Dissociation is understood as an extreme, splitting-off variant of this process, where whole segments of experience or identity become inaccessible. Pierre Janet, a contemporary of Freud, framed dissociation slightly differently as a failure of integration (desagregation), but the question asks specifically about most psychodynamic theorists, who root the phenomenon in repression. The concept matters clinically because it explains why dissociative amnesia typically covers traumatic content and can, in theory, be recovered when the repressive barrier is lifted. Conflict is the broader engine behind all defenses, but the specific mechanism producing dissociative symptoms is repression itself.","traps":{"b":"Ego state theory is a later structural elaboration, not the core psychodynamic explanation for dissociation itself.","c":"Conflict drives neurotic symptoms broadly, but psychodynamic theory specifies repression as the mechanism that pushes material out of awareness in dissociation.","d":"Gender identity has no role in the classical psychodynamic account of dissociation."}},{"q":"In statistical hypothesis testing, rejecting a true null hypothesis represents which type of error?","concept":"The answer is a Type I error because that is defined precisely as rejecting a null hypothesis that is in fact true, which amounts to a false positive: concluding that an effect or difference exists when in reality it does not. This framework of two error types comes from the foundational work of Jerzy Neyman and Egon Pearson on hypothesis testing. The null hypothesis states that there is no effect, no difference, or no relationship, and the researcher decides whether to reject it based on the data. Two mistakes are possible. A Type I error (also called an alpha error) occurs when the null is true but we wrongly reject it, for example concluding a drug works when it actually does nothing; its probability is the significance level, alpha, which researchers set in advance, commonly at 0.05, meaning a 5 percent risk of a false positive. A Type II error (a beta error) is the reverse: the null is actually false but we fail to reject it, a false negative, such as missing a drug's real effect; its probability is beta, and 1 minus beta is the test's statistical power. A memory aid: Type I is a false alarm (crying wolf when there is none), while Type II is a miss (failing to sound the alarm when the wolf is real). Since the question describes rejecting a true null, the answer is a Type I error.","traps":{"b":"A Type II error is failing to reject a null hypothesis that is actually false (a false negative), the opposite of the situation described here.","c":"Sampling error is the natural random variation between a sample statistic and the true population value, not a decision error in hypothesis testing.","d":"Standard error is a measure of the variability of a sample statistic across samples, not a type of hypothesis-testing error at all."}},{"q":"A network of associated facts and concepts that make up our knowledge of the world is referred to as:","concept":"A network of associated facts and concepts that constitute our general knowledge of the world is semantic memory, so d is correct. This question rests on the distinction, introduced by Endel Tulving, between two kinds of long-term declarative (explicit) memory. Semantic memory is the store of general facts, meanings, concepts, vocabulary, and knowledge about the world that is not tied to any particular time or place in which it was learned. Knowing that Paris is the capital of France, that a triangle has three sides, or what the word democracy means are all semantic memories; you typically cannot recall when or where you first acquired them. Episodic memory, by contrast, is memory for specific personally experienced events, bound to a particular context of time and place, such as remembering your last birthday party or what you ate this morning. Both semantic and episodic memory are subtypes of explicit (declarative) memory, which is consciously accessible and can be verbally described. This is contrasted with implicit (non-declarative) memory, which includes skills, habits, and conditioned responses that influence behaviour without conscious recall, such as riding a bicycle. The phrase network of associated facts and concepts strongly signals the organised, web-like structure of conceptual knowledge that defines semantic memory specifically, rather than the broader categories or the event-based alternative.","traps":{"a":"Episodic memory stores specific, time-and-place-tagged personal experiences, not a general network of facts and concepts about the world.","b":"Explicit memory is the broad umbrella category for consciously accessible memory that contains both semantic and episodic memory, so it is too general to be the precise answer for a factual knowledge network.","c":"Implicit memory (Peter Graf and Daniel Schacter, 1985) covers unconscious skills, habits, and conditioned responses, which are non-declarative and entirely unlike a conscious network of facts."}},{"q":"Trail Making Test helps to assess ______ in children with ADHD.","concept":"The Trail Making Test helps assess inattention in children with ADHD, so the answer is a. The Trail Making Test has two parts. Part A requires the examinee to connect numbered circles in ascending order as quickly as possible, and Part B requires alternating between numbers and letters in sequence (1, A, 2, B, and so on). Part A primarily taps visual scanning, sustained attention, visuomotor speed, and the ability to maintain focus on a target sequence, while Part B adds set shifting and cognitive flexibility, an executive demand. In ADHD, the component the test is best suited to capture among the options given is inattention: completing the trails requires sustained, focused attention and continuous visual search, and children with the inattentive features of ADHD show slowed, error prone performance reflecting lapses of attention and difficulty maintaining the mental set. Although ADHD also involves impulsivity and hyperactivity, those are better captured by other measures such as continuous performance tests for impulsivity or behavioural observation for overactivity. The Trail Making Test specifically indexes the attentional and processing speed dimension, mapping onto the inattentive symptom cluster. The exam discriminator is matching the Trail Making Test's core demand, sustained attention and visual tracking, to inattention rather than to the motoric or impulsive symptoms of ADHD.","traps":{"b":"Social skills are interpersonal abilities assessed by observation or rating scales, not by a paper and pencil attention and sequencing task.","c":"Impulsivity is better measured by continuous performance tests with commission errors; the Trail Making Test chiefly indexes attention and speed.","d":"Over activity is the motoric hyperactivity symptom, observed behaviourally, not what a sustained attention tracking task primarily measures."}},{"q":"When people are able to think systematically about an issue and focus on arguments, they are likely to take the ______ route to persuasion.","concept":"When people think systematically about an issue and focus on arguments, they take the central route to persuasion, so the answer is d. This is the core of the Elaboration Likelihood Model developed by Richard Petty and John Cacioppo, which proposes two routes by which attitudes are changed. The central route operates when people are both motivated and able to think carefully about a message; they elaborate on it, scrutinise the strength and quality of the arguments, and are persuaded by the merits of the case. Attitude change produced this way is more enduring, more resistant to counterargument, and more predictive of behaviour, because it is grounded in genuine cognitive engagement. The peripheral route operates when motivation or ability to process is low; instead of analysing arguments, people rely on superficial cues such as the attractiveness or credibility of the source, the sheer number of arguments, catchy slogans, or emotional reactions. Persuasion via the peripheral route is weaker and more transient. The determining factors for which route is taken are the elaboration likelihood, chiefly the person's motivation (for example personal relevance) and cognitive capacity (for example freedom from distraction). The question specifies thinking systematically and focusing on arguments, which is the hallmark of the central route. The exam discriminator is matching effortful argument scrutiny to the central route as opposed to cue based peripheral processing.","traps":{"a":"The peripheral route relies on superficial cues like source attractiveness rather than systematic argument analysis, the opposite of what is described.","b":"Emotional is not one of the two named routes in the Elaboration Likelihood Model; emotional cues belong to peripheral processing.","c":"Situational is not a route in the model; the two recognised routes are central and peripheral."}},{"q":"According to Adler, middle children tend to be :","concept":"The correct answer is competitive. Alfred Adler, founder of Individual Psychology, argued that birth order shapes personality through the child's subjective experience of their family position. He described the middle (second-born) child as growing up in a race, always with an older sibling ahead to catch up to and often a younger sibling behind. This creates a characteristically competitive, ambitious striving: the middle child is spurred to overtake the pacesetter older sibling and frequently becomes achievement-oriented and even rebellious against the perceived unfairness of never being first. Adler saw this as generally healthy, and he considered the second-born often the best adjusted of the sibships. He contrasted this with the firstborn, who feels dethroned by the arrival of a sibling and may become conservative and power-oriented, the youngest or pampered child, who may remain dependent and prone to inferiority feelings, and the only child, who may struggle to share. Adler is also the originator of the inferiority complex concept. The specific hallmark of the middle child in his framework is competitiveness driven by the pace-setting sibling ahead.","traps":{"a":"Overachievement is more typical of the firstborn striving to regain lost status in Adler's scheme.","c":"Resentment of the older child's freedom is not the trait Adler emphasized for the middle child.","d":"Pervasive inferiority feelings are linked to the pampered or the youngest child, not the driving middle child."}},{"q":"The ‘satiety centre’ of the brain is located in the:","concept":"The correct answer is the ventromedial hypothalamus. The hypothalamus is the key brain region regulating hunger and eating, and two of its nuclei play opposing roles that form a classic dual-centre model (Eliot Stellar, 1954). The ventromedial hypothalamus (VMH) functions as the satiety centre: when stimulated, it produces a feeling of fullness and stops eating, and crucially, when the VMH is damaged or lesioned, an animal overeats dramatically and becomes obese, a syndrome called hyperphagia, because the off switch for eating is destroyed. In contrast, the lateral hypothalamus (LH) functions as the hunger or feeding centre: stimulating it triggers eating, while lesioning it causes the animal to stop eating and lose weight (aphagia), because the on switch for hunger is destroyed. So the simple mnemonic is: ventromedial equals satiety (stop eating), lateral equals hunger (start eating). These findings come from classic lesion studies and underpin the understanding of appetite regulation, which is now known to involve hormones such as leptin (signalling satiety from fat stores) and ghrelin (signalling hunger from the stomach) acting on these hypothalamic centres. The question asks for the satiety centre, which is the ventromedial hypothalamus, so option d is correct.","traps":{"a":"The lateral hypothalamus is the hunger or feeding centre that initiates eating, the opposite of the satiety centre.","b":"The ventromedial hippocampus is not the appetite centre; the hippocampus governs memory, and the satiety centre is in the hypothalamus, not the hippocampus.","c":"The lateral thalamus is a sensory relay structure, not involved in regulating satiety or hunger."}},{"q":"Human motor development is governed by certain basic principles. Which of the following is Not a basic principle of motor development?","concept":"The correct answer is that motor development proceeds from distal to proximal, because this statement is reversed and therefore NOT a true principle. The genuine principle is the proximodistal trend: development proceeds from proximal (near the centre or midline of the body, such as the trunk and shoulders) to distal (toward the extremities, such as the hands and fingers). Infants gain control of their arms and shoulders before they master fine finger movements, so option C states the direction backwards and is the false statement the question wants. The other two directional principles are stated correctly: the cephalocaudal trend means development proceeds from head to foot, so infants control their heads and upper bodies before their legs. The gross-to-fine principle means large-muscle movements (crawling, walking) develop before fine-motor skills (grasping small objects, writing). Option D, that motor development shapes social and behavioural expectations, is also a valid point about its wider significance. These maturational principles derive from work by developmental theorists including Arnold Gesell, who emphasised orderly, genetically guided maturation of motor skills. The item tests whether the student can spot the reversed proximodistal statement among otherwise accurate principles.","traps":{"a":"Gross-to-fine progression is a genuine principle: large-muscle movements develop before delicate finger control, so this is a true statement, not the exception.","b":"The cephalocaudal principle (head to feet) is a real and central principle of motor development, so it does not fit the 'not a principle' answer.","d":"That motor development influences social and behavioural expectations is a valid observation about its broader role, so it is not the false principle sought."}},{"q":"A child with autism is taught to request a \"break\" using a picture card instead of engaging in a tantrum. This intervention is a key component of:","concept":"The answer is Applied Behavior Analysis, ABA, because teaching a child with autism to request a break using a picture card in place of a tantrum is a functional communication training intervention that is a core component of ABA. Understand ABA and the specific principle at work. Applied Behavior Analysis is the systematic application of the principles of operant conditioning, derived from B. F. Skinner's work, to socially significant behavior, and it is the most empirically supported intervention framework for autism spectrum disorder. A central ABA concept is that challenging behaviors like tantrums often serve a function, meaning they are maintained by their consequences; a functional behavior assessment identifies what the behavior achieves for the child, for example escape from a demand, access to attention, or access to a tangible item. In this vignette, the tantrum likely functions to obtain a break or escape from a nonpreferred activity. Functional communication training, a well-established ABA procedure, teaches the child a socially acceptable alternative behavior that serves the same function more efficiently, here handing over or pointing to a picture card that means break. When the appropriate communicative response reliably produces the desired outcome (the break) and the tantrum no longer does, the child shifts to the functional request, and the problem behavior extinguishes. This often uses picture-based systems such as the Picture Exchange Communication System (PECS). The intervention is grounded in reinforcement, replacement behavior, and functional analysis, all hallmarks of behavior analysis, so the correct framework is Applied Behavior Analysis, not the cognitive, play, or psychoanalytic approaches.","traps":{"a":"CBT relies on the client's cognitive and verbal capacity to examine and restructure thoughts, which is not the primary mechanism when teaching a nonverbal child to swap a tantrum for a picture-card request; the technique described is behavioral, not cognitive.","c":"Play therapy is a psychodynamic or humanistic approach using play as the medium for emotional expression and relationship, not the systematic functional-communication training with picture cards described here.","d":"Psychoanalytic psychotherapy explores unconscious conflict and relies on insight and interpretation, which is neither developmentally appropriate for this task nor the framework behind teaching a functional communication response."}},{"q":"Individuals with obsessive-compulsive personality disorder are primarily motivated by a need for which of the following?","concept":"The correct answer is control. Obsessive-Compulsive Personality Disorder (OCPD), classified in Cluster C of the DSM-5 by the American Psychiatric Association, is a pervasive pattern of preoccupation with orderliness, perfectionism, and mental and interpersonal control, at the expense of flexibility, openness, and efficiency. The central motivating dynamic is a need for control: over one's own behavior, over other people, and over the environment. This manifests as rigid adherence to rules and schedules, excessive devotion to work, hoarding, reluctance to delegate unless others submit to one's exact methods, and moral inflexibility. Note that OCPD is distinct from Obsessive-Compulsive Disorder (OCD): OCPD is an ego-syntonic personality style without true obsessions and compulsions, whereas OCD, an anxiety-spectrum condition, involves ego-dystonic intrusive thoughts and ritualized behaviors. Psychodynamic writers tracing back to Sigmund Freud linked the anal-retentive character (orderliness, parsimony, obstinacy) to this control-oriented pattern. The perfectionism seen in OCPD is often self-defeating, producing inefficiency rather than the efficiency one might expect, which is exactly why control, not efficiency, is the underlying need.","traps":{"a":"OCPD individuals are often paradoxically inefficient because perfectionism interferes with task completion, so efficiency is not the core drive.","b":"Achievement motivation is broader and not the defining need; the disorder centers on orderliness and control rather than accomplishment itself.","d":"Autonomy is more characteristic of other patterns; OCPD centers on controlling self, others, and the environment through rules."}},{"q":"A researcher is studying the same group of individuals over a 10-year period. This design is called:","concept":"A longitudinal design is a research method in which the same individuals are observed and measured repeatedly over an extended period, in this case ten years, to study change and development over time. Its major strength is that, by following the same people, it can track individual growth trajectories and examine how variables change within persons, allowing stronger inferences about the sequence of developmental change; its drawbacks include high cost, long duration, and attrition (participant dropout). It contrasts most directly with the cross-sectional design, which compares different groups (often different ages) at a single point in time; cross-sectional studies are faster but cannot separate developmental change from cohort differences. A case-control design retrospectively compares individuals who have an outcome with those who do not, and an experimental design involves the deliberate manipulation of an independent variable with random assignment. Because studying the same group of individuals over a ten-year span is the defining feature of a longitudinal design, option c is correct.","traps":{"a":"A cross-sectional design studies different age groups at a single point in time, not the same group over years.","b":"A case-control design compares groups with and without an outcome retrospectively; it does not follow one group over time.","d":"An experimental design involves manipulating an independent variable, which the observational follow-up described does not require."}},{"q":"Which of the following refers to the notion of the confirmatory bias?","concept":"The correct answer is all of the above. Confirmatory bias (confirmation bias) is the pervasive tendency to seek, interpret, favour, and recall information in a way that confirms one's pre-existing beliefs, hypotheses, or expectations, while giving disproportionately little attention to information that contradicts them. The concept was demonstrated experimentally by Peter Wason, whose selection task and 2-4-6 rule-discovery task showed that people tend to test hypotheses by looking for confirming rather than disconfirming evidence. In clinical assessment and diagnosis, confirmation bias is a serious source of error: a clinician who forms an early diagnostic hypothesis may then ignore or discount symptoms and information that do not fit that hypothesis (option A), may disregard evidence that contradicts a stereotype about a patient group (option B), and may interpret genuinely ambiguous or neutral information as though it supports the favoured hypothesis (option C). All three describe real manifestations of the same underlying bias, so the inclusive answer is correct. This bias contributes to premature closure and diagnostic overshadowing, which is why clinicians are trained to actively consider alternative hypotheses and seek disconfirming evidence, and why structured assessment reduces error. This item tests recognition that confirmation bias operates through several complementary mechanisms in clinical judgment.","traps":{"a":"Ignoring disconfirming information is one manifestation of confirmation bias, but selecting it alone omits the other equally valid manifestations listed.","b":"Ignoring information that contradicts stereotypes is a form of confirmation bias, but it is only one aspect of the broader tendency.","c":"Interpreting ambiguous information as supporting one's hypothesis is a genuine feature of confirmation bias, but it too is only one part of the whole."}},{"q":"The process of recording a behaviour causes the behaviour to change, even before any treatment is implemented. This is known as:","concept":"Reactivity is the phenomenon in which the act of observing or recording a behavior changes that behavior, even before any intervention is introduced. It is a central concept in behavioral assessment and self-monitoring. When a person knows they are being watched, or when they themselves begin keeping a record of a target behavior (counting cigarettes smoked, logging snacks eaten, tallying times they bite their nails), the behavior tends to shift, usually in the socially desirable direction. Reactivity is closely tied to the famous Hawthorne effect (Hawthorne studies by Elton Mayo and colleagues, 1924-1932; term by Henry Landsberger, 1958), where workers at the Hawthorne plant improved their productivity simply because they were being studied, regardless of the actual changes made to their conditions. In clinical practice this cuts two ways. On one hand reactivity is a threat to the validity of baseline data: the recorded behavior may not represent the person's true habitual rate. On the other hand clinicians deliberately exploit reactivity, because self-monitoring can itself be therapeutic, the simple act of tracking an undesired behavior often reduces it. The key idea the question tests is that the change here arises from measurement and awareness, not from any treatment, which is precisely the definition of reactivity. The other options are all behavioral techniques for building or changing behavior, not effects of observation.","traps":{"b":"Shaping is the operant procedure of reinforcing successive approximations toward a target behavior; it is an active intervention to create behavior, not a side effect of merely recording it.","c":"Applied Behavior Analysis is the broad applied discipline that uses learning principles to change socially significant behavior; it is a whole field, not the specific effect of observation on behavior.","d":"Chaining is the technique of linking individual behaviors into a sequence by reinforcing them in order; it builds complex behavior and is unrelated to the reactivity of measurement."}},{"q":"When a person begins by making a small request that the other person is not likely to refuse is known as","concept":"This is a compliance technique from social psychology, studied experimentally by Jonathan Freedman and Scott Fraser. The foot-in-the-door technique works by first securing agreement to a trivial request that almost no one would refuse (signing a petition, wearing a small badge). Once a person has said yes, a larger request that follows is far more likely to be granted. The mechanism is best explained through Daryl Bem's self-perception theory: after agreeing to the small favour, people infer from their own behaviour that they are the kind of helpful, cooperative person who does such things, and to stay consistent with that new self-image they comply with the bigger ask. It also draws on Leon Festinger's pressure toward consistency. Notice the contrast with its opposite, the door-in-the-face (Robert Cialdini and colleagues, 1975) technique, where a large refused request is followed by a smaller one and works through reciprocal concession. The key phrase in the stem, 'begins by making a small request that the other person is not likely to refuse,' is the defining signature of foot-in-the-door. Marketers, fundraisers, and negotiators exploit this escalation from small to large commitments precisely because early agreement quietly reshapes how people see themselves, making later, costlier compliance feel natural rather than coerced.","traps":{"a":"There is no established compliance tactic called the 'deadline technique'; scarcity/deadline appeals are a different lever and not what a small-then-large request describes.","c":"The door-in-the-face technique is the mirror image: it opens with a large, likely-refused request and then retreats to the smaller real one.","d":"Conformity is yielding to group pressure to match others, not a sequential-request persuasion strategy aimed at one person."}},{"q":"Trait approaches to personality focuses on","concept":"The correct answer is: specific psychological attributes along which individuals tend to differ in consistent and stable way.. The trait tradition (Allport, Cattell, Eysenck, the Big Five) defines a trait as a specific, enduring attribute along which people differ consistently (across situations) and stably (across time). Option (b) states exactly that. (d) is the situationist critique of trait theory, the opposite emphasis.","traps":{"a":"'Broad patterns' is closer to a type approach; the trait approach targets specific measurable dimensions of difference.","c":"Communicating expected behaviour from similarities is not the trait definition.","d":"Stressing situational determinants is the situationist position that opposes trait theory."}},{"q":"The ______ lobe processes auditory information.","concept":"The answer is the temporal lobe because it contains the primary auditory cortex, the cortical destination for sound. The cerebral cortex is divided into four lobes, and a reliable way to master them is to pair each with its primary sensory or motor function. The occipital lobe, at the back of the head, is dedicated to vision and houses the primary visual cortex. The parietal lobe, behind the central sulcus, processes somatosensory information (touch, pressure, temperature, pain) and spatial awareness, with the primary somatosensory cortex along its postcentral gyrus. The frontal lobe handles voluntary movement (primary motor cortex on the precentral gyrus), executive functions, planning, and (in the left hemisphere) Broca's area for speech production. The temporal lobe, sitting on the side of the brain roughly behind the temple and above the ear, is the home of the primary auditory cortex, located on the superior temporal gyrus (Heschl's gyrus), which receives and processes auditory information relayed up from the cochlea via the auditory pathway. The temporal lobe also contains Wernicke's area for language comprehension and parts of the hippocampus for memory, both of which fit logically with hearing and understanding spoken language. So auditory processing maps to the temporal lobe. A useful mnemonic: temporal is near the temple and the ear, so it handles what you hear. Lock the four-way map (occipital-vision, parietal-touch, frontal-movement, temporal-hearing) and these lobe questions become automatic.","traps":{"a":"The occipital lobe processes VISION, not hearing; it houses the primary visual cortex at the back of the brain.","b":"The parietal lobe processes somatosensory information such as touch, temperature, and spatial location, not auditory input.","d":"The frontal lobe handles motor control, planning, and executive function (plus speech production in Broca's area), not the reception of auditory information."}},{"q":"When infants and very young children live in environments where they have no exposure to or experience with language, their language development is severely limited. These limitations are not reversible. This supports which of the following terms?","concept":"The correct answer is sensitive periods of development. A sensitive period (or critical period) is a specific window of time early in development during which the brain is especially receptive to particular environmental input, and during which certain abilities, such as language, are most readily acquired. If the necessary experience is absent during this window, the ability may fail to develop normally and, crucially, the deficit can be permanent and not fully reversible even with later exposure. Eric Lenneberg formulated the critical period hypothesis for language, proposing that language must be acquired before puberty for native-like mastery to be possible. Tragic natural cases such as Genie, a child deprived of language until age 13, support this: despite later intervention she never acquired normal grammar, illustrating the irreversibility the question emphasises. The concept parallels Konrad Lorenz's work on imprinting in birds, another time-bounded sensitive period. Nativism (option D), Noam Chomsky's view that humans possess an innate language acquisition device, is related but does not by itself explain the irreversible harm from deprivation within a bounded time frame. Semantics and syntax are simply components of language. This item tests the concept of a bounded, largely irreversible developmental window.","traps":{"b":"Semantics is the study of meaning in language; it does not explain why deprivation causes irreversible limitations.","c":"Syntax concerns the rules governing sentence structure; it is a component of language, not the developmental-timing concept described.","d":"Nativism (associated with Noam Chomsky) claims language is innately programmed; while related, it does not specifically capture the irreversibility tied to a bounded time window, which is the sensitive or critical period."}},{"q":"Habit reversal training (HRT) is primarily used as a behavioral intervention to reduce which type of repetitive behavior?","concept":"The answer is tics because Habit Reversal Training is the behavioural treatment of choice for tic disorders (including Tourette's syndrome) as well as for other body-focused repetitive behaviours such as trichotillomania (hair-pulling) and nail-biting. HRT was developed by Nathan Azrin and Gregory Nunn in the 1970s and consists of several linked components that work together. The first is awareness training, in which the person learns to notice the tic or habit and the premonitory urge or early-warning sensations that precede it, since these behaviours are often carried out semi-automatically without full awareness. The second is developing a competing response, a physically incompatible action that the person performs deliberately whenever they feel the urge (for example, tensing an opposing muscle group so the tic cannot be executed). The third involves social support and motivation strategies to reinforce the new response. By repeatedly substituting the competing response for the tic whenever the urge arises, the tic weakens over time. This is a first-line non-pharmacological treatment and, in expanded form, is part of the package known as CBIT (Comprehensive Behavioral Intervention for Tics). The distractors each have their own signature treatment, and matching disorder to treatment is the exam skill: OCD to exposure and response prevention, phobias to systematic desensitization (Joseph Wolpe, 1958), and tics to habit reversal training. So the correct answer is tics.","traps":{"b":"Stuttering is treated mainly with fluency-shaping and speech-modification techniques, not primarily habit reversal training.","c":"Obsessive-compulsive behaviours are treated principally with exposure and response prevention (ERP), not habit reversal training.","d":"Aggressive behaviours are addressed through anger management, behaviour management, and social-skills training, not habit reversal training."}},{"q":"When an individual has dissociative amnesia it is usually associated with several types of memory disturbances. In contrast, in generalized amnesia :","concept":"The correct answer is that there is a failure of recall that encompasses the person's entire life. Dissociative amnesia, described in the DSM-5 of the American Psychiatric Association, is an inability to recall important autobiographical information, usually of a traumatic or stressful nature, that is too extensive to be ordinary forgetting and is not due to a substance or neurological condition. It takes several forms distinguished by the scope of memory loss. Localized amnesia is failure to recall events during a specific circumscribed period (for example, the hours around a trauma); selective amnesia is failure to recall some but not all events of a period; and systematized amnesia targets a specific category of information. Generalized amnesia is the rarest and most severe form: it involves a complete loss of memory for one's entire life history, including personal identity, autobiographical events, and sometimes previously learned skills and knowledge. Because it encompasses the person's whole life rather than a limited window, option b is correct. The other options describe the more circumscribed subtypes (localized for a week, selective or partial for childhood or for traumatic events only), which is precisely the contrast the question draws with the total sweep of generalized amnesia.","traps":{"a":"Loss limited to the past week is a circumscribed or localized amnesia, not the total loss defining generalized amnesia.","c":"Loss confined to childhood is selective or partial, not the complete life-span loss of generalized amnesia.","d":"Failure limited to traumatic events describes localized or selective amnesia tied to specific incidents, not generalized amnesia."}},{"q":"When two variables that are being used as predictors in a statistical analysis have a high correlation with each other, this statistical problem is known as:","concept":"The answer is multicollinearity because that is the precise term for the situation in regression where two or more predictor (independent) variables are highly correlated with one another. The prefix 'multi' plus 'collinearity' literally signals several predictors lying along the same line, moving together. Multicollinearity is a problem because multiple regression tries to estimate the unique, independent contribution of each predictor to the outcome, but when predictors overlap heavily, the model cannot cleanly separate their effects. The practical consequences are that the estimated regression coefficients become unstable and can swing wildly with small changes in the data, their standard errors inflate, and it becomes hard to tell which predictor is really doing the work, even though the model's overall predictive accuracy may stay fine. It is commonly diagnosed with the Variance Inflation Factor (VIF) or by examining a correlation matrix among predictors, and it is often remedied by dropping or combining redundant variables. The distractors name genuinely different regression concerns: heteroscedasticity is about the residuals having non-constant variance, autocorrelation is about residuals being correlated over time or sequence, and homogeneity of variance is actually a desirable assumption rather than a fault. Because the question describes two predictors being highly correlated with each other, the answer is multicollinearity.","traps":{"a":"Heteroscedasticity is the problem of unequal error variance across the range of predicted values (the scatter fans out), not a correlation between predictors.","c":"Autocorrelation is when a variable is correlated with its own values at earlier time points (common in time-series data), not correlation between two different predictors.","d":"Homogeneity of variance is an assumption being satisfied (equal variances across groups), not a problem, and it concerns variance, not predictor intercorrelation."}},{"q":"Alcohol-induced amnesia may be an example of :","concept":"The correct answer is state-dependent memory. State-dependent memory refers to the finding that retrieval is enhanced when a person's internal physiological or psychological state at recall matches their state during original encoding. It is a form of encoding specificity, the broader principle articulated by Endel Tulving and Donald Thomson that memory is best when retrieval cues reinstate the conditions of learning. With alcohol, information encoded while intoxicated is often recalled more easily when the person is again intoxicated than when sober, and vice versa; this is why a person may forget, while sober, where they placed something while drunk, then recall it upon drinking again. Early demonstrations of drug and alcohol state-dependent learning were reported by researchers including Donald Overton. This differs from context-dependent memory, where the match is in external environment (studied by Duncan Godden and Alan Baddeley in their diver experiments), and from mood-dependent memory, where the match is in emotional state, studied by Gordon Bower. Note this is distinct from an alcohol blackout, in which severe intoxication blocks the formation of new memories entirely. The item specifically describes retrieval governed by internal state, which is the definition of state-dependent memory.","traps":{"a":"Dependent memory is not a standard term and does not name the phenomenon of retrieval tied to internal state.","c":"Independent memory implies retrieval unaffected by context, the opposite of what alcohol-linked recall demonstrates.","d":"State-independent memory would mean the internal state does not matter, contradicting the state-dependent effect."}},{"q":"A clinician is interested in understanding a client's characteristic way of thinking, feeling, and behaving in close relationships, by having them tell stories about ambiguous pictures. The MOST appropriate test to administer would be the:","concept":"The answer is the Thematic Apperception Test, the TAT, because the technique of having a client tell stories about ambiguous pictures in order to reveal their characteristic ways of thinking, feeling, and relating is precisely what the TAT was designed to do. Understand the logic of projective testing and the specific design of the TAT. Projective techniques rest on the projective hypothesis: when a person is confronted with an ambiguous stimulus that has no clear meaning of its own, they impose structure on it by projecting their own inner needs, conflicts, motives, and relationship patterns onto it, so their response reveals otherwise hidden aspects of personality. The TAT, developed by Henry Murray and Christiana Morgan at Harvard in the 1930s, uses a set of cards depicting ambiguous interpersonal scenes, often people in emotionally suggestive but unclear situations. The examinee is asked to make up a story for each card: what is happening, what led up to it, what the characters are thinking and feeling, and how it turns out. Because the pictures involve people in relationships, the stories tend to expose the storyteller's dominant needs, press (environmental forces), and characteristic interpersonal themes, which Murray analyzed through his theory of needs and press. Clinicians examine recurring themes, the hero the client identifies with, and how conflicts are resolved. The stem's exact scenario, understanding a client's way of relating in close relationships by having them tell stories about ambiguous pictures, is the definition of the TAT, distinguishing it from the inkblot-based Rorschach and the objective MMPI (Starke Hathaway and J. Charnley McKinley, 1943).","traps":{"a":"The Rorschach uses ambiguous inkblots, not pictures of people or scenes to tell stories about, and it focuses on perceptual organization; while projective, it is not the storytelling test the vignette describes.","c":"A sentence completion test presents sentence stems for the client to finish and elicits brief associations, not the elaborated narratives about relationships that emerge from telling stories to pictures.","d":"The MMPI is an objective, self-report personality inventory with true-false items and empirically keyed scales, the opposite of a projective storytelling technique."}},{"q":"According to the \"Dodo Bird Verdict,\" what is the primary factor responsible for equivalent outcomes across different therapeutic modalities?","concept":"The answer is common factors such as the therapeutic alliance and empathy because the Dodo Bird Verdict is the proposition that different, seemingly quite distinct forms of psychotherapy produce roughly equivalent outcomes, and that this equivalence is best explained not by the unique techniques of any one approach but by the factors they all share. The colourful name comes from Lewis Carroll's Alice in Wonderland, in which the Dodo bird, after a chaotic race, declares that 'everybody has won, and all must have prizes'. The idea was introduced to psychotherapy research by Saul Rosenzweig in 1936 and later supported empirically by meta-analyses, notably by Lester Luborsky and colleagues, which found comparatively small differences in effectiveness between bona fide therapies. If technique is not the main driver, what is? The common-factors view, associated with researchers such as Jerome Frank and later Bruce Wampold, points to elements present across virtually all therapies: a strong therapeutic alliance (the collaborative, trusting bond between client and therapist), therapist empathy, warmth, and positive regard, the instillation of hope and positive expectations, and having a coherent rationale and structure for change. On this account, the relationship itself does much of the healing. The exam trap is choosing 'specific techniques', which is exactly what the verdict de-emphasises. The correct answer is the shared common factors, above all the therapeutic alliance and empathy.","traps":{"a":"Specific techniques are precisely what the Dodo Bird Verdict argues do NOT account for the roughly equal effectiveness across therapies, so this is the opposite of its claim.","c":"The clinician's theoretical orientation is downplayed by the verdict, which holds that outcomes are similar regardless of which school the therapist follows.","d":"The patient's specific diagnosis is not the explanation the verdict offers; it points instead to shared common factors across therapies."}},{"q":"Hypnagogic hallucinations most commonly occur in:","concept":"The correct answer is normal. Hypnagogic hallucinations are vivid perceptual experiences (most often visual or auditory) that occur during the transition from wakefulness into sleep, that is, while falling asleep. Their counterpart, hypnopompic hallucinations, occur on waking up, during the transition from sleep to wakefulness. The crucial teaching point is that these hallucinations occurring at the threshold of sleep are normal phenomena, experienced by a large proportion of healthy people, and are not by themselves indicative of any psychiatric or neurological disorder. They arise because the brain is shifting between sleep and waking states, and fragments of dream-like imagery intrude into a partially conscious mind. Common examples include hearing one's name called, hearing a doorbell or knock, brief flashes of imagery, or the sensation of falling (the hypnic jerk). Because they happen at sleep onset in ordinary people, the correct association is with the normal state rather than with illness. Clinically, very frequent or intense hypnagogic hallucinations, especially combined with sleep paralysis, excessive daytime sleepiness, and cataplexy, form part of the tetrad of narcolepsy, but in isolation occasional hypnagogic hallucinations are a benign, normal experience.","traps":{"a":"Schizophrenia involves hallucinations during full wakefulness (classically auditory), not the benign sleep-onset hallucinations described.","b":"Delirium produces hallucinations (often visual) as part of an acute confusional state, not the normal sleep-transition phenomenon.","c":"Drug dependence and intoxication can cause hallucinations, but hypnagogic hallucinations specifically occur in normal people at sleep onset."}},{"q":"It has been found that the Muller-Lyer illusion exists in certain cultures in which there are :","concept":"The correct answer is buildings with corners. The Muller-Lyer illusion, devised by Franz Carl Muller-Lyer, is the classic figure in which two lines of equal length appear unequal because one has outward-pointing arrowheads (fins) and the other inward-pointing ones. A leading explanation is the carpentered world hypothesis, advanced by Marshall Segall, Donald Campbell, and Melville Herskovits in their cross-cultural research. They argued that people raised in carpentered environments, cities and towns full of rectangular buildings, straight edges, and right-angled corners, learn to interpret the arrow configurations as depth cues (like the near and far corners of a room or building), which biases their length judgments and makes them highly susceptible to the illusion. In contrast, people from non-carpentered environments with few straight lines and corners, such as certain rural or open-landscape cultures, showed markedly weaker susceptibility. This finding challenged the assumption that perception is purely innate and universal, demonstrating that ecological and cultural experience shapes even basic visual processing. Therefore the illusion is stronger in cultures whose environments feature buildings with corners and angles, making option b correct.","traps":{"a":"Forests with trees relate to the vegetation of non-carpentered environments, which show a weaker illusion, not a stronger one.","c":"People from environments lacking corners and angles are less susceptible, so this is the opposite of the answer.","d":"Sex ratio has no established role in susceptibility to this geometric illusion."}},{"q":"IQ if any body has a mental age of 8 and a chronological age of 10 is:","concept":"The correct answer is 80. The intelligence quotient, in its original ratio form introduced by William Stern and popularised through the Stanford-Binet test revised by Lewis Terman, is calculated with the formula IQ equals mental age divided by chronological age, multiplied by 100. Here the mental age is 8 and the chronological age is 10, so IQ equals (8 divided by 10) times 100, which equals 0.8 times 100, equals 80. The logic is that a child whose mental age matches their chronological age has an IQ of 100 (average), a child whose mental development is ahead of their years scores above 100, and a child whose mental age lags behind their chronological age, as here, scores below 100. Option b (125) results from reversing the ratio, dividing chronological age by mental age, which is the wrong direction; option d (92) is near average but does not match the calculation; and option a (108) does not follow from the formula. Mental age, the concept underlying this, originated with Alfred Binet and Theodore Simon, who devised the first practical intelligence test. Although modern tests use deviation IQ (David Wechsler) rather than this ratio formula, understanding the classic ratio IQ remains a staple of assessment questions and illustrates the historical foundation of intelligence testing.","traps":{"a":"108 does not result from the IQ formula with these values; it appears to be an arbitrary distractor.","b":"125 would result from reversing the ratio (10 divided by 8), which is the wrong direction.","d":"92 is close to 100 but does not match the correct computation of mental age over chronological age times 100."}},{"q":"If Ramesh works in a call centre, and has to work very hard, do a lot of work, and that too at a fast rate, he experiences:","concept":"The correct answer is pressure. In the psychology of stress, several distinct sources of stress are commonly distinguished, and pressure is the demand to perform, produce, or behave in a certain way, often at a high level, at speed, or to meet expectations and deadlines. Ramesh's situation, having to work very hard, complete a large volume of work, and do so at a fast rate, is a textbook example of pressure: he faces intense demands on his performance and pace. The distractors describe other sources of stress: conflict (option a) arises when a person must choose between incompatible goals or motives, such as an approach-avoidance conflict studied by Kurt Lewin, and is about competing demands rather than workload; frustration (option b) is the stress that results when progress toward a goal is blocked or thwarted, such as being unable to complete a task because of obstacles; and unpredictability (option c) is the stress that comes from not knowing what will happen or being unable to anticipate events, which increases stress because control and predictability tend to reduce it. Understanding these categories matters because identifying the specific source of a person's stress guides how to address it: pressure calls for time management, workload adjustment, and coping skills, whereas conflict, frustration, and unpredictability each require different strategies, a distinction central to stress and health psychology.","traps":{"a":"Conflict is stress arising from having to choose between incompatible goals, not from a heavy, fast workload.","b":"Frustration is stress from being blocked from a goal, not from demanding workload demands.","c":"Unpredictability refers to stress from not knowing what will happen, not from the sheer volume and pace of work."}},{"q":"Which is NOT typically considered a learning difficulty?","concept":"Learning difficulties, or specific learning disorders, are neurodevelopmental conditions that impair specific academic skills despite adequate general intelligence and instruction. Dyslexia affects reading and decoding, dysgraphia affects written expression and handwriting, dyscalculia affects mathematics, and auditory processing disorder impairs the brain's processing of the sounds it hears, all of which directly interfere with acquiring particular academic skills and are classed among learning difficulties. ADHD (attention-deficit/hyperactivity disorder), by contrast, is a neurodevelopmental disorder of attention, impulse control, and hyperactivity. Although ADHD frequently co-occurs with learning disorders and can secondarily hinder academic performance, it is not itself a specific learning disorder: its core deficit is in attention and self-regulation, not in a specific academic skill such as reading, writing, or arithmetic. This distinction is important for accurate diagnosis and intervention. Because dysgraphia, dyslexia, and auditory processing disorder are learning difficulties while ADHD is an attention disorder, option b is the correct exception.","traps":{"a":"Dysgraphia is a specific learning disorder affecting written expression, so it IS a learning difficulty.","c":"Dyslexia is the prototypical specific learning disorder (reading), so it IS a learning difficulty.","d":"Auditory processing disorder impairs processing of heard information and is commonly grouped with learning difficulties."}},{"q":"Which approach to perception emphasizes the role of unconscious inference and probabilistic reasoning in interpreting sensory information?","concept":"The correct answer is Constructivist approach. The constructivist approach to perception holds that perception is an active process in which the brain constructs our experience of the world by combining incoming sensory data with prior knowledge, expectations, and probabilistic inference. Its intellectual root is Hermann von Helmholtz's idea of unconscious inference: because the retinal image is ambiguous and impoverished, the perceptual system unconsciously infers the most likely interpretation of the scene, effectively making a best guess. Richard Gregory later championed this top-down, hypothesis-testing view of perception. Because the question emphasises unconscious inference and probabilistic reasoning, the constructivist approach is the correct match, making option B right. The direct perception approach (option C), developed by James J. Gibson, is the direct opposite: it argues that the environment provides rich, sufficient information (invariants and affordances) so that perception is direct and requires no inference or internal construction. The computational approach (option D pairing aside), associated with David Marr, treats vision as a series of information-processing computations from image to 3-D model, focusing on the algorithms rather than on unconscious inference per se. The information processing approach describes perception and cognition as flowing through stages, a general framework rather than the specific inference-based constructivist claim. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)","traps":{"a":"The computational approach, associated with David Marr, models vision as information-processing algorithms but is not defined by unconscious inference.","c":"The direct perception approach, from James Gibson, holds that the environment provides sufficient information directly, opposing the need for inference.","d":"The information processing approach is a broad framework of stages of processing, not specifically the unconscious-inference view."}},{"q":"What term describes the phenomenon where individuals fail to notice significant changes in their enviornment, even when those changes are obvious?","concept":"The correct answer is Change blindness. Change blindness is the surprising failure to detect a substantial change in a visual scene, especially when the change coincides with a brief disruption such as a flicker, a blink, an eye movement, or a cut in a film. Because our visual system does not store a complete, detailed representation of every part of a scene, we often miss even large, obvious alterations when attention is not directed to the changing element. This is precisely the phenomenon the question describes, making option A correct. It was demonstrated in striking experiments by Daniel Simons and colleagues, including studies in which an experimenter asking for directions was swapped for a different person mid-conversation without the participant noticing. Inattentional blindness (option B), also studied by Daniel Simons and Christopher Chabris in the famous invisible gorilla experiment, is the related but distinct failure to notice an unexpected object that is in plain view because attention is engaged elsewhere; it concerns missing something present, not missing a change between two views. Perceptual constancy (option C) is the ability to perceive an object as stable in size, shape, or colour despite changing sensory input, an unrelated concept. Selective attention (option D) is the general process of focusing on certain stimuli while filtering out others. Change blindness specifically names the failure to spot changes. (no official key in the paper; answer determined by subject expertise)","traps":{"b":"Inattentional blindness is failing to see an unexpected object because attention is elsewhere, not failing to notice a change between two views.","c":"Perceptual constancy is the stable perception of objects despite changes in sensory input, an entirely different phenomenon.","d":"Selective attention is the process of focusing on some stimuli while ignoring others, not the failure to detect changes."}}]});
